window.VAULT={"version":2,"tags":["Scaling","Latency","Databases","Sharding","Migration","Caching","Consistency","Transactions","Messaging","Reliability","APIs","Observability","Operations","Architecture","Leadership","Cost & compliance"],"categories":[{"id":"scale","name":"Scale & Traffic","topics":["Request path & scaling basics","Monolith vs services","Overload, backpressure & spikes","Sync, async & queues"]},{"id":"data","name":"Data & Storage","topics":["SQL, NoSQL & OLTP vs OLAP","Partitioning & hot keys","Indexing","Migrations & backfills","Retention & deletion","Multi-tenancy"]},{"id":"caching","name":"Caching & Performance","topics":["Caching strategy","Pagination"]},{"id":"consistency","name":"Consistency & Transactions","topics":["Idempotency","Consistency models & CAP","Distributed transactions & reconciliation","Consensus & distributed IDs"]},{"id":"reliability","name":"Reliability & Failure","topics":["Failure modes & degradation","Retries, breakers & bulkheads","Multi-region & outages","Releases & rollback","SLOs","Recovery"]},{"id":"streaming","name":"Streaming & Async","topics":["Event-driven design","Delivery semantics","Poison messages & dead letters","Ordering","Schedulers & long-running jobs","Event schema evolution"]},{"id":"apis","name":"APIs & Boundaries","topics":["Service boundaries & gateway","Versioning, compatibility & deprecation","Rate limits & quotas","Partial failure"]},{"id":"observability","name":"Observability & Operations","topics":["Logging & tracing","Metrics, dashboards & alerts","Latency debugging","Runbooks & failure drills"]},{"id":"evolution","name":"Evolution, Cost & Trade-offs","topics":["MVP, platforms & cost","Build vs buy","Extensibility, experiments & compliance","Legacy migration"]},{"id":"judgment","name":"Judgment & Leadership","topics":["Trade-offs & design reviews","Simplicity & over/under-engineering","Design docs & ownership"]}],"questions":[{"id":"walk-one-request-dns-database","cat":"scale","topic":"Request path & scaling basics","level":"fundamental","source":"both","q":"Walk one request from DNS to the database and back. Where does each millisecond go?","also":["How does a request flow end-to-end through a production system?"],"a":["Name every hop: DNS, TLS, CDN or edge, load balancer, service, cache, database. Give each hop a latency budget, then say which hops dominate p99 and why.","DNS, edge or CDN, load balancer, gateway, service, cache, datastore, and back. State what each hop does, what can fail there, and where latency accumulates."],"tags":["Latency"],"legacy":[0,93],"answer":{"thesis":"Name every hop, give each a latency budget, and say which hops dominate the tail. For most web requests the network round trips (DNS, connection setup, TLS) and the slowest dependency call dominate, not the application code.","pitch":"A browser request goes: DNS resolution, usually cached but potentially tens of milliseconds on a miss; TCP and TLS setup, one to two round trips to the edge, which is why terminating TLS at a nearby CDN or edge point matters; the edge either serves from cache or forwards over a warm connection to the origin's load balancer; the load balancer picks a healthy instance; the service authenticates, maybe checks a cache, then queries the database over a pooled connection; the database finds rows via an index, from memory if hot, from disk if not; and the response returns the same way, possibly compressed. I'd put rough numbers on each and point out that p50 is dominated by network distance while p99 is dominated by queueing, cache misses, garbage collection and the slowest downstream call. That tells you where to optimise: move the edge closer, reuse connections, cache, and cut the slowest dependency.","points":[{"h":"DNS","d":"Usually answered from the browser or OS cache. On a miss, a recursive resolver lookup can take tens of milliseconds. Low TTLs help failover but increase misses."},{"h":"Connection setup","d":"TCP needs one round trip; TLS 1.3 adds one more (TLS 1.2 adds two). At 80 ms round trip time to a distant origin, that's 160–240 ms before the first byte. An edge 10 ms away cuts this dramatically."},{"h":"Edge or CDN","d":"Serves cacheable content directly. For dynamic requests it forwards over pre-established, long-lived connections to the origin, avoiding repeated setup costs."},{"h":"Load balancer","d":"Adds well under a millisecond typically, but its algorithm and health checks decide whether you land on a slow or overloaded instance."},{"h":"Service work","d":"Authentication, validation, business logic and serialisation. Often a few milliseconds, but GC pauses and thread-pool queueing appear here in the tail."},{"h":"Cache and database","d":"An in-memory cache hit is around a millisecond or less in the same zone. A database query using an index on hot data takes about a millisecond; a cold disk read or a bad plan can take hundreds."},{"h":"Where p99 comes from","d":"Queueing under load, cache misses, slow dependencies, retries, and fan-out (the request waits for the slowest of many calls)."}],"decision":"To cut median latency: move termination closer to users, reuse connections, cache aggressively. To cut tail latency: reduce fan-out, set timeouts, remove queueing, fix slow queries and GC. Measure each hop with tracing before optimising.","numbers":["Order-of-magnitude figures: same-zone network round trip around 0.5 ms or less; cross-region tens of milliseconds; intercontinental 100 ms or more; memory reference nanoseconds; SSD random read around 0.1 ms.","Budget example for a 200 ms target: DNS and connection 40 ms (with edge), edge to origin 20 ms, service 30 ms, cache or database 20 ms, response transfer 30 ms, margin 60 ms."],"pitfalls":["Optimising application code when most time is spent in round trips.","Opening a new database or HTTP connection per request instead of pooling.","Looking only at averages, which hide the tail where users notice slowness.","Forgetting response size: large payloads over slow mobile links can dominate."],"followups":[{"q":"How would you measure where the time goes?","a":"Real-user monitoring for client-side phases (DNS, connect, TLS, first byte), distributed tracing for server hops, and database query timing. Compare p50 and p99 per hop."},{"q":"What changes for a mobile app on a poor network?","a":"Round trips dominate even more: batch requests, reuse connections (HTTP/2 or HTTP/3), compress, and cache on the device."},{"q":"How does HTTP/3 help?","a":"QUIC combines transport and TLS handshakes, avoids head-of-line blocking across streams, and handles network changes better, which helps lossy mobile connections."}],"example":"A checkout page at 900 ms p99 for users in Asia hitting a US origin. Tracing showed 400 ms in connection setup and TLS. Terminating TLS at a regional edge with warm connections to the origin cut p99 to 450 ms without touching application code.","related":["read-traffic-grows-10x-one","size-system-50-000-requests","p50-flat-but-p99-doubled","should-cache-client-edge-service"],"sources":[{"title":"Ilya Grigorik: High Performance Browser Networking","url":"https://hpbn.co/"},{"title":"Jeff Dean and Luiz Barroso: The tail at scale","url":"https://research.google/pubs/the-tail-at-scale/"}]}},{"id":"read-traffic-grows-10x-one","cat":"scale","topic":"Request path & scaling basics","level":"intermediate","source":"both","q":"Read traffic grows 10x in one quarter. What do you change first, and what do you refuse to change?","also":["How do I design a service that can handle 10x more traffic with minimal changes?"],"a":["Measure the bottleneck before acting. Cache and add read replicas before sharding. Hold the API contract and data model steady so clients and teams do not have to move.","Stateless services behind a load balancer, caching for hot reads, replicas, partitionable data, and autoscaling with tested limits. Find the first bottleneck by load testing, not guessing."],"tags":["Scaling","Caching"],"legacy":[1,94],"answer":{"thesis":"Measure where the first bottleneck will be, then add read capacity in order of cost: caching, read replicas, then partitioning. Hold the API contract and the data model steady so clients and other teams don't have to change with you.","pitch":"First I'd find out what breaks first at 10x, with load tests and the current saturation of each tier, rather than guess. For read growth the usual order is: cache hot, read-mostly data, at the edge for public content and in a shared cache for computed results, which can absorb most of the growth if the hit ratio is high; add read replicas for the database, with read-your-writes handled for users who just wrote; make the service tier stateless and autoscaled; and only then partition data if a single primary or dataset is the limit. What I'd refuse to change is the public API contract and the core data model, because changing those forces every client and team to move at once; I'd also refuse a rewrite under deadline pressure. The goal is to scale behind stable interfaces.","points":[{"h":"Measure before acting","d":"Load test at 2x, 5x, 10x in a production-like environment, and look at saturation per tier (CPU, connections, cache memory, database I/O). The first bottleneck is often not where people expect."},{"h":"Cache first","d":"Edge caching for public or per-region content, an application cache for expensive computed results, and HTTP caching headers. A 90% hit ratio means the backend sees one tenth of the reads."},{"h":"Read replicas second","d":"Replicas scale reads horizontally. Handle replication lag: route reads needing fresh data to the primary or use session guarantees."},{"h":"Stateless services, autoscaled","d":"Move session state to a shared store, set autoscaling on saturation signals, and make sure connection pools don't overwhelm the database as instances multiply."},{"h":"Partition last","d":"Sharding is the most expensive change: data movement, cross-shard queries, operational complexity. Do it when a single dataset or primary truly can't keep up."},{"h":"What not to change","d":"Public API contracts, core data model and identifiers. Changing these during a growth spurt multiplies risk and coordination cost."},{"h":"Protect against the spike","d":"Rate limits, load shedding and graceful degradation buy time while capacity catches up."}],"decision":"Cache, then replicas, then horizontal service scaling, then partitioning, in that order unless measurement shows a different first bottleneck. Never trade API stability for a quick scaling win.","numbers":["Cache math: at 10x traffic and a 90% hit ratio, origin reads are 10 × 0.1 = 1x today's load. At 80% hit ratio, origin load is 2x, which shows how sensitive capacity is to hit ratio.","Replica math: if the primary can serve 5,000 reads/s and you need 40,000, you need around 8 read-serving nodes plus headroom, assuming reads spread evenly."],"pitfalls":["Sharding first because it sounds like 'real' scaling.","Adding replicas but sending all reads to them, breaking read-your-writes.","Autoscaling app servers until database connections are exhausted.","Changing API shapes 'while we're at it', forcing client releases during the crunch."],"followups":[{"q":"The cache hit ratio is only 50%. What do you do?","a":"Analyse the key distribution: long-tail keys may not be worth caching, TTLs may be too short, or keys may include unnecessary variance (like query parameter order). Fix key design and pre-warm hot keys."},{"q":"What if writes also grow 10x?","a":"Then batch and queue writes, reduce write amplification (indexes), and plan partitioning sooner, since replicas don't help writes."},{"q":"How do you avoid database connection exhaustion?","a":"Connection poolers (such as PgBouncer), per-instance pool caps, and fewer, larger instances, or a data access service that multiplexes connections."}],"example":"A content platform expected 10x reads from a partnership. Load tests showed the database saturating at 3x. Edge caching of article pages (hit ratio 94%) plus two read replicas carried the launch; sharding, originally planned, wasn't needed.","related":["walk-one-request-dns-database","should-cache-client-edge-service","give-users-read-writes-reads","trade-offs-between-vertical-horizontal"],"sources":[{"title":"AWS Builders' Library: Caching challenges and strategies","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/builders-library/caching-challenges-and-strategies/"},{"title":"Martin Kleppmann: Designing Data-Intensive Applications, chapter 5 (Replication)","url":"https://dataintensive.net/"}]}},{"id":"choose-load-balancing-strategy-long","cat":"scale","topic":"Request path & scaling basics","level":"intermediate","source":"mine","q":"Choose a load balancing strategy for long-lived connections versus short RPCs.","also":[],"a":["Round robin, least-request, power of two choices, consistent hashing. Connection draining, slow start for new instances, and what happens to sticky state on scale-in."],"tags":["Scaling","Latency"],"legacy":[4],"answer":{"thesis":"Short RPCs balance well per request with least-outstanding-requests or power-of-two-choices. Long-lived connections balance per connection, so the risk is imbalance over time; counter it with connection-aware placement, periodic rebalancing, slow start for new instances and graceful draining.","pitch":"For short RPCs, per-request balancing works: round robin is fine when instances and requests are uniform, but least outstanding requests or power of two choices, which picks two random backends and sends to the less loaded one, handles uneven request costs and slow instances much better with little coordination. For long-lived connections like WebSockets, gRPC streams or database connections, the load balancer only decides once per connection, so after a deploy or scale-out the new instances get few connections while old ones stay overloaded. There I'd use least-connections placement, slow start so new instances aren't flooded, connection lifetimes or server-initiated reconnects to rebalance gradually, and draining on scale-in, telling clients to reconnect elsewhere before the instance stops. If connections carry sticky state, consistent hashing limits how many move when the pool changes.","points":[{"h":"Round robin","d":"Simple and fair when requests cost the same and instances are identical. Degrades with heterogeneous requests or a slow instance, which still gets its full share."},{"h":"Least outstanding requests","d":"Sends to the backend with the fewest in-flight requests. Naturally routes around slow instances. Requires the balancer to track in-flight counts."},{"h":"Power of two choices","d":"Pick two backends at random and choose the less loaded. Gets most of the benefit of least-loaded without global state and avoids herding onto one 'best' instance."},{"h":"Long-lived connections balance per connection","d":"gRPC over HTTP/2 multiplexes many requests on one connection, so an L4 balancer balances connections, not requests. Use L7 balancing or client-side balancing for gRPC."},{"h":"Rebalancing over time","d":"Cap connection lifetime (for example minutes to an hour, with jitter) or have servers send GOAWAY or reconnect hints so load spreads after scale-out."},{"h":"Slow start and draining","d":"New instances ramp traffic gradually to warm caches and JIT. On scale-in, stop new connections and ask clients to reconnect, waiting for in-flight work."},{"h":"Consistent hashing for sticky state","d":"When connections depend on per-key state (sessions, partitions), consistent hashing moves only about 1/N of keys when a node joins or leaves."}],"decision":"Short RPCs: least outstanding requests or power of two choices at L7. Long-lived streams: least connections plus bounded lifetimes and server-driven rebalancing, with slow start and draining. Sticky state: consistent hashing with bounded load.","numbers":["Consistent hashing: adding a 10th node moves roughly 1/10 of keys, versus nearly all keys with modulo hashing.","Connection lifetime example: 30 minutes ± 20% jitter means a full fleet rebalances within about half an hour after scale-out, without a reconnect storm."],"pitfalls":["Using an L4 balancer for gRPC and finding all requests from a client go to one backend.","No slow start: a fresh instance receives its full share of connections while caches are cold, and its latency spikes.","Killing instances without draining, forcing thousands of clients to reconnect at once.","Fixed connection lifetimes without jitter, causing synchronised reconnects."],"followups":[{"q":"How does client-side load balancing compare?","a":"Clients get a list of backends (from service discovery) and balance themselves, avoiding an extra hop and handling per-request balancing over multiplexed connections. It needs good discovery and health information in every client."},{"q":"What about WebSockets during a deploy?","a":"Drain gradually: stop new connections to old instances, send reconnect messages in batches with jitter, and let clients reconnect to new instances."},{"q":"Why not always pick the least-loaded instance?","a":"With stale load information, every balancer picks the same 'best' instance and overloads it. Power of two choices avoids this herding."}],"example":"A chat service on WebSockets saw new instances sit idle after scale-out while old ones ran hot. Adding a 45-minute connection lifetime with jitter, least-connections placement and slow start evened load within an hour of each scale event.","related":["walk-one-request-dns-database","design-admission-control-service-stays","trade-offs-between-vertical-horizontal"],"sources":[{"title":"Google SRE book: Load balancing in the datacenter","url":"https://sre.google/sre-book/load-balancing-datacenter/"},{"title":"gRPC blog: gRPC load balancing","url":"https://grpc.io/blog/grpc-load-balancing/"}]}},{"id":"size-system-50-000-requests","cat":"scale","topic":"Request path & scaling basics","level":"advanced","source":"mine","q":"Size a system for 50,000 requests per second with a 100 ms hard deadline.","also":[],"a":["Back-of-envelope CPU, memory and network per request. Fan-out cost, timeout budget split across calls, 30 to 40 percent headroom, and what you drop when the deadline is about to be missed."],"tags":["Scaling","Latency"],"legacy":[5],"answer":{"thesis":"Work it out per request, then multiply: CPU time, memory and network per request give instance counts; the 100 ms deadline gives a latency budget split across hops; headroom of 30–40% covers spikes and failures. Then say what you drop when the deadline is about to be missed.","pitch":"I'd do back-of-envelope sizing in four steps. Per-request cost: measure or estimate CPU time, say 4 ms per request; at 50,000 requests per second that's 200 CPU-seconds per second, so 200 cores busy, and at a target of 60% utilisation about 340 cores, or roughly 22 sixteen-core instances, plus enough to survive losing a zone. Concurrency by Little's law: 50,000 per second × 0.05 s average latency is 2,500 requests in flight, which sizes thread pools and connections. Latency budget: of 100 ms, give perhaps 10 to network, 20 to the service, 40 to dependencies called in parallel with their own timeouts, and keep 30 in reserve. Dependencies: if each request makes three downstream calls, those services see 150,000 calls per second. And I'd decide in advance what we drop near the deadline: optional calls, then lower-priority requests, so the core response stays within 100 ms.","points":[{"h":"Per-request resource cost","d":"CPU milliseconds, memory per in-flight request, bytes in and out. Get them from profiling or a load test of one instance, not guesses."},{"h":"Compute instance count","d":"Cores = requests/s × CPU seconds per request ÷ target utilisation. Then round up for zone failure (N+1 zones) and deploys (instances out of rotation)."},{"h":"Concurrency with Little's law","d":"In-flight = arrival rate × latency. This sizes thread pools, connection pools and memory, and shows how quickly a latency increase exhausts them."},{"h":"Split the latency budget","d":"Allocate the 100 ms across network, service time and dependencies, with timeouts at each step below the remaining budget. Parallelise independent calls."},{"h":"Fan-out multiplies downstream load","d":"Each downstream call multiplies requests per second for that dependency, and the request waits for the slowest call, so the tail matters more with fan-out."},{"h":"Headroom","d":"30–40% spare capacity covers traffic spikes, a lost zone, and performance regressions until autoscaling catches up."},{"h":"Deadline behaviour","d":"Propagate the deadline; skip optional calls when the remaining budget is low; shed low-priority traffic before high-priority traffic misses its deadline."}],"decision":"Size from measured per-request costs, add zone-failure and spike headroom, and enforce the deadline with timeouts and degradation. Validate with a load test at 1.5x the target before trusting the numbers.","numbers":["CPU: 50,000 req/s × 4 ms = 200 cores busy; ÷ 0.6 utilisation ≈ 333 cores ≈ 21 sixteen-core instances; across 3 zones with one-zone loss tolerance, run about 32 instances.","Network: 50,000 req/s × 20 KB response ≈ 1 GB/s ≈ 8 Gbit/s egress, which matters for instance network limits and cost.","Concurrency: 50,000 req/s × 50 ms average = 2,500 in flight; if latency rises to 100 ms, 5,000."],"pitfalls":["Sizing on average latency and ignoring the tail and fan-out.","Forgetting that dependencies see multiplied traffic.","No headroom for zone loss, so a single-zone outage becomes an overload.","Timeouts that add up to more than the overall deadline."],"followups":[{"q":"How do you get the per-request CPU cost if the system doesn't exist yet?","a":"Prototype the hot path and benchmark it, or use costs from a similar existing service, and plan to correct after the first load test."},{"q":"The p99 of one dependency is 80 ms. Can you meet 100 ms?","a":"Only with a tight timeout and a fallback, a hedged request, or by moving that call off the critical path (prefetch or cache), since its tail alone consumes most of the budget."},{"q":"What's the memory footprint?","a":"In-flight requests × memory per request plus caches and runtime overhead per instance. 2,500 in flight × 200 KB = 500 MB across the fleet, small compared to caches."}],"example":"An ad-serving endpoint at 50,000 req/s with a 100 ms deadline: profiling showed 3 ms CPU per request. The team ran 30 instances across three zones at about 50% utilisation, gave the bidding call a 40 ms timeout with a default ad as fallback, and kept p99 at 70 ms.","related":["walk-one-request-dns-database","design-admission-control-service-stays","one-request-fans-out-five","p50-flat-but-p99-doubled"],"sources":[{"title":"Jeff Dean and Luiz Barroso: The tail at scale","url":"https://research.google/pubs/the-tail-at-scale/"},{"title":"Google SRE book: Handling overload","url":"https://sre.google/sre-book/handling-overload/"}]}},{"id":"trade-offs-between-vertical-horizontal","cat":"scale","topic":"Request path & scaling basics","level":"fundamental","source":"ref","q":"What are the trade-offs between vertical and horizontal scaling?","also":[],"a":["Vertical is simple and has a ceiling and a single point of failure. Horizontal needs statelessness or partitioning, adds coordination cost, and scales further. Most systems do vertical first, then horizontal where the data allows."],"tags":["Scaling"],"legacy":[48],"answer":{"thesis":"Vertical scaling is simple and keeps everything on one machine, but it has a ceiling, a cost curve that steepens, and a single point of failure. Horizontal scaling removes the ceiling and adds redundancy, but requires statelessness or partitioning and adds coordination. Most systems scale stateless tiers horizontally early and data tiers vertically for as long as practical.","pitch":"Vertical scaling means a bigger machine: more cores, memory, faster disks. It needs no code changes, keeps transactions and joins local and simple, and modern hardware goes very far. The costs: there's a ceiling, the largest instances cost disproportionately more, resizing often means a restart or failover, and one big machine is still one failure domain. Horizontal scaling means more machines: it scales further and gives redundancy, but the workload must be stateless or partitioned, and you inherit load balancing, data distribution, consistency and more network failure modes. In practice I'd scale application servers horizontally from the start because they're stateless and it gives redundancy, and scale the database vertically plus replicas until a single primary genuinely can't keep up, then partition.","points":[{"h":"Vertical: simplicity","d":"No architectural change. Single-node transactions, joins and consistency stay easy. Great for databases and for early-stage systems."},{"h":"Vertical: limits","d":"A hard ceiling on the largest instance, cost per unit of capacity rising at the top end, and a single failure domain unless paired with replicas."},{"h":"Horizontal: scale and redundancy","d":"Add capacity incrementally and survive node loss. Fits stateless services naturally."},{"h":"Horizontal: complexity","d":"Requires externalising state or partitioning data, load balancing, service discovery, and dealing with partial failure and consistency across nodes."},{"h":"Stateless tiers go horizontal early","d":"Web and API servers with state moved to caches or databases scale out cheaply and gain redundancy."},{"h":"Data tiers stay vertical longer","d":"A large primary with read replicas often covers far more growth than expected. Partitioning is a large, hard-to-reverse step."},{"h":"Elasticity and cost","d":"Horizontal scaling allows scaling in during quiet hours, saving money; a big vertical instance runs at peak size all the time."}],"decision":"Scale out stateless services; scale up stateful stores until cost, ceiling or failure-domain concerns force partitioning. Revisit when you approach roughly half of the largest available instance, to leave time to plan.","numbers":["Headroom check: if your database runs at 60% of a 32-core instance and the largest available is 192 cores, vertical scaling buys roughly another 6x before the ceiling, often years of growth.","Failure domain: one large node at 100% of traffic vs 10 nodes at 10% each; losing one node removes 100% vs 10% of capacity."],"pitfalls":["Partitioning a database prematurely and paying the complexity forever.","Scaling up until hitting the ceiling with no plan, then needing an emergency re-architecture.","Horizontally scaled services that still hold local state (sessions, caches) and break on rebalancing.","Ignoring that a single big node is a single point of failure."],"followups":[{"q":"When would you scale a database horizontally first?","a":"When the dataset or write rate clearly exceeds one node from the start (large-scale event ingestion, IoT telemetry) or when the data is naturally partitioned by tenant with isolation requirements."},{"q":"How do you make a stateful service horizontally scalable?","a":"Move state out (database, cache, object store), or partition it so each node owns a key range, with rebalancing and replication."},{"q":"Isn't vertical scaling downtime-prone?","a":"Resizing often requires a restart; with a replica you fail over to a resized standby, so downtime is a brief failover."}],"example":"A fintech's Postgres primary grew from 8 to 64 cores over three years with two read replicas, handling 15x growth. Application servers scaled from 4 to 60 instances. Partitioning was planned only when write volume approached half of the largest instance's capacity.","related":["read-traffic-grows-10x-one","splitting-monolith-pay-itself","shard-data-user-tenant-region"],"sources":[{"title":"Martin Kleppmann: Designing Data-Intensive Applications, chapter 1 (Scalability)","url":"https://dataintensive.net/"}]}},{"id":"splitting-monolith-pay-itself","cat":"scale","topic":"Monolith vs services","level":"intermediate","source":"both","q":"When does splitting a monolith pay for itself?","also":["How do I decide when to split a monolith into services?"],"a":["Team ownership lines, different deploy cadence, different scaling profiles. Price in the costs: network failure modes, distributed data, on-call load. A modular monolith is a valid answer.","Split along team and data ownership lines when deploy independence or scaling needs diverge. Cost: network failures, distributed data, operational load. A modular monolith may be enough."],"tags":["Scaling","Architecture"],"legacy":[2,92],"answer":{"thesis":"Splitting pays when team coordination, deploy coupling or very different scaling needs cost more than the network failures, distributed data and operational load you take on. A well-structured modular monolith is often the better answer, and a prerequisite for a clean split anyway.","pitch":"I'd look for three signals. Organisational: many teams blocking each other in one codebase and deploy pipeline, so release coordination and merge conflicts dominate. Change rate: some parts change daily and others rarely, but everything ships together and carries the same risk. Scaling and runtime profile: one component needs very different resources, like GPU inference, a high-throughput ingest path, or stricter isolation for compliance. Against that I'd price the costs: every call becomes a network call that can fail and be slow, data must be split with clear ownership and eventual consistency between services, and you need service discovery, tracing, versioned contracts and more on-call. If the signals are weak, I'd invest in a modular monolith: enforce module boundaries and data ownership inside one deployable. When a module clearly needs independence, extract that one, along a boundary already proven in the code.","points":[{"h":"Signal: teams blocking each other","d":"When more than a handful of teams share one deploy pipeline, coordination costs grow fast. Independent deployability per team is the strongest reason to split."},{"h":"Signal: divergent change rates and risk","d":"Fast-moving features coupled to stable, critical code means every release risks the critical path."},{"h":"Signal: divergent runtime needs","d":"Different scaling, hardware, language, or isolation requirements for one component."},{"h":"Cost: network and partial failure","d":"In-process calls become remote calls with latency, timeouts, retries and versioning. Debugging spans processes."},{"h":"Cost: distributed data","d":"Each service owns its data. Cross-service queries and transactions become APIs, events, sagas and reconciliation."},{"h":"Cost: platform and operations","d":"CI/CD per service, service discovery, observability, security between services, and on-call for more moving parts. You need a platform before many services."},{"h":"Modular monolith first","d":"Enforce module boundaries (separate packages, no shared tables between modules, internal APIs). This captures much of the benefit and makes later extraction straightforward."}],"decision":"Split when independent deployment or scaling of a specific part is clearly worth the cost, one module at a time. Stay with a modular monolith when the team is small, boundaries are still moving, or platform maturity is low.","numbers":["Heuristic: if a monolith's main pain is a 2-hour build or test suite, fix the build first; that's cheaper than splitting.","Team-size heuristic: a single deployable tends to strain when more than several teams (tens of engineers) commit to it daily."],"pitfalls":["Splitting by technical layer (UI service, data service) instead of by business capability.","A distributed monolith: services that must deploy together and share a database.","Splitting before boundaries are understood, then redrawing them across network calls.","No platform investment, so each new service reinvents deployment and monitoring."],"followups":[{"q":"Which module would you extract first?","a":"One with a clear boundary, its own data, a distinct scaling or change profile, and a team ready to own it. Avoid starting with the most entangled core."},{"q":"How do you split the database?","a":"Give each module ownership of its tables inside the monolith first, remove cross-module joins, then move the tables with the extracted service and replace joins with API calls or replicated read models."},{"q":"How do you know the split worked?","a":"Lead time and deploy frequency for the team improved, incidents didn't increase, and the extracted service deploys independently without coordinated releases."}],"example":"A lending company with four teams kept a modular monolith with strict module boundaries. When the underwriting team needed ML models, GPU instances and daily releases, they extracted underwriting as a service, along a boundary that had already existed as a module for a year.","related":["draw-service-boundaries-lending-platform","trade-offs-between-vertical-horizontal","tell-design-over-engineered","choose-between-building-platform-one"],"sources":[{"title":"Martin Fowler: Monolith first","url":"https://martinfowler.com/bliki/MonolithFirst.html"},{"title":"Martin Fowler: Microservice trade-offs","url":"https://martinfowler.com/articles/microservice-trade-offs.html"}]}},{"id":"design-admission-control-service-stays","cat":"scale","topic":"Overload, backpressure & spikes","level":"advanced","source":"both","q":"Design admission control so a service stays up through a 20x spike.","also":["How do I protect downstream services from traffic spikes?"],"a":["Shed by priority, bound every queue, use adaptive concurrency limits, apply per-tenant quotas, and fail fast with Retry-After. Protect the critical path first, batch work last.","Rate limits and concurrency caps at the caller, queues that smooth bursts, circuit breakers, load shedding by priority, and caching in front of the dependency."],"tags":["Scaling","Reliability"],"legacy":[3,95],"answer":{"thesis":"Under a 20x spike you cannot serve everyone, so decide who gets served. Admission control rejects excess work early and cheaply, by priority and by tenant, keeps every queue bounded, and adapts concurrency limits to measured latency so the work you accept still completes on time.","pitch":"The goal is to keep goodput up: serve as many requests successfully as capacity allows rather than accepting everything and timing out everyone. I'd put admission control at the edge of the service. First, per-tenant and per-client quotas, so one client's spike doesn't consume everyone's capacity. Second, priority classes: interactive and revenue-critical requests over background and batch work, with lower classes shed first. Third, an adaptive concurrency limit that measures latency and lowers the number of in-flight requests when latency rises, like TCP congestion control. Fourth, bounded queues with short timeouts, rejecting rather than queueing when full, and preferring to drop requests that have waited long, since their clients have probably given up. Rejections are fast and explicit, 429 or 503 with Retry-After, so well-behaved clients back off. Autoscaling helps over minutes, but admission control protects you in the seconds before it does.","points":[{"h":"Protect goodput, not throughput","d":"Accepting more than you can process makes every request slow and many fail. Rejecting the excess keeps latency and success high for the work accepted."},{"h":"Per-tenant quotas","d":"Token buckets per client or tenant isolate noisy neighbours, so a spike from one source mostly affects that source."},{"h":"Priority-based shedding","d":"Classify requests (critical, normal, best-effort). Under pressure, shed best-effort first, then normal, keeping critical paths alive."},{"h":"Adaptive concurrency limits","d":"Algorithms such as gradient or AIMD-style limits adjust allowed concurrency based on observed latency, finding capacity without manual tuning."},{"h":"Bounded queues and deadlines","d":"Queues have a maximum length and a maximum wait. Drop requests whose deadline has passed; LIFO under overload can serve fresh requests that still have waiting clients."},{"h":"Cheap, explicit rejection","d":"Reject before doing expensive work, with 429 or 503 and Retry-After, so clients back off rather than retry immediately."},{"h":"Autoscaling is the second line","d":"Scaling takes minutes; admission control acts in milliseconds. Use both, and ensure scaling isn't blocked by the overload itself."}],"decision":"Every externally facing service gets per-client quotas, bounded queues and a concurrency limit. Priority shedding where traffic classes differ in value. Tune with load tests at several times peak.","numbers":["At 20x a normal 5,000 req/s, you receive 100,000 req/s. If capacity is 15,000 req/s, admission control must reject about 85% quickly, at a cost of perhaps microseconds each, rather than letting all 100,000 queue.","Rejection cost matters: if rejecting takes 0.1 ms of CPU and serving takes 5 ms, you can reject about 50 requests for the cost of serving one."],"pitfalls":["Unbounded queues that turn a spike into minutes of latency for everyone.","Rejecting after doing most of the work (authentication, database lookups).","Global limits only, so one tenant's spike starves everyone.","Clients that retry 429s immediately, amplifying the spike."],"followups":[{"q":"How do you classify priority?","a":"By endpoint and caller: checkout and login are critical; search suggestions and analytics beacons are best-effort. Carry the class in a header set at the edge."},{"q":"Why LIFO under overload?","a":"With FIFO, every request waits behind old ones whose clients may have given up. LIFO serves the newest requests, which still have someone waiting, improving useful work done."},{"q":"How does this interact with autoscaling?","a":"Shed load immediately, while autoscaling adds capacity over minutes. Make sure shedding metrics don't hide demand from the autoscaler; scale on offered load, not just served load."}],"example":"A ticketing API hit 25x traffic when sales opened. Per-account token buckets, best-effort shedding of seat-map refreshes, and an adaptive concurrency limit kept purchase requests at 99% success with p99 under 600 ms while 70% of traffic was rejected with Retry-After.","related":["design-backpressure-high-traffic-system","design-per-tenant-rate-limits","retries-amplifying-outage-design-retries","list-ways-three-tier-service"],"sources":[{"title":"AWS Builders' Library: Using load shedding to avoid overload","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/builders-library/using-load-shedding-to-avoid-overload/"},{"title":"Google SRE book: Handling overload","url":"https://sre.google/sre-book/handling-overload/"},{"title":"Netflix: concurrency-limits library","url":"https://github.com/Netflix/concurrency-limits"}]}},{"id":"design-backpressure-high-traffic-system","cat":"scale","topic":"Overload, backpressure & spikes","level":"advanced","source":"ref","q":"How do I design for backpressure in a high-traffic system?","also":[],"a":["Bounded queues, propagate pressure upstream (blocking, credits, 429s), shed load deliberately, and size consumers to drain bursts. Unbounded buffers only move the failure later."],"tags":["Reliability","Scaling"],"legacy":[51],"answer":{"thesis":"Backpressure means a slow consumer can slow down its producers instead of being buried. Bound every buffer, propagate 'I'm full' upstream (blocking, credits, 429s), and decide deliberately where excess load is dropped. Unbounded buffers only move the failure later and make it bigger.","pitch":"In a pipeline, the slowest stage sets the throughput. If upstream keeps sending faster than that, the buffer between them grows until memory runs out or latency becomes useless. Backpressure fixes this by making capacity visible upstream. Inside a process, that's bounded queues where producers block or get rejected when full. Between services, it's flow control: HTTP/2 and gRPC have window-based flow control, streaming systems use pull-based consumption or credits, and request-response APIs return 429 or 503 to signal 'slow down'. At the edge, where you can't slow the user, you shed load deliberately. With queues like Kafka, consumers pull at their own pace and lag grows instead of memory, which is fine for a while, as long as you monitor lag and scale consumers or shed upstream before retention runs out.","points":[{"h":"Every buffer must be bounded","d":"Queues, channels, thread pools and in-memory lists all have a maximum size. Unbounded buffers convert overload into out-of-memory crashes or minutes of latency."},{"h":"Signal upstream","d":"Blocking producers (in-process), credits or windows (streaming protocols), and explicit rejections with Retry-After (HTTP). The producer must react by slowing down."},{"h":"Pull beats push for consumers","d":"Pull-based consumers (Kafka, SQS) take work at their own rate; the backlog lives in durable storage instead of in consumer memory."},{"h":"Monitor lag and age, not just depth","d":"The age of the oldest message tells you how stale processing is. Alert on lag growth rate and time until retention is exceeded."},{"h":"Decide where to drop","d":"At some point load must be shed. Choose the place: at the edge (reject requests), by sampling (drop some telemetry), or by degrading (skip optional processing)."},{"h":"Scale the bottleneck stage","d":"Backpressure buys time; adding consumers or capacity to the slowest stage fixes throughput. Partition counts and downstream limits cap how far that goes."}],"decision":"Bounded buffers everywhere, explicit backpressure signals between components, durable queues where work can wait, and deliberate shedding where it can't. Treat growing lag as an incident before it becomes data loss.","numbers":["Backlog time: a queue receiving 12,000 msgs/s with consumers handling 10,000 msgs/s grows by 2,000/s, 7.2 million per hour. With 24 hours of retention and no fix, data starts expiring after about a day.","Drain time after the spike: with a backlog of 7.2 million and 3,000 msgs/s of spare consumer capacity, draining takes about 40 minutes."],"pitfalls":["Unbounded in-memory queues in services, which hide the problem until the process dies.","Producers that ignore 429s or retry immediately.","Monitoring queue depth without age, missing that the oldest message is hours old.","Scaling consumers beyond what the downstream database can handle, moving the bottleneck."],"followups":[{"q":"How do you apply backpressure to end users?","a":"You can't slow them directly, so reject or degrade: 429 with Retry-After, waiting rooms for big events, or simpler responses."},{"q":"What happens if Kafka retention is about to expire unprocessed messages?","a":"Scale consumers, extend retention temporarily, divert to a slower but durable overflow store, or prioritise processing and accept dropping low-value messages explicitly."},{"q":"How does gRPC flow control help?","a":"HTTP/2 stream and connection windows stop a sender when the receiver hasn't consumed data, so a slow receiver isn't overwhelmed at the transport level."}],"example":"A metrics ingestion pipeline buffered in memory and crashed during spikes. The fix: bounded in-process queues, 429s to agents with Retry-After, Kafka between ingest and aggregation, and lag-age alerts. Spikes now show up as lag that drains afterwards instead of crashes.","related":["design-admission-control-service-stays","should-use-queue-versus-direct","design-dead-letter-queues-retry","list-ways-three-tier-service"],"sources":[{"title":"AWS Builders' Library: Avoiding insurmountable queue backlogs","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/builders-library/avoiding-insurmountable-queue-backlogs/"},{"title":"Google SRE book: Handling overload","url":"https://sre.google/sre-book/handling-overload/"}]}},{"id":"choose-between-synchronous-asynchronous-communication","cat":"scale","topic":"Sync, async & queues","level":"intermediate","source":"ref","q":"How do I choose between synchronous and asynchronous communication?","also":[],"a":["Synchronous when the caller needs the answer to proceed and latency is bounded. Asynchronous to decouple availability, absorb bursts and run slow work. Cost of async: eventual results, retries, duplicate handling."],"tags":["Messaging"],"legacy":[49],"answer":{"thesis":"Use synchronous calls when the caller needs the answer to continue and the work is fast and bounded. Use asynchronous messaging to decouple availability, absorb bursts, fan out to many consumers, or run slow work. Asynchrony costs you immediate answers and simple error handling, so pay it only where you gain something.","pitch":"My rule: if the user or caller can't proceed without the result, and it can be produced within the latency budget, call synchronously, as with checking a price, authorising a payment, or reading a profile. If the work is slow, can be done later, or other systems just need to know something happened, make it asynchronous: emit an event or enqueue a job, return quickly, and process in the background. Async gives decoupling, since the producer stays up when consumers are down; buffering, which absorbs spikes; and fan-out, where many consumers react without the producer knowing them. Its costs are real: results arrive later, so the UI needs pending states; errors happen after the request returned, so you need retries, dead-letter handling and notifications; and duplicates and ordering need design. Most systems mix both: a synchronous core decision, then asynchronous side effects.","points":[{"h":"Synchronous when the answer is needed now","d":"Validations, authorisations, reads to render a page. Keep these calls few, fast and with timeouts."},{"h":"Asynchronous for slow or deferrable work","d":"Sending email, generating reports, video processing, updating search indexes. The caller gets an acknowledgement and maybe a job id."},{"h":"Availability decoupling","d":"With a queue in between, the producer keeps accepting work while a consumer is down; the backlog drains later. Synchronous chains multiply unavailability."},{"h":"Load levelling","d":"Queues absorb bursts so consumers can run at a steady rate sized for average rather than peak load."},{"h":"Fan-out and loose coupling","d":"Publishing an event lets new consumers subscribe without changing the producer."},{"h":"The costs of async","d":"Eventual results and pending UI states, failure handling after the fact, at-least-once delivery requiring idempotency, ordering concerns, and harder end-to-end tracing."}],"decision":"Synchronous for the critical decision in a user request; asynchronous for side effects and slow work. If a synchronous chain is more than two or three calls deep, look for steps that can move to async or be served from local data.","numbers":["Availability of synchronous chains multiplies: three dependencies at 99.9% each give at most about 99.7% for the chain. With async side effects, the user path depends only on the synchronous parts."],"pitfalls":["Making everything async, so simple requests become hard-to-debug event chains with no immediate answer.","Long synchronous chains where any slow service blocks the user.","Async processing without visibility: users don't know whether their job succeeded.","Forgetting idempotency for at-least-once delivery."],"followups":[{"q":"How does the user learn the result of async work?","a":"Return a job or resource id, then poll, subscribe via WebSocket or server-sent events, or receive a notification or webhook."},{"q":"Can you do request-reply over a queue?","a":"Yes, with correlation ids and reply queues, but it combines the latency of async with the coupling of sync. Use it when the transport itself is asynchronous (for example mainframe or partner integrations)."},{"q":"How do you trace async flows?","a":"Propagate trace context in message headers and link consumer spans to producer spans, so a trace follows the message."}],"example":"Loan application submission validates input and runs the instant credit decision synchronously (under 2 seconds), returning approved or declined. Document generation, CRM updates, welcome emails and analytics happen asynchronously from an ApplicationDecided event.","related":["should-use-queue-versus-direct","event-driven-request-response-order","track-status-long-running-jobs","design-backpressure-high-traffic-system"],"sources":[{"title":"Enterprise Integration Patterns: Messaging","url":"https://www.enterpriseintegrationpatterns.com/patterns/messaging/"},{"title":"Martin Kleppmann: Designing Data-Intensive Applications, chapter 11 (Stream processing)","url":"https://dataintensive.net/"}]}},{"id":"should-use-queue-versus-direct","cat":"scale","topic":"Sync, async & queues","level":"intermediate","source":"ref","q":"When should I use a queue versus direct RPC?","also":[],"a":["Queue for buffering, fan-out, retries and independent consumer speed. RPC for request-scoped answers with a tight deadline. Never put a queue in a path that needs a synchronous response without a reply mechanism."],"tags":["Messaging"],"legacy":[50],"answer":{"thesis":"Use a queue when work can wait and you want buffering, retries, independent consumer speed or fan-out. Use direct RPC when the caller needs a response within a tight deadline. A queue in a path that needs a synchronous answer adds latency and failure modes without the benefits.","pitch":"I'd ask: does the caller need the result to respond? If yes, and the work is fast, RPC is simpler: one hop, immediate errors, easy tracing. If the work can complete later, a queue gives me durable buffering through spikes and consumer outages, built-in retries with dead-lettering, consumers scaled independently of producers, and the ability to add consumers without touching the producer. Queues also smooth load onto a fragile downstream such as a legacy system or rate-limited partner API. The costs: an extra component to run, at-least-once delivery so consumers must be idempotent, ordering only within a partition or group, and results that come back later. Request-reply over a queue exists, but if you need the answer in 100 ms, a direct call with a timeout and fallback is almost always better.","points":[{"h":"RPC for request-scoped answers","d":"Fast, bounded work whose result the caller needs. Simple semantics, immediate error feedback, and straightforward tracing."},{"h":"Queues for deferrable work","d":"The producer records intent durably and returns; consumers process at their own pace."},{"h":"Buffering and load levelling","d":"Queues absorb spikes and protect slow or fragile downstreams. Consumers can be sized for average load."},{"h":"Retries and dead-lettering","d":"Failed messages are retried with backoff and eventually moved to a dead-letter queue for inspection, without the producer waiting."},{"h":"Fan-out","d":"Topics with multiple subscriptions let several consumers process the same event independently."},{"h":"Costs of queues","d":"Operating the broker, at-least-once delivery and idempotency, ordering constraints, monitoring of lag, and harder debugging across asynchronous hops."}],"decision":"RPC when the caller waits for the result and the deadline is tight. Queue when the work is slow, deferrable, needs retries independent of the caller, must survive consumer outages, or feeds multiple consumers. A task queue (one consumer per job) differs from a log or topic (many consumers, replay); choose by whether you need replay and fan-out.","numbers":["Latency: a direct in-region RPC adds about a millisecond of network time; a queue hop adds publish, broker persistence and consumer polling, typically milliseconds to tens of milliseconds, or more with batching."],"pitfalls":["A queue in a synchronous user path, then polling for the result inside the request.","Assuming exactly-once delivery and skipping idempotency.","Using a queue to hide a consumer that can never keep up; the backlog only grows.","No dead-letter handling, so poison messages block or vanish."],"followups":[{"q":"Queue or topic?","a":"A queue distributes each message to one consumer in a group (work distribution). A topic delivers each message to every subscribing group (fan-out). Many systems use topics with consumer groups to get both."},{"q":"How would you rate-limit calls to a partner API that allows 50 requests per second?","a":"Put requests on a queue and run consumers that together don't exceed 50/s, with backoff on 429s. The queue absorbs bursts from your side."},{"q":"When is RPC with retries enough?","a":"When failures are brief and the caller can wait for a few retries within its deadline. If outages are longer or work must survive restarts, use a durable queue."}],"example":"Invoice PDF generation moved from an RPC inside the 'complete order' request (adding 1.5 s and failing when the PDF service was down) to a queue. Orders now complete in 200 ms; PDFs are generated within a minute and retried on failure.","related":["choose-between-synchronous-asynchronous-communication","design-dead-letter-queues-retry","design-backpressure-high-traffic-system","track-status-long-running-jobs"],"sources":[{"title":"Enterprise Integration Patterns: Point-to-point channel","url":"https://www.enterpriseintegrationpatterns.com/patterns/messaging/PointToPointChannel.html"},{"title":"Enterprise Integration Patterns: Publish-subscribe channel","url":"https://www.enterpriseintegrationpatterns.com/patterns/messaging/PublishSubscribeChannel.html"}]}},{"id":"boundary-between-oltp-olap-data","cat":"data","topic":"SQL, NoSQL & OLTP vs OLAP","level":"fundamental","source":"mine","q":"Where is the boundary between OLTP and OLAP, and how does data cross it?","also":[],"a":["Row store for transactions, columnar store for analytics. Move data by change data capture, not dual writes. Cover lag, backfill, and schema drift between the two sides."],"tags":["Databases"],"legacy":[8],"answer":{"thesis":"OLTP serves many small, latency-sensitive transactions on current state; OLAP serves few large scans over history. Keep them in separate stores and move data across with change data capture, never with application dual writes, and treat lag, backfills and schema drift as first-class concerns.","pitch":"The boundary is about access pattern. The transactional database handles point reads and short writes with strict latency and correctness; analytics wants to scan billions of rows, aggregate across history and join widely, which would wreck OLTP latency if run there. So analytics lives in a columnar warehouse or lakehouse. Data crosses via change data capture from the database's log into a stream, then into the warehouse, which is reliable because it reads committed changes in order, unlike the application writing to both places. I'd also plan for an initial snapshot plus streaming, monitoring of lag, handling of schema changes through a registry or contract, and deletes, including legal erasure, flowing through to the analytical side.","points":[{"h":"Different access patterns, different engines","d":"OLTP: row-oriented, indexed point lookups, short transactions, high concurrency. OLAP: columnar storage, compression, vectorised scans over a few columns of many rows."},{"h":"Don't run analytics on the primary","d":"Large scans evict hot pages from cache and hold resources, raising tail latency for customers. A read replica helps a little but still uses row storage that's slow for scans."},{"h":"CDC over dual writes","d":"Reading the database log (binlog, WAL) captures every committed change in order, including those from scripts and migrations. Application dual writes miss failures and diverge."},{"h":"Snapshot plus stream","d":"Start with a consistent snapshot, then apply changes from the log position at which it was taken. Tools like Debezium coordinate this."},{"h":"Lag as an SLO","d":"Publish freshness, for example 'warehouse within 15 minutes of production', and alert on it. Consumers can then decide whether data is fresh enough for their use."},{"h":"Schema drift and contracts","d":"A column rename in OLTP can break dashboards silently. Use a schema registry or data contracts, and test pipelines against schema changes before deployment."},{"h":"Deletes and erasure flow through","d":"Propagate deletes as tombstones, and make sure erasure requests reach the warehouse, derived tables and backups according to policy."}],"decision":"Separate stores once analytics queries become heavy or broad. Use CDC for continuous movement; batch extracts are acceptable for daily reporting. Hybrid HTAP databases can serve light operational analytics but don't remove the need for a warehouse for broad historical analysis.","numbers":["Columnar compression on analytical data is often several times to ten times or more, and queries read only the columns they need, which is why scans that take minutes on a row store can take seconds in a warehouse.","CDC latency is typically seconds to a minute end to end; warehouse loading in micro-batches every few minutes is common."],"pitfalls":["Application dual writes to the database and the warehouse that silently diverge.","Analysts querying the production primary 'just this once'.","No plan for schema changes, so pipelines break after a routine migration.","Deleted or erased data persisting forever in the warehouse."],"followups":[{"q":"What if the business needs real-time analytics?","a":"Stream changes into a real-time analytical store or materialised views over the stream, with seconds of lag, and keep the warehouse for historical depth."},{"q":"How do you handle a backfill of a year of data?","a":"Bulk export from a snapshot or replica into the warehouse, then resume CDC from the snapshot's log position so there's no gap or duplicate."},{"q":"Who owns the data contract?","a":"The producing team owns the schema and its compatibility; the platform provides the registry and checks."}],"example":"A lending company streams its Postgres WAL through Debezium into Kafka and loads the warehouse every five minutes. Risk dashboards run there instead of on replicas, and production p99 latency drops during month-end reporting.","related":["model-data-differently-oltp-versus","decide-between-sql-nosql-search","design-outbox-inbox-pattern-reliable","design-retention-soft-delete-legal"],"sources":[{"title":"Debezium: change data capture","url":"https://debezium.io/"},{"title":"Martin Kleppmann: Designing Data-Intensive Applications, chapter 3 (OLTP and OLAP storage)","url":"https://dataintensive.net/"}]}},{"id":"decide-between-sql-nosql-search","cat":"data","topic":"SQL, NoSQL & OLTP vs OLAP","level":"fundamental","source":"ref","q":"How do I decide between SQL, NoSQL, and search engines?","also":[],"a":["Start from access patterns and consistency needs. Relational for transactions and ad hoc queries, key-value or wide-column for known-key scale, search engines for text and faceting as a derived index, never the source of truth."],"tags":["Databases"],"legacy":[53],"answer":{"thesis":"Choose from access patterns and consistency needs, not fashion. A relational database is the default for transactional data and flexible queries; key-value or wide-column stores fit very high-scale access by known keys; search engines are derived indexes for text and faceting, never the source of truth.","pitch":"I'd list the queries and invariants first. If the data has relationships, needs transactions, and the queries aren't all known up front, a relational database is the right default, and modern Postgres or MySQL scale further than people assume with indexing, replicas and partitioning. If access is overwhelmingly by known key at very high throughput with simple consistency needs, like sessions, user feeds or IoT time series, a key-value or wide-column store like DynamoDB or Cassandra gives predictable latency and horizontal scale, at the cost of designing your tables around your queries and losing ad hoc joins. Full-text search, relevance ranking, fuzzy matching and faceted filters belong in a search engine like Elasticsearch or OpenSearch, fed from the source of truth via CDC, because search indexes aren't designed to be your system of record. Often the answer is all three, each doing what it's good at.","points":[{"h":"Start from queries and invariants","d":"Write down the top queries, write patterns, consistency requirements and growth. The store choice should fall out of that list."},{"h":"Relational as the default","d":"Transactions, constraints, joins, ad hoc queries and mature tooling. Scales a long way vertically, with read replicas and partitioning, before you need anything else."},{"h":"Key-value and wide-column for known-key scale","d":"Predictable single-digit-millisecond access at huge scale when you know the access patterns. You model tables per query and give up joins and flexible querying."},{"h":"Document stores for aggregate-shaped data","d":"Useful when data is read and written as a self-contained document with varying shape. Watch for needing cross-document transactions or joins later."},{"h":"Search engines are derived indexes","d":"Great at text relevance, fuzzy matching, aggregations for facets. Eventually consistent, with weaker durability guarantees; rebuildable from the source of truth."},{"h":"Operational cost counts","d":"Every new store adds backups, upgrades, monitoring, on-call knowledge and security review. Prefer fewer stores your team can run well."}],"decision":"Relational unless a specific access pattern or scale requirement rules it out. Add a key-value store for a well-understood, high-volume known-key workload. Add search as a derived index when users need text search or faceting. Avoid a store per service just because you can.","numbers":["A single well-tuned relational primary on modern hardware can handle thousands to tens of thousands of simple transactions per second; read replicas multiply read capacity.","Managed key-value stores advertise consistent single-digit-millisecond latency at practically unbounded scale when keys are well distributed."],"pitfalls":["Choosing NoSQL for scale you don't have, then rebuilding joins and transactions in application code.","Using a search engine as the primary store and losing data on cluster problems.","Modelling a key-value store like a relational one and needing scans for common queries.","Adding a new store per team without the operational capacity to run it."],"followups":[{"q":"When would you move off relational?","a":"When a specific workload exceeds what partitioning and replicas can handle, or needs a data model (graph traversal, time series at very high ingest) that relational handles poorly. Move that workload, not everything."},{"q":"How do you keep search in sync?","a":"CDC or an outbox stream from the source of truth into the indexer, idempotent upserts by id, and a full reindex job for recovery."},{"q":"What about NewSQL or distributed SQL?","a":"They offer relational semantics with horizontal scale, at the cost of higher write latency and operational complexity. Worth it when one relational node truly isn't enough and you need transactions across shards."}],"example":"A marketplace keeps orders and payments in Postgres, user sessions and carts in a key-value store, and listing search in OpenSearch fed by CDC from Postgres. Rebuilding the search index from Postgres takes two hours and is tested monthly.","related":["boundary-between-oltp-olap-data","model-data-differently-oltp-versus","pick-partition-key-multi-tenant","decide-buy-build"],"sources":[{"title":"Martin Kleppmann: Designing Data-Intensive Applications, chapter 2 (data models)","url":"https://dataintensive.net/"},{"title":"AWS DynamoDB: Best practices for partition key design","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/bp-partition-key-design.html"}]}},{"id":"model-data-differently-oltp-versus","cat":"data","topic":"SQL, NoSQL & OLTP vs OLAP","level":"fundamental","source":"ref","q":"How do I model data differently for OLTP versus OLAP workloads?","also":[],"a":["OLTP: normalized rows, narrow transactions, point lookups. OLAP: denormalized wide tables or star schemas in columnar storage, loaded from OLTP by change data capture or batch."],"tags":["Databases"],"legacy":[96],"answer":{"thesis":"Model OLTP for correct, fast writes and point reads: normalised tables, narrow transactions, indexes for known lookups. Model OLAP for fast scans and simple queries over history: denormalised fact and dimension tables in columnar storage, loaded from OLTP.","pitch":"In OLTP the goal is integrity under concurrent writes, so I'd normalise: each fact stored once, constraints in the database, short transactions, and indexes for the access paths the application uses. Denormalise only where a hot read path justifies it. In OLAP the goal is answering broad questions quickly, so I'd use a dimensional model: fact tables recording events like payments or loan disbursals with numeric measures and foreign keys, and dimension tables like customer, product and date, which may be wide and denormalised. Changing attributes are handled with slowly changing dimensions so history is preserved: a customer's region at the time of the payment, not today's. Data flows from OLTP by CDC or batch, is cleaned and conformed, and partitioned by date for pruning.","points":[{"h":"OLTP: normalise for integrity","d":"Third normal form, foreign keys and constraints prevent anomalies under concurrent updates. Each update touches few rows."},{"h":"OLTP: index for known access paths","d":"Index the lookups the application performs; every extra index slows writes. Keep transactions short to reduce lock contention."},{"h":"OLAP: facts and dimensions","d":"A star schema: a central fact table (one row per event, with measures) joined to dimension tables. Simple for analysts and efficient for columnar engines."},{"h":"OLAP: preserve history","d":"Slowly changing dimensions (for example type 2, with valid-from and valid-to rows) record attribute changes so reports reflect what was true at the time."},{"h":"OLAP: partition and cluster","d":"Partition fact tables by date and cluster by common filter columns so queries scan only relevant data."},{"h":"The transformation layer","d":"Raw replicated tables land first, then are cleaned, conformed (consistent ids, currencies, time zones) and modelled. Version this logic like code."}],"decision":"Normalised OLTP plus dimensional OLAP is the standard pairing. Denormalise OLTP only for measured hot reads, and prefer wide denormalised tables in OLAP when they simplify analyst queries without breaking history.","numbers":["Date partitioning example: a fact table with 3 years of daily partitions; a query for last month scans about 30 of roughly 1,100 partitions, under 3% of the data."],"pitfalls":["Copying the OLTP schema straight into the warehouse and making analysts write ten-way joins.","Overwriting dimension attributes, so historical reports change retroactively.","Denormalising OLTP early and then fighting update anomalies.","Mixing currencies or time zones in fact tables without conforming them."],"followups":[{"q":"When would you denormalise in OLTP?","a":"For a hot read path where joins are measurably expensive, such as storing an order total on the order row, with the update kept in the same transaction."},{"q":"Star schema or one big wide table?","a":"Wide tables are simpler for many tools and fast in columnar engines; star schemas manage changing dimensions and reuse better. Many teams model a star and publish wide tables on top."},{"q":"How do you handle late-arriving data?","a":"Load by event time into the right partition, reprocess affected aggregates, and track which partitions changed."}],"example":"A lender's OLTP has loans, repayments and customers in normalised tables. The warehouse has a repayments fact table (amount, days late, keys) and dimensions for customer (type 2, tracking region and risk band over time), product and date, so 'delinquency by risk band at origination' is a simple query.","related":["boundary-between-oltp-olap-data","decide-between-sql-nosql-search","design-indexes-table-takes-90"],"sources":[{"title":"Kimball Group: Dimensional modeling techniques","url":"https://www.kimballgroup.com/data-warehouse-business-intelligence-resources/kimball-techniques/dimensional-modeling-techniques/"},{"title":"Martin Kleppmann: Designing Data-Intensive Applications, chapter 3","url":"https://dataintensive.net/"}]}},{"id":"pick-partition-key-multi-tenant","cat":"data","topic":"Partitioning & hot keys","level":"intermediate","source":"both","q":"Pick a partition key for a multi-tenant ledger, then show how it fails.","also":["What does a good partition key look like in a distributed store?"],"a":["Tenant, account or time as candidates. Failure cases: one giant tenant, time-based hot tail, cross-partition queries. Mitigate with composite keys, salting for heavy keys, and a resharding plan.","High cardinality, even access, aligned with the dominant query, and stable over time. Avoid monotonically increasing keys that create a hot tail."],"tags":["Sharding","Databases"],"legacy":[6,97],"answer":{"thesis":"For a multi-tenant ledger, partition by account (scoped by tenant), because every invariant, balance checks and posting order, lives at the account level. Then show where it breaks: giant tenants, hot accounts, and cross-account queries, and how you'd handle each.","pitch":"The candidates are tenant, account and time. Time is wrong for a ledger: all writes go to the newest partition. Tenant alone is attractive for isolation but one large tenant becomes a hot partition and some tenants outgrow a partition. Account, as in (tenant_id, account_id), is my choice because the ledger's invariants, balance never negative and postings ordered, are per account, so a single-partition transaction can enforce them. Then the failure modes. A hot account, like a merchant settlement account receiving thousands of credits per second, overwhelms its partition; I'd split it into sub-accounts or buffer credits and post them in batches. Transfers between accounts on different partitions need a saga or two-step posting rather than one transaction. Tenant-wide queries like monthly statements scatter across partitions, so they go to a read model or the warehouse. And the plan needs a way to move a large tenant to dedicated capacity.","points":[{"h":"List the candidates and their skew","d":"Tenant: isolation, but skewed by tenant size. Time: everything hits the latest partition. Account: matches invariants and spreads load if accounts are numerous."},{"h":"Partition where invariants live","d":"Balance checks and posting order are per account, so (tenant_id, account_id) lets one local transaction enforce them without distributed coordination."},{"h":"Failure: hot accounts","d":"A platform's settlement or fee account may receive a huge share of postings. Split it into N sub-accounts with a roll-up, or buffer and batch credits that don't need immediate visibility."},{"h":"Failure: cross-partition transfers","d":"A transfer touches two accounts that may live on different partitions. Use a two-step posting (debit with a pending state, then credit) driven by a saga, idempotent per transfer id."},{"h":"Failure: tenant-wide queries","d":"Statements, tenant totals and reporting scatter across partitions. Serve them from a projection or the warehouse rather than live scatter-gather on the ledger."},{"h":"Failure: giant tenants","d":"Some tenants will dwarf others. Support moving a tenant to dedicated partitions or a dedicated cell, which needs online migration tooling."},{"h":"Plan resharding from day one","d":"Use many logical partitions mapped to fewer physical ones, so rebalancing moves logical partitions rather than re-keying data."}],"decision":"Partition by account within tenant for ledgers and other per-entity invariants. Handle hot accounts by splitting or batching, cross-account operations with sagas, and reporting with projections. Reserve tenant-level partitioning for when strict tenant isolation is the primary requirement.","numbers":["Logical partitions example: 4,096 logical partitions mapped onto 16 physical nodes; growing to 32 nodes moves whole logical partitions, about half of them, without changing keys.","Hot-account splitting: a settlement account receiving 5,000 postings/s split into 10 sub-accounts brings each to 500/s."],"pitfalls":["Partitioning by time and creating a permanently hot newest partition.","Partitioning by tenant and discovering the largest tenant is 40% of traffic.","Expecting single transactions across accounts on different partitions.","Running tenant-wide reports as live scatter-gather queries against the ledger."],"followups":[{"q":"How do you keep a balance correct with sub-accounts?","a":"The total balance is the sum of sub-account balances; each sub-account enforces its own non-negative rule, and debits draw from sub-accounts with funds or from a rebalancing step."},{"q":"How do you move a large tenant to its own partitions?","a":"Dual-write or replicate its accounts to the new location, backfill, verify balances, switch routing in the tenant directory, then stop the old writes."},{"q":"Why not just use a distributed SQL database with cross-partition transactions?","a":"You can, at the cost of higher latency for those transactions. Keeping invariants single-partition still matters for throughput and tail latency."}],"example":"A payments platform keys its ledger by (tenant_id, account_id) across 4,096 logical partitions. Each merchant's fee account is split into 16 sub-accounts after it became hot during sales, and monthly statements are generated from a projection in the warehouse.","related":["hot-partition-shows-up-2","shard-data-user-tenant-region","handle-multi-tenant-data-isolation","replace-two-phase-commit-across"],"sources":[{"title":"AWS DynamoDB: Designing partition keys to distribute your workload","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/bp-partition-key-uniform-load.html"},{"title":"Martin Kleppmann: Designing Data-Intensive Applications, chapter 6 (Partitioning)","url":"https://dataintensive.net/"}]}},{"id":"hot-partition-shows-up-2","cat":"data","topic":"Partitioning & hot keys","level":"advanced","source":"both","q":"A hot partition shows up at 2 a.m. Diagnose it and fix it without downtime.","also":["How do I avoid hot partitions and skew?"],"a":["Confirm skew from per-partition metrics. Short-term: cache the hot key, rate limit, split the range. Long-term: change the key, add a salt, rebalance online. Say how you verify the fix.","Choose a well-distributed key, salt or split heavy keys, cache hot reads, and monitor per-partition load so skew is seen before it becomes an outage."],"tags":["Sharding","Operations","Databases"],"legacy":[7,98],"answer":{"thesis":"Prove it's skew and find the key, buy time with the cheapest mitigation aimed at that key, then fix the key design online. Don't start by adding capacity: a hot key stays on one partition however many nodes you add.","pitch":"At 2 a.m. I'm mitigating, not redesigning. First I confirm from per-partition metrics that one partition is saturated while the rest are mostly idle; that rules out general overload. Then I find the hot key from top-key sampling or access logs and work out why it's hot: one huge tenant, a client stuck in a retry loop, a viral item, or a monotonically increasing key piling writes onto the newest range. The mitigation targets that key: rate-limit or block the offending client, put a short-TTL cache in front of hot reads, or split the range if the store supports it. Once things are stable, the real fix is the key design (a composite key, salting only the heavy keys, or moving a giant tenant to dedicated partitions), rolled out with dual writes and an online backfill. I call it fixed when the per-partition load distribution has flattened, not just when errors stop.","points":[{"h":"Confirm it's skew, not overload","d":"Look at per-partition throughput, latency and throttling. Skew looks like one partition at its limit while the cluster average is low. Cluster-level dashboards hide it: a 30% average can contain one partition at 100%."},{"h":"Find the key and the cause","d":"Use whatever the store offers for top keys (key sampling, per-partition metrics, consumer lag per partition) plus access logs. Typical causes: one very large tenant, a retry storm from one client, a celebrity item, or timestamp and auto-increment keys concentrating writes at the tail of the range."},{"h":"Mitigate in minutes","d":"Hot reads: cache in front with a short TTL and request coalescing. Hot writes: rate-limit the offender, queue or shed non-critical writes, or flip a feature flag that turns off the trigger. If the store can split a range or rebalance automatically, let it or force it."},{"h":"Protect everyone else on that partition","d":"Isolate the hot tenant or client (bulkhead, per-tenant limits) so the other tenants sharing the partition recover first. Post status updates in the incident channel while you work."},{"h":"Fix the key shape","d":"Options: a composite key such as (tenant, entity) instead of (tenant); salt only the heavy keys with N suffixes and scatter-gather on read; prefix time-based keys with a hash bucket; give giant tenants dedicated partitions or cells."},{"h":"Migrate without downtime","d":"Dual-write to the new key layout, backfill with throttling, verify with counts and checksums, switch reads, then stop writing the old layout. Changing a partition key in place is usually a full rewrite, so treat it as a migration."},{"h":"Verify and prevent","d":"Watch the ratio of the hottest partition to the median until it flattens. Add alerts on per-partition saturation, track top tenants by load, and load test with skewed traffic instead of uniform."}],"decision":"Hot reads: cache and replicate. Hot writes: split or salt the key. One tenant hot: isolate the tenant. Keys drifting hot over time (monotonic): change the key shape. Adding nodes doesn't help any of these, because a single key always maps to a single partition.","numbers":["DynamoDB documents a ceiling of 3,000 read units and 1,000 write units per second per partition. A key that needs more is throttled however much capacity the table has.","Salting a heavy key into N buckets divides its per-partition write load by N but multiplies read fan-out by N. With N = 10, one logical read becomes 10 parallel reads, which is why you salt only the heavy keys.","Working heuristic for spotting skew: the hottest partition at its limit while the median sits at a fraction of it, for example 100% against 20%."],"pitfalls":["Scaling out the cluster: the hot key still lives on one partition.","Stopping at the cache: it hides the hot read while the key keeps growing, and the next spike hits writes.","Salting every key instead of only the heavy ones: every read becomes a scatter-gather.","Changing the partition key in place without planning the data rewrite.","Declaring victory when errors stop instead of checking that load per partition has evened out."],"followups":[{"q":"The hot key is a single counter, such as likes on a viral post.","a":"Shard the counter: N sub-counters, write to a random one, sum them on read or aggregate periodically. Accept that the displayed total is slightly stale."},{"q":"How would you have caught this before 2 a.m.?","a":"Alert on per-partition saturation and on the max-to-median ratio, track the top tenants by load over time, and run load tests with skewed (Zipf-like) traffic instead of uniform traffic."},{"q":"The hot tenant is your largest customer. Do you rate-limit them?","a":"Short term, protect everyone else and talk to them. Long term, give them a dedicated cell or partitions so their growth becomes their own capacity, priced accordingly."},{"q":"Walk me through re-keying without downtime.","a":"Expand and contract: dual-write to the new layout, run a throttled and resumable backfill, verify, move reads, stop the old writes, then clean up after a full cycle with no reads on the old layout."}],"example":"A multi-tenant orders table is partitioned by tenant_id. A large retailer runs a flash sale; its partition throttles, failing writes for the 40 smaller tenants that share it. On-call caps the retailer's write rate and queues its non-urgent updates, and the other tenants recover. The follow-up changes the key to (tenant_id, hash bucket of order_id) for tenants above a size threshold, migrated with dual writes over a week.","related":["pick-partition-key-multi-tenant","shard-data-user-tenant-region","migrate-billion-row-table-new","caching-story-hot-keys-large"],"sources":[{"title":"AWS DynamoDB: Designing partition keys to distribute your workload","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/bp-partition-key-uniform-load.html"},{"title":"AWS DynamoDB: Best practices for partition key design","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/bp-partition-key-design.html"},{"title":"Martin Kleppmann: Designing Data-Intensive Applications, chapter 6 (Partitioning)","url":"https://dataintensive.net/"}]}},{"id":"shard-data-user-tenant-region","cat":"data","topic":"Partitioning & hot keys","level":"intermediate","source":"ref","q":"How do I shard data by user, tenant, region, or something else?","also":[],"a":["Shard on the dimension most queries filter by and that spreads load evenly. Cover cross-shard queries, rebalancing, and tenant or region affinity for locality and compliance."],"tags":["Sharding","Databases"],"legacy":[54],"answer":{"thesis":"Shard on the dimension that most queries filter by and that spreads load evenly, then add a second dimension where compliance or locality demands it. Plan rebalancing and cross-shard queries before the first shard fills.","pitch":"I'd pick the shard key by asking which value nearly every request carries and whether it distributes well. For consumer apps that's usually user id: requests are per user and users are numerous. For B2B SaaS it's usually tenant id, which also gives isolation and makes tenant export and deletion easy, but tenant sizes are skewed, so large tenants need special handling. Region is rarely the primary key for load, but it's often a top-level constraint for data residency and latency: first route to a region, then shard within it. Whatever the key, I'd use a directory or consistent hashing over many logical shards so rebalancing is moving shards, not rewriting keys, and I'd keep cross-shard queries out of hot paths by maintaining projections.","points":[{"h":"The key most requests carry","d":"If 95% of queries include user_id, sharding by user keeps them single-shard. Queries without the key become scatter-gather and should be rare or served elsewhere."},{"h":"Even distribution","d":"High cardinality and no dominant values. User ids usually distribute well; tenants and regions usually don't."},{"h":"Tenant sharding for B2B","d":"Natural isolation, per-tenant backup, export and deletion, and easier noisy-neighbour control. Requires a strategy for very large tenants (dedicated shards)."},{"h":"Region as a top-level constraint","d":"Data residency or latency can require that data lives in a region. Route by region first via a directory, then shard by user or tenant within the region."},{"h":"Directory or consistent hashing","d":"A lookup service mapping keys to shards gives flexibility (move one tenant) at the cost of a dependency. Hashing over many logical shards needs no lookup but moves data more coarsely."},{"h":"Cross-shard work","d":"Global aggregates, search and admin views go to projections, a warehouse or a search index. Cross-shard transactions use sagas."}],"decision":"Consumer: shard by user. B2B: shard by tenant with dedicated capacity for large tenants. Regulated or global: region first, then user or tenant. Always over-provision logical shards and keep a directory for exceptions.","numbers":["Over-provisioning: start with hundreds or thousands of logical shards on a few physical nodes so you can grow by moving shards for years without re-keying."],"pitfalls":["Sharding by region alone and getting one enormous shard for the biggest market.","Choosing a key that many important queries don't carry, making scatter-gather the norm.","No directory, so moving one problematic tenant requires rehashing everything.","Ignoring how to handle users who move regions or tenants that merge."],"followups":[{"q":"A user moves from the EU to the US. What happens to their data?","a":"Residency rules decide. Often data stays in the original region until a deliberate migration; the directory points to its current home, and the move is a copy, verify and switch."},{"q":"How do you run a global report?","a":"Not against shards directly: stream changes into a warehouse and query there, or maintain pre-aggregated counters per shard and sum them."},{"q":"When is consistent hashing better than a directory?","a":"When keys are uniform and you don't need per-key placement control. A directory wins when individual tenants need to be placed or moved deliberately."}],"example":"A B2B SaaS routes requests through a tenant directory to one of three regional clusters, and within each region hashes tenants across 1,024 logical shards. Two very large tenants have dedicated shards; global usage reporting runs in the warehouse.","related":["pick-partition-key-multi-tenant","hot-partition-shows-up-2","handle-multi-tenant-data-isolation","plan-data-residency-regional-regulations"],"sources":[{"title":"Martin Kleppmann: Designing Data-Intensive Applications, chapter 6 (Partitioning)","url":"https://dataintensive.net/"},{"title":"AWS Builders' Library: Workload isolation using shuffle-sharding","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/builders-library/workload-isolation-using-shuffle-sharding/"}]}},{"id":"design-indexes-table-takes-90","cat":"data","topic":"Indexing","level":"intermediate","source":"both","q":"Design indexes for a table that takes 90 percent writes.","also":["How should I index for read-heavy versus write-heavy workloads?"],"a":["Every index taxes writes. Keep the few that serve real queries, prefer covering indexes, consider LSM engines, and push ad hoc reads to a replica or search store.","Read-heavy: more indexes, covering indexes, materialized views. Write-heavy: few indexes, append-friendly storage, deferred or asynchronous secondary structures."],"tags":["Databases"],"legacy":[9,99],"answer":{"thesis":"On a write-heavy table every index is a tax on every write, so keep only indexes that serve real, frequent queries, make those covering where it pays, and push ad hoc or analytical reads to a replica, search index or warehouse. Consider an LSM-based engine if writes dominate at scale.","pitch":"With 90% writes, each secondary index multiplies write work: every insert updates every index, causing more I/O, more WAL or redo, more lock and latch contention, and more replication volume. So I'd start from the read queries that actually matter, by frequency and latency requirement, and give each the minimum index that serves it, ideally covering so the read doesn't touch the table. I'd drop unused or duplicate indexes, which databases can report. Primary key choice matters: an append-friendly, time-ordered key keeps inserts at the end of the B-tree instead of scattering them. Reads that don't need to be on the primary, such as reporting, search and exploration, move to a replica with extra indexes or to a different store. And if write volume is huge and mostly appends, an LSM-tree engine handles it better than a B-tree.","points":[{"h":"Every index costs on every write","d":"Inserts and updates must maintain each index, adding I/O, log volume, contention and replication traffic. Five indexes can mean several times the write work of the bare table."},{"h":"Index for real queries only","d":"Inventory queries by frequency and latency need. Use the database's statistics on index usage to find unused indexes and drop them."},{"h":"Covering and composite indexes","d":"One composite index in the right column order can serve several queries; including the selected columns avoids table lookups. Fewer, better indexes beat many narrow ones."},{"h":"Append-friendly primary keys","d":"Time-ordered keys (bigint sequences, UUIDv7) insert at the right edge of the B-tree. Random keys scatter writes and cause page splits."},{"h":"Move secondary reads elsewhere","d":"A replica can carry extra indexes if your database allows, or stream data to a search index or warehouse for exploration and reporting."},{"h":"Consider LSM engines","d":"LSM trees (RocksDB-based stores, Cassandra) turn random writes into sequential ones and handle very high ingest, trading some read cost and compaction overhead."},{"h":"Partition by time","d":"For append-mostly data, time partitions keep active indexes small and make retention a cheap partition drop instead of mass deletes."}],"decision":"Minimal indexes on the primary: the primary key and the one to three indexes that serve hot reads. Everything else on replicas or derived stores. Re-evaluate with real usage statistics every few months.","numbers":["Rough write amplification: a table with 1 primary key and 4 secondary indexes performs about 5 index updates per insert. Removing 2 unused indexes cuts index maintenance by about 40%.","Time partitioning: dropping a monthly partition is a metadata operation; deleting the same rows one by one generates a huge amount of log and vacuum work."],"pitfalls":["Adding an index for every slow query report without checking write impact.","Duplicate or overlapping indexes (an index on (a) and another on (a, b)).","Random UUID primary keys on a high-ingest table.","Deleting old rows individually instead of dropping partitions."],"followups":[{"q":"How do you add an index to a busy table safely?","a":"Use the database's online or concurrent index build, during lower traffic, while watching replication lag, and test the plan on production-size data first."},{"q":"A critical read is slow but you can't add another index. Options?","a":"Serve it from a cache or materialised view updated asynchronously, or from a replica with its own index, or reshape the query to use an existing composite index."},{"q":"When does an LSM engine hurt?","a":"Read-heavy workloads with many point lookups across levels, and workloads with heavy updates or deletes that create compaction pressure."}],"example":"An event ingestion table took 20,000 inserts/s with seven indexes and lagging replication. Usage stats showed three indexes unused and two overlapping. Reducing to three indexes and partitioning by day cut write latency in half and replication lag disappeared; investigative queries moved to the warehouse.","related":["model-data-differently-oltp-versus","generate-unique-ids-across-regions","design-retention-soft-delete-legal"],"sources":[{"title":"Markus Winand: Use The Index, Luke","url":"https://use-the-index-luke.com/"},{"title":"Martin Kleppmann: Designing Data-Intensive Applications, chapter 3 (B-trees and LSM-trees)","url":"https://dataintensive.net/"}]}},{"id":"migrate-billion-row-table-new","cat":"data","topic":"Migrations & backfills","level":"advanced","source":"both","q":"Migrate a billion-row table to a new schema with zero downtime.","also":["How do I design a migration strategy for a huge table?"],"a":["Expand, dual write, throttled backfill, verify with checksums, shadow reads, cut over, then contract. Keep a rollback path until the old column has been unused for a full cycle.","Expand and contract with dual writes, a throttled resumable backfill, verification before cutover, a rollback window, and cleanup only after the old path is provably idle."],"tags":["Migration","Reliability","Databases"],"legacy":[10,91],"answer":{"thesis":"Migrate in phases that are each reversible: expand the schema, dual-write, backfill in throttled and resumable chunks, verify, shadow-read, cut over reads, then contract. At a billion rows the dangers are locks, replication lag and correctness drift, so every phase is observable and stoppable.","pitch":"I'd never do this as one big ALTER or copy. Phase one, expand: create the new table or columns without touching existing reads. Phase two, dual-write: the application writes both old and new shapes, ideally in one transaction, or via CDC from the old table, so new data lands in both. Phase three, backfill: copy existing rows in primary-key ranges with a throttle tied to replication lag and database load, idempotent and resumable from a checkpoint. Phase four, verify: row counts and checksums per key range, plus sampled deep comparisons. Phase five, shadow reads: read from both, serve the old, log mismatches. Phase six, cut over reads behind a flag, ramping gradually. Phase seven, stop writing the old shape and drop it only after a full cycle without rollback. At a billion rows the backfill might take days, which is fine as long as it's safe.","points":[{"h":"Expand without blocking","d":"Create new tables or nullable columns using online DDL where needed. Avoid operations that rewrite the table or take long exclusive locks."},{"h":"Dual-write new data","d":"Application writes both shapes in one transaction, or a CDC pipeline mirrors changes from old to new. Choose one and make it idempotent."},{"h":"Throttled, resumable backfill","d":"Process key ranges in batches (thousands of rows), checkpoint progress, and pause automatically when replication lag or latency rises. Upsert so reruns are safe and don't overwrite newer dual-written data."},{"h":"Verify before trusting","d":"Compare counts and checksums per range, then sample rows for field-level comparison. Fix and re-run ranges that differ."},{"h":"Shadow reads","d":"Read from both representations for a portion of traffic, serve the old result, and log mismatches. This catches transformation bugs with production queries."},{"h":"Cut over gradually","d":"Switch reads behind a flag by percentage or by tenant. Keep dual writes running so rollback is a flag flip."},{"h":"Contract last","d":"Stop old writes, wait a full business cycle, then drop old columns or tables. Archive if regulations require."}],"decision":"Use expand-and-contract with dual writes for any large table that must stay online. Use CDC-based copying when application changes are hard, and specialised online schema change tools (gh-ost, pt-online-schema-change) for in-place schema changes on MySQL.","numbers":["Backfill time: 1 billion rows at a throttled 20,000 rows/s is about 14 hours of pure copy time; with pauses for load, plan for one to three days.","Batch sizing: batches of 1,000–10,000 rows keep each transaction short, limiting lock time and replication bursts."],"pitfalls":["Backfill overwriting rows that dual writes already updated with newer data; use version or timestamp checks.","Ignoring replication lag, so replicas fall minutes behind and read-your-writes breaks.","No checkpointing, so a failure at row 800 million restarts from zero.","Dropping the old column in the same release as the cutover."],"followups":[{"q":"How do you handle rows updated during the backfill?","a":"Dual writes keep the new table current; the backfill uses insert-if-absent or compares versions so it never overwrites a newer value."},{"q":"How would you verify a billion rows efficiently?","a":"Checksums aggregated per key range in both stores; only ranges with mismatched checksums get row-level comparison."},{"q":"What if you can't change the application to dual-write?","a":"Use CDC from the old table into the new one, or an online schema change tool that uses triggers or the binlog to keep a shadow table in sync."}],"example":"Moving a 1.2-billion-row transactions table to a partitioned layout: dual writes via the application, a backfill throttled to keep replica lag under 2 seconds that ran for 40 hours, checksum verification per million-id range, a week of shadow reads with zero mismatches, then a tenant-by-tenant read cutover.","related":["safely-backfill-data-production","schema-changes-downtime","plan-rollback-release-has-already","design-migration-paths-legacy-system"],"sources":[{"title":"Martin Fowler: Parallel change (expand and contract)","url":"https://martinfowler.com/bliki/ParallelChange.html"},{"title":"GitHub: gh-ost, online schema migration for MySQL","url":"https://github.com/github/gh-ost"}]}},{"id":"safely-backfill-data-production","cat":"data","topic":"Migrations & backfills","level":"advanced","source":"ref","q":"How do I safely backfill data in production?","also":[],"a":["Throttle against production load, make the job idempotent and resumable, process in key ranges, verify with counts and checksums, and keep it observable and stoppable."],"tags":["Migration","Databases"],"legacy":[56],"answer":{"thesis":"A safe backfill is idempotent, resumable, throttled against live load, verifiable and stoppable. Treat it as a production service with metrics and a kill switch, not a one-off script.","pitch":"I'd write the backfill so running it twice is harmless: compute values deterministically and upsert, or skip rows already processed, and never overwrite newer data written by live traffic. It processes data in key ranges with a persistent checkpoint, so it can stop and resume anywhere. Its rate adapts to the database's health: back off when replication lag, CPU or p99 latency rise, and run harder off-peak. It emits progress, error counts and throughput, and has a kill switch. Before the full run I'd test it on a production snapshot and then on a small slice of production, and afterwards verify with counts, checksums or sampled comparisons. And I'd decide in advance what happens with rows that fail: skip and log them for a retry pass, not abort the whole job.","points":[{"h":"Idempotent by design","d":"Deterministic transformation plus upsert, or a processed marker. Use conditional writes so a backfilled value never replaces a newer value from live traffic."},{"h":"Resumable with checkpoints","d":"Iterate by primary key ranges and store the last completed key durably. Avoid OFFSET pagination, which gets slower and skips or repeats rows as data changes."},{"h":"Adaptive throttling","d":"Measure replication lag, database CPU and application latency; slow down or pause when thresholds are crossed. Fixed sleeps are a weak substitute."},{"h":"Observable and stoppable","d":"Dashboards for progress, rate, errors and estimated completion. A feature flag or config to pause instantly."},{"h":"Test on realistic data","d":"Run against a recent production snapshot to catch edge cases (nulls, legacy formats, huge rows), then a small production slice."},{"h":"Handle failures per row","d":"Log failing keys with reasons to a dead-letter table and continue. Re-run only those after fixing the cause."},{"h":"Verify the result","d":"Counts per range, checksums, and sample comparisons against expected values; sign off before switching anything to depend on the data."}],"decision":"Run as a managed job (a worker with checkpoints) for anything beyond a few million rows or anything touching hot tables. Small, cold-table backfills can be simpler, but still idempotent.","numbers":["Throttle example: target replication lag under 1 second and database CPU under 60%; batch 2,000 rows; if lag exceeds 1 second, halve the rate; if under 0.2 seconds, increase by 10%."],"pitfalls":["OFFSET/LIMIT pagination over a changing table.","Overwriting values that live traffic updated after the backfill read them.","One giant transaction that locks rows for minutes and floods the log.","Aborting the whole job on the first bad row."],"followups":[{"q":"The backfill needs data from another service. How do you avoid overloading it?","a":"Apply the same throttling to calls to that service, coordinate a rate with its owners, cache lookups, or use its bulk export instead of per-row calls."},{"q":"How do you estimate how long it will take?","a":"Measure throughput on a slice under the throttle, multiply by total rows, and add margin for pauses during peak hours."},{"q":"How do you backfill derived data like a search index?","a":"Same pattern, often simpler: rebuild into a new index alias, verify counts, then swap the alias atomically."}],"example":"Backfilling a new 'risk_band' column on 300 million loan rows: a worker processes 5,000-row ranges, writes only where risk_band is null, checkpoints after each batch, and pauses when replica lag exceeds 1 second. It completed in 30 hours with 212 rows logged for manual review.","related":["migrate-billion-row-table-new","schema-changes-downtime","design-pagination-massive-datasets-efficiently"],"sources":[{"title":"Markus Winand: We need tool support for keyset pagination (no OFFSET)","url":"https://use-the-index-luke.com/no-offset"},{"title":"Martin Fowler: Parallel change (expand and contract)","url":"https://martinfowler.com/bliki/ParallelChange.html"}]}},{"id":"schema-changes-downtime","cat":"data","topic":"Migrations & backfills","level":"advanced","source":"ref","q":"How do I do schema changes without downtime?","also":[],"a":["Expand and contract: add nullable, deploy code that writes both, backfill, switch reads, remove the old. Avoid long locks and test the online DDL path against production-size data."],"tags":["Migration","Databases"],"legacy":[70],"answer":{"thesis":"Zero-downtime schema changes come from two habits: never make a change that breaks the currently running code, and avoid operations that take long locks or rewrite large tables. Expand and contract across several deploys, using online DDL or shadow-table tools for the heavy steps.","pitch":"The application and the schema change at different times, so every schema change must work with both the old and new code. Adding is safe if it's nullable or has a default the database can apply without a rewrite. Renaming or changing a type is done as add new, write both, backfill, switch reads, then drop old, spread across releases. Then the lock problem: some DDL takes an exclusive lock or rewrites the table, and even a brief lock can queue behind a long-running transaction and block all traffic. So I'd set a short lock timeout and retry, build indexes concurrently, and for heavy changes use online tools like gh-ost for MySQL or the database's own online DDL. I'd test each migration against a production-sized copy and run it with monitoring and a way to abort.","points":[{"h":"Compatible with running code","d":"During deploys, old and new code run together. A migration must not remove or rename anything the old code uses, and must not require something the old code doesn't provide."},{"h":"Expand and contract across releases","d":"Add the new column or table, deploy code that writes both, backfill, deploy code that reads new, then remove old in a later release."},{"h":"Know which DDL locks or rewrites","d":"Behaviour differs by database and version: adding a column with a constant default is instant in recent Postgres and MySQL; changing a column type often rewrites the table."},{"h":"Lock timeouts protect traffic","d":"A DDL waiting for a lock blocks everything queued behind it. Set a short lock timeout (a few seconds) so the migration fails fast and retries instead of stalling production."},{"h":"Online index builds","d":"Use concurrent or online index creation. It takes longer and can fail, so check for invalid indexes afterwards."},{"h":"Shadow-table tools for heavy changes","d":"gh-ost and pt-online-schema-change copy the table in the background, keep it in sync, and swap names at the end, avoiding long locks."},{"h":"Rehearse on production-size data","d":"A migration that takes 2 seconds on staging can take an hour on production. Test timings and lock behaviour on a realistic copy."}],"decision":"Additive changes can ship any time with lock timeouts. Renames, type changes and drops always use expand and contract over multiple releases. Heavy rewrites on large tables use online tools during low-traffic windows.","numbers":["Lock timeout example: lock_timeout of 2–5 seconds on migration sessions; if it fails, retry with backoff rather than holding a queue of blocked queries."],"pitfalls":["Renaming a column in one step while old code still reads it.","A migration waiting on a lock behind a long analytics query, blocking all writes.","Adding a NOT NULL column without a default on an old database version that rewrites the table.","Running migrations automatically on deploy without timing tests on production-size data."],"followups":[{"q":"How do you make a column NOT NULL safely?","a":"Add it nullable, backfill, add a check constraint as not validated, validate it separately (which doesn't block writes in Postgres), then set NOT NULL."},{"q":"Who runs migrations: the app on startup or a separate step?","a":"A separate, controlled step in the pipeline, with lock timeouts and monitoring, so many app instances don't race to run it and failures don't block deploys."},{"q":"How do you handle a migration that fails halfway?","a":"Make each migration small and transactional where the database supports transactional DDL; otherwise make it idempotent so it can be rerun."}],"example":"Renaming 'amount' to 'amount_minor' on a 400-million-row payments table: release 1 adds amount_minor; release 2 writes both; a throttled backfill fills history; release 3 reads amount_minor; release 4, two weeks later, drops amount. No single step held a lock longer than a second.","related":["migrate-billion-row-table-new","plan-rollback-release-has-already","safely-backfill-data-production"],"sources":[{"title":"GitHub: gh-ost, online schema migration for MySQL","url":"https://github.com/github/gh-ost"},{"title":"PostgreSQL documentation: ALTER TABLE","url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/sql-altertable.html"}]}},{"id":"design-retention-soft-delete-legal","cat":"data","topic":"Retention & deletion","level":"intermediate","source":"both","q":"Design retention, soft delete and legal erasure so they do not contradict each other.","also":["How do I design soft delete, archival, and retention?"],"a":["Different clocks for each: soft delete for undo, retention for policy, erasure for law. Cover backups, derived stores, logs, and how you prove deletion happened.","Deleted flag or tombstone for undo, tiered storage for old data, retention policies with automated purge, and explicit handling for legal deletion and backups."],"tags":["Cost & compliance","Databases"],"legacy":[11,100],"answer":{"thesis":"Soft delete, retention and legal erasure run on different clocks for different reasons: undo, policy and law. Model them separately, decide which wins when they conflict (usually a legal hold or regulatory retention beats an erasure request, and erasure beats ordinary retention), and make sure each one reaches backups, logs and derived stores.","pitch":"I'd separate the three. Soft delete is a product feature: a deleted_at marker so users can undo, with queries filtering it out, and a purge after, say, 30 days. Retention is policy: how long each data class is kept, like logs for 90 days or transaction records for years if regulation requires it, enforced by automated purges, ideally partition drops. Erasure is a legal right in many jurisdictions: delete or anonymise a person's personal data on request, within a deadline. They conflict, for example when someone asks for erasure but financial regulation requires keeping their loan records, so I'd write the precedence down: legal holds and regulatory retention win, but only for the data they cover, and the rest is erased. Then the hard part is coverage: replicas, caches, search indexes, the warehouse, logs, and backups. For backups, crypto-shredding helps: encrypt each person's data with their own key and delete the key.","points":[{"h":"Soft delete for undo","d":"deleted_at column, default query filters, unique constraints that account for deleted rows, and a scheduled hard purge after the undo window."},{"h":"Retention per data class","d":"Classify data (transactions, logs, analytics events, support tickets) with a retention period and a legal basis. Automate purges; time partitioning makes them cheap."},{"h":"Erasure as a workflow","d":"Receive and verify the request, find the person's data across systems by stable ids, delete or anonymise, record completion, and meet the statutory deadline."},{"h":"Precedence rules","d":"Legal hold > regulatory retention > erasure request > ordinary retention > soft delete. Apply per data class, so retained financial records don't keep unrelated marketing data alive."},{"h":"Coverage beyond the primary","d":"Caches, search indexes, the warehouse, event streams, logs and third-party processors all hold copies. Deletes must propagate (tombstones, CDC) or be scheduled per system."},{"h":"Backups and crypto-shredding","d":"Rewriting backups is impractical. Either let them expire within a known window and re-apply erasures on restore, or encrypt personal data per subject and destroy the key."},{"h":"Prove it","d":"Keep an audit log of what was deleted, when and where, without storing the deleted personal data itself."}],"decision":"Model the three explicitly with a data inventory. Use anonymisation where records must be kept for aggregate or regulatory reasons but identity needn't be. Use crypto-shredding for data that ends up in immutable stores.","numbers":["Example policy: soft-delete undo window 30 days; application logs 90 days; loan and payment records retained for the regulatory period (often several years); erasure requests completed within the statutory deadline, for example one month under GDPR."],"pitfalls":["Soft-deleted rows still visible through a search index or analytics table.","Erasure that removes the main record but leaves personal data in logs, events or backups.","Retention purges that delete data under a legal hold.","No data inventory, so nobody knows every place a customer's data lives."],"followups":[{"q":"A customer with an active loan requests erasure. What happens?","a":"Data needed to service the loan and meet regulatory retention is kept with that legal basis documented; everything else (marketing preferences, analytics identity) is erased now, and the retained data is erased when the obligation ends."},{"q":"How does erasure work with an event log you can't rewrite?","a":"Keep personal data out of events where possible (reference ids instead), or encrypt personal fields per subject and delete the key."},{"q":"How do you find all the data for one person?","a":"A data inventory mapping systems to data classes, a stable subject id carried everywhere, and per-system erasure handlers invoked by one orchestrated workflow."}],"example":"A lender soft-deletes draft applications for 30 days, retains loan records for the regulatory period, and handles erasure via a workflow that anonymises marketing and analytics data immediately, flags retained loan data with its legal basis, and destroys the subject's encryption key once retention ends.","related":["boundary-between-oltp-olap-data","plan-data-residency-regional-regulations","should-each-layer-log-must"],"sources":[{"title":"GDPR Article 17: Right to erasure","url":"https://gdpr-info.eu/art-17-gdpr/"},{"title":"GDPR Article 12: Time limits for responding to requests","url":"https://gdpr-info.eu/art-12-gdpr/"}]}},{"id":"handle-multi-tenant-data-isolation","cat":"data","topic":"Multi-tenancy","level":"intermediate","source":"ref","q":"How do I handle multi-tenant data isolation?","also":[],"a":["Row-level with tenant id on every query and enforced in the data layer, schema per tenant, or database per tenant. Trade isolation strength against cost and operational load. Plan noisy-neighbor controls and tenant export."],"tags":["Sharding","Reliability"],"legacy":[55],"answer":{"thesis":"Pick an isolation model per tier, not one for everyone: shared tables with a tenant id for most tenants, and schema- or database-per-tenant for those who need stronger isolation or have outgrown the pool. Enforce tenant scoping in the data layer, not in each query, and design noisy-neighbour controls and tenant export from the start.","pitch":"There are three models. Pooled: shared tables with tenant_id on every row; cheapest and easiest to operate, weakest isolation. Schema-per-tenant: separate schemas in a shared database; better logical isolation, but migrations run per schema and catalogues grow. Database- or cluster-per-tenant: strongest isolation and per-tenant backup, restore and residency, at much higher cost and operational load. I'd usually run pooled for most tenants and offer dedicated databases for large or regulated tenants. In the pooled model, the key is enforcement: tenant id must come from the authenticated context, never from request parameters, and be applied automatically, through row-level security in Postgres or a mandatory tenant filter in the data access layer, with tests that try cross-tenant access. Then per-tenant quotas and rate limits for noisy neighbours, per-tenant encryption keys if required, and tooling for tenant export, deletion and moving a tenant between models.","points":[{"h":"Pooled: shared tables, tenant_id everywhere","d":"Most efficient and simplest to migrate and monitor. Isolation depends entirely on correct scoping, so enforcement must be systematic."},{"h":"Schema-per-tenant","d":"Clearer separation and easy per-tenant export. Migrations must run across thousands of schemas, and some databases degrade with very large catalogues."},{"h":"Database-per-tenant","d":"Strong isolation, per-tenant performance, backups, residency and encryption. Costly at small sizes; needs automation for provisioning and migrations."},{"h":"Enforce scoping in the data layer","d":"Derive tenant id from authentication, set it in the session, and apply it via row-level security or a mandatory repository filter. Code reviews alone aren't enforcement."},{"h":"Test for cross-tenant leaks","d":"Automated tests that authenticate as tenant A and attempt to read tenant B's resources by id across every endpoint."},{"h":"Noisy neighbours","d":"Per-tenant rate limits, query timeouts, concurrency caps and resource pools. Watch per-tenant resource usage to spot those who need to move."},{"h":"Tenant lifecycle tooling","d":"Export, deletion, and migration of a tenant from pooled to dedicated without downtime."}],"decision":"Pooled by default with row-level security or an enforced filter. Dedicated databases for tenants who pay for it, are regulated, or are large enough to disturb others. Schema-per-tenant mainly when tenant counts are modest and per-tenant customisation is needed.","numbers":["Cost intuition: pooled can host thousands of small tenants on one cluster; database-per-tenant has a floor cost per tenant (instance, backups, monitoring) that dominates for small tenants."],"pitfalls":["Taking tenant id from a request parameter or header the client controls.","One missed WHERE tenant_id = ? in a new query, leaking data.","Caches keyed without tenant id, serving one tenant's data to another.","No path to move a large tenant out of the pool."],"followups":[{"q":"How does row-level security work in practice?","a":"The application sets a session variable with the tenant id after authentication; policies on each table restrict rows to that tenant, so a query missing a filter still can't see other tenants."},{"q":"How do you run migrations across 5,000 tenant databases?","a":"An orchestrated rollout with batches, canaries, retries and a status table, and backward-compatible changes so tenants at different versions keep working."},{"q":"A tenant asks for their own encryption keys. Can pooled support that?","a":"Partially, with per-tenant data keys for application-level encryption of sensitive fields; full key isolation is easier with dedicated databases."}],"example":"A SaaS runs 3,000 tenants pooled in Postgres with row-level security tied to a session tenant id, and 12 enterprise tenants on dedicated clusters provisioned from the same infrastructure code. A nightly test suite tries cross-tenant reads on every API endpoint.","related":["pick-partition-key-multi-tenant","shard-data-user-tenant-region","design-per-tenant-rate-limits","plan-data-residency-regional-regulations"],"sources":[{"title":"PostgreSQL documentation: Row security policies","url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/ddl-rowsecurity.html"},{"title":"AWS Builders' Library: Workload isolation using shuffle-sharding","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/builders-library/workload-isolation-using-shuffle-sharding/"}]}},{"id":"should-cache-client-edge-service","cat":"caching","topic":"Caching strategy","level":"fundamental","source":"ref","q":"Where should I cache: client, edge, service, or DB layer?","also":[],"a":["Closer to the user means lower latency and weaker control over invalidation. Match the layer to data volatility and sharing: edge for public static, service for computed results, DB layer for hot rows."],"tags":["Caching","Latency"],"legacy":[57],"answer":{"thesis":"Cache as close to the user as the data's sharing and freshness allow. The closer the cache, the bigger the latency win and the weaker your control over invalidation, so match each layer to how volatile and how shared the data is.","pitch":"I'd walk the layers. The client, meaning the browser or app, is best for per-user data and static assets with long cache lifetimes and versioned file names; it removes requests entirely but you can't invalidate it, so you rely on short TTLs or versioning. The edge or CDN is best for public or per-region content shared by many users, like images, scripts and catalogue pages, with TTLs, purge APIs and stale-while-revalidate. The service-side cache, Redis or Memcached, is best for computed results and hot objects shared across instances, where you control invalidation precisely. In-process caches are the fastest but per instance, fine for small, slow-changing reference data. The database's own buffer cache already holds hot pages; a cache in front helps when queries are expensive, not just when rows are hot. So the choice is driven by who shares the data, how stale it can be, and whether you need to invalidate it on demand.","points":[{"h":"Client cache","d":"HTTP caching headers (Cache-Control, ETag) and app storage. Zero network cost on hits. Use content hashes in asset URLs so you can cache 'forever' and still deploy changes."},{"h":"Edge / CDN","d":"Shared across users in a region, absorbs most traffic for public content, and protects the origin. Supports TTLs, purges and serving stale content when the origin is down."},{"h":"Service-side shared cache","d":"Redis or Memcached for computed results, sessions, and hot objects. Precise invalidation and data structures, at the cost of a network hop and another system to run."},{"h":"In-process cache","d":"Fastest (no network), but duplicated per instance and inconsistent across them. Good for reference data with short TTLs."},{"h":"Database layer","d":"Buffer pools and query caches. Materialised views for expensive aggregations. A separate cache helps when the work, not the I/O, is expensive."},{"h":"Choose by sharing and volatility","d":"Personal and stable: client. Public and stable: edge. Shared and changing: service cache with invalidation. Highly volatile or must be correct: maybe don't cache."}],"decision":"Layer them: versioned static assets at client and edge with long TTLs; public pages at the edge with short TTLs and stale-while-revalidate; computed and per-user data in a service cache; nothing cached that must be strictly current (balances used for decisions).","numbers":["Latency intuition: client hit ~0 ms, edge hit typically tens of milliseconds closer than origin, service cache hit around a millisecond in-zone, in-process hit microseconds."],"pitfalls":["Caching personalised responses at the edge without varying on user, leaking one user's page to another.","Long client TTLs on non-versioned URLs, so users keep stale code after a deploy.","Caching everything in-process across 100 instances, multiplying memory and staleness.","Caching data used for correctness decisions."],"followups":[{"q":"How do you cache personalised pages?","a":"Cache the shared shell at the edge and fetch personal fragments separately, or use edge logic that varies by a coarse segment instead of individual user."},{"q":"What does stale-while-revalidate give you?","a":"The cache serves a slightly stale response immediately while fetching a fresh one in the background, hiding origin latency and smoothing load."},{"q":"How do you invalidate CDN content quickly?","a":"Purge APIs by URL or tag (surrogate keys), or version the URL so new content has a new key."}],"example":"A product catalogue serves images and scripts from a CDN with year-long TTLs and hashed file names, product pages at the edge with a 60-second TTL and stale-while-revalidate, prices from a Redis cache invalidated on price change, and the cart directly from the database.","related":["design-cache-invalidation-rules-not","measure-whether-cache-actually-helping","read-traffic-grows-10x-one","walk-one-request-dns-database"],"sources":[{"title":"MDN: HTTP caching","url":"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Caching"},{"title":"AWS Builders' Library: Caching challenges and strategies","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/builders-library/caching-challenges-and-strategies/"}]}},{"id":"pick-between-write-through-write","cat":"caching","topic":"Caching strategy","level":"fundamental","source":"ref","q":"How do I pick between write-through, write-back, and write-around caching?","also":[],"a":["Write-through keeps cache and store aligned at write cost. Write-back is fast but risks loss on cache failure. Write-around avoids polluting the cache with data that is rarely read."],"tags":["Caching"],"legacy":[58],"answer":{"thesis":"Write-through updates cache and store together, keeping them aligned at the cost of write latency. Write-back acknowledges after updating the cache and writes the store later: fast, but risks loss. Write-around writes only to the store and lets reads populate the cache, avoiding pollution with rarely read data. Most application caches use cache-aside with write-around plus invalidation.","pitch":"The question is what happens on a write. Write-through: write to the store and the cache synchronously, so reads right after a write hit a fresh cache; writes are slower and you may cache data nobody reads. Write-back, or write-behind: write to the cache, acknowledge, and flush to the store asynchronously, which is great for write-heavy, loss-tolerant workloads like counters and metrics but dangerous for anything you can't lose, because a cache failure loses unflushed writes. Write-around: write only to the store, and the next read misses and loads it; best when written data is often not read soon, avoiding cache churn. In practice, for most services I'd use cache-aside: read from cache, on miss load from the database and populate; on write, update the database and delete the cache key, so the next read loads fresh data.","points":[{"h":"Write-through","d":"Synchronous write to store and cache. Reads after writes are fresh. Write latency includes both, and rarely read data still occupies cache."},{"h":"Write-back (write-behind)","d":"Write to cache, acknowledge, persist later in batches. Lowest write latency and fewer store writes. Data loss if the cache fails before flushing; ordering and durability need care."},{"h":"Write-around","d":"Write to the store only. The cache fills on read. Avoids polluting the cache with write-once data; first read after a write is a miss."},{"h":"Cache-aside, the common default","d":"Application owns caching: read-through on miss, and on write update the database then delete (not update) the cache key."},{"h":"Delete rather than update on write","d":"Updating the cache on write can race with concurrent writes and leave a stale value. Deleting forces a fresh load; combined with TTLs, it bounds staleness."},{"h":"Durability decides write-back","d":"Use write-back only when losing a window of writes is acceptable or the cache itself is durable and replicated."}],"decision":"Cache-aside with delete-on-write for most application data. Write-through when reads immediately follow writes and a miss is expensive. Write-back for high-volume, loss-tolerant aggregates (view counts, rate counters). Write-around for write-heavy data that's rarely read back soon.","numbers":["Write-back batching: 10,000 counter increments/s flushed every second as aggregated updates per key can turn into a few hundred database writes/s instead of 10,000."],"pitfalls":["Write-back for financial or order data, losing writes on a cache node failure.","Update-on-write races: two writers interleave and the cache ends up with the older value.","Write-through for data that's rarely read, filling the cache with cold entries.","Forgetting that the cache and the database can't be updated atomically; design for brief inconsistency."],"followups":[{"q":"Why delete instead of update on write?","a":"With concurrent writers, 'update database then set cache' can apply in a different order to each store, leaving stale data. Delete makes the next read load whatever is current."},{"q":"Can delete-on-write still leave stale data?","a":"Yes, a reader can load an old value just before the write and put it into the cache just after the delete. TTLs bound the staleness; versioned keys or leases close the gap further."},{"q":"When would you use a read-through cache library?","a":"When you want the caching logic centralised (the cache loads from the store on miss), which simplifies application code but couples the cache to the data source."}],"example":"A social app keeps post view counts with write-back: increments go to Redis and are flushed every 5 seconds, accepting the loss of a few seconds of counts on failure. Post content uses cache-aside: edits update Postgres and delete the cached post.","related":["design-cache-invalidation-rules-not","should-cache-client-edge-service","handle-cache-stampede-thundering-herd"],"sources":[{"title":"AWS Builders' Library: Caching challenges and strategies","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/builders-library/caching-challenges-and-strategies/"},{"title":"Nishtala et al.: Scaling Memcache at Facebook (NSDI 2013)","url":"https://www.usenix.org/conference/nsdi13/technical-sessions/presentation/nishtala"}]}},{"id":"design-cache-invalidation-rules-not","cat":"caching","topic":"Caching strategy","level":"intermediate","source":"ref","q":"How do I design cache invalidation rules that do not become a nightmare?","also":[],"a":["Prefer TTLs with jitter plus event-driven invalidation for the few cases that need it. Version keys instead of deleting, keep key construction in one place, and accept bounded staleness explicitly."],"tags":["Caching"],"legacy":[59],"answer":{"thesis":"Make staleness a decision, not an accident: give every cached item a TTL with jitter that states how stale it may be, add event-driven invalidation only for data that must change faster than the TTL, prefer versioned keys over deletes, and build keys in one place.","pitch":"Invalidation gets nightmarish when it's ad hoc: every code path that writes data has to remember every cache key derived from it. I'd simplify with four rules. First, every entry has a TTL that encodes acceptable staleness for that data, with jitter so entries don't expire together. Second, for data that must update quickly, like prices or permissions, invalidate on change via events, so the service that owns the data publishes a change event and cache owners react, rather than writers calling caches directly. Third, where possible use versioned keys: include a version or updated-at in the key so a change creates a new key and old entries simply age out, which avoids races between delete and refill. Fourth, centralise key construction in one module so the mapping from data to keys is in one place. And I'd accept bounded staleness explicitly in product requirements.","points":[{"h":"TTL as a staleness contract","d":"Choose a TTL per data type from how stale it may be (catalogue description: an hour; price: a minute; permissions: seconds). Every entry has one, as a safety net."},{"h":"Jitter","d":"Randomise TTLs (for example ±10%) so entries created together don't expire together and cause a load spike."},{"h":"Event-driven invalidation for fast-changing data","d":"The owning service publishes change events; cache layers subscribe and delete or refresh affected keys. Writers don't need to know about every cache."},{"h":"Versioned keys","d":"Key includes a version (product:123:v42). Updates bump the version; readers use the new key, and old entries expire. No delete-refill race."},{"h":"One place for key construction","d":"A shared module builds keys from inputs, so invalidation logic and read logic agree, and you can find every key derived from an entity."},{"h":"Bounded staleness is a product decision","d":"Agree how stale each view may be, write it down, and monitor it. Then cache behaviour is testable."}],"decision":"TTL plus jitter for everything; event-driven invalidation for the few data types with tight freshness needs; versioned keys where races matter; don't cache data where any staleness is unacceptable.","numbers":["Jitter example: a 300-second TTL with ±30 seconds of jitter spreads expirations of a batch of keys over a minute instead of one instant."],"pitfalls":["No TTL on entries 'because we invalidate', so a missed invalidation means stale data forever.","Writers calling cache deletes for keys they know about, missing derived keys (lists, aggregates).","Identical TTLs on a batch of keys, causing synchronised expiry.","Key construction duplicated across services with slight differences."],"followups":[{"q":"How do you invalidate derived data like a list of a user's posts?","a":"Either keep short TTLs on lists, invalidate list keys on post change events, or store lists as ids and fetch items separately so only item caches need invalidation."},{"q":"What if the invalidation event is lost?","a":"The TTL bounds the damage. For critical data, use versioned keys or check a version stamp on read."},{"q":"How do you test invalidation?","a":"Integration tests that write through the real path and assert the next read reflects it, plus monitoring of a sample of cache entries against the source."}],"example":"A marketplace caches listings for 10 minutes with jitter, but prices use versioned keys bumped by a PriceChanged event, so price updates appear within a second while descriptions can lag.","related":["pick-between-write-through-write","handle-cache-stampede-thundering-herd","should-cache-client-edge-service","give-users-read-writes-reads"],"sources":[{"title":"AWS Builders' Library: Caching challenges and strategies","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/builders-library/caching-challenges-and-strategies/"},{"title":"Nishtala et al.: Scaling Memcache at Facebook (NSDI 2013)","url":"https://www.usenix.org/conference/nsdi13/technical-sessions/presentation/nishtala"}]}},{"id":"handle-cache-stampede-thundering-herd","cat":"caching","topic":"Caching strategy","level":"intermediate","source":"ref","q":"How do I handle cache stampede and thundering herd?","also":[],"a":["Request coalescing so one caller rebuilds, stale-while-revalidate, TTL jitter, early refresh, and a lock or lease with a short timeout."],"tags":["Caching","Reliability"],"legacy":[60],"answer":{"thesis":"A stampede happens when a hot entry expires and many requests rebuild it at once. Make sure only one caller rebuilds (request coalescing or a lease), let others use stale data while it does (stale-while-revalidate), and avoid synchronised expiry (jitter, early refresh).","pitch":"When a popular key expires, every concurrent request misses and goes to the database at the same moment, which can take the database down and keep the cache empty. I'd layer defences. Request coalescing: within a process, concurrent misses for a key wait on one in-flight load, often called single-flight. Across processes, a short lease or lock in the cache, as described in Facebook's memcache paper, where the first misser gets the right to rebuild and others wait briefly or get the stale value. Stale-while-revalidate: keep the old value past its soft TTL and serve it while one caller refreshes in the background. Probabilistic early refresh: as an entry nears expiry, a small and increasing fraction of requests refresh it early, so it rarely expires under load. And jitter on TTLs so a batch of keys doesn't expire together. For extreme hot keys, pre-warm or refresh them on a schedule.","points":[{"h":"Understand the failure","d":"Hot key expires, N concurrent requests miss, N identical database queries run. If the query is slow, the herd grows while it runs."},{"h":"In-process request coalescing","d":"Concurrent requests for the same key share one in-flight load (single-flight). Cuts the herd to one per instance."},{"h":"Distributed leases or locks","d":"First miss acquires a short-lived lease in the cache; others wait briefly, retry, or receive stale data. The lease must expire in case the holder dies."},{"h":"Stale-while-revalidate","d":"Store a soft expiry and a hard expiry. After soft expiry, serve stale and refresh in the background; only after hard expiry do callers block."},{"h":"Probabilistic early expiration","d":"Each read near expiry refreshes with a probability that rises as expiry approaches, so refresh happens before expiry, spread across time."},{"h":"Jitter and pre-warming","d":"Randomised TTLs avoid mass expiry; scheduled refresh of known hot keys keeps them always warm."},{"h":"Protect the origin anyway","d":"Concurrency limits on the expensive query path, so even a stampede that gets through can't overwhelm the database."}],"decision":"Single-flight in every service that caches; stale-while-revalidate for hot, tolerably stale data; distributed leases for very expensive rebuilds; jitter always.","numbers":["Herd size: a key read 5,000 times/s with a 200 ms rebuild query sees about 1,000 concurrent misses without coalescing; with single-flight across 20 instances, 20; with a distributed lease, 1."],"pitfalls":["Locks without expiry: a crashed holder leaves the key locked and everyone waiting.","Waiting callers with no timeout, piling up behind a slow rebuild.","Coalescing only in-process while running hundreds of instances.","Cold start after a cache flush or failover with no warming plan."],"followups":[{"q":"The whole cache cluster restarts. How do you avoid a database meltdown?","a":"Warm the hottest keys before taking traffic, ramp traffic gradually, keep coalescing and concurrency limits on the database path, and consider a replicated cache so a node loss doesn't empty everything."},{"q":"How does stale-while-revalidate interact with correctness?","a":"It serves data up to soft TTL plus refresh time old. Only use it for data where that's acceptable."},{"q":"What about negative caching?","a":"Cache 'not found' results briefly too, or a flood of requests for a missing key hammers the database."}],"example":"A homepage feed cached for 60 seconds caused a database spike every minute at peak. Adding single-flight, a 10-second stale-while-revalidate window, and ±10% TTL jitter flattened database load to one rebuild per key per minute.","related":["design-cache-invalidation-rules-not","caching-story-hot-keys-large","design-admission-control-service-stays","measure-whether-cache-actually-helping"],"sources":[{"title":"Nishtala et al.: Scaling Memcache at Facebook (leases)","url":"https://www.usenix.org/conference/nsdi13/technical-sessions/presentation/nishtala"},{"title":"RFC 5861: HTTP Cache-Control extensions for stale content","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5861"}]}},{"id":"caching-story-hot-keys-large","cat":"caching","topic":"Caching strategy","level":"intermediate","source":"ref","q":"What is my caching story for hot keys and large objects?","also":[],"a":["Replicate hot keys across nodes or add a local near-cache, split large values, compress, and cap object size. Watch for one key saturating one cache shard."],"tags":["Caching","Scaling"],"legacy":[61],"answer":{"thesis":"Hot keys concentrate load on one cache shard; large objects waste memory, bandwidth and latency. Spread hot keys (local near-caches, replicas of the key) and break up or shrink large values (split, compress, cap size), and watch per-shard load so one key can't saturate one node.","pitch":"In a sharded cache, each key lives on one node, so a very hot key, like a celebrity profile or a flash-sale product, can saturate that node's CPU or network while the rest idle. I'd first add a small in-process near-cache with a short TTL in each service instance, which absorbs most reads for the hottest keys. For extreme cases, replicate the key: store it under N suffixed keys on different shards and read a random one, accepting slightly more work on updates. For large objects, the problems are memory pressure, eviction of many small useful keys, slow network transfer and serialisation, and blocking on single-threaded caches like Redis. So I'd cap object size, compress, split large values into chunks or store only the frequently used parts, and keep really large blobs in object storage with the cache holding metadata or a pointer. And monitor per-shard ops and bandwidth plus a top-keys report.","points":[{"h":"Detect hot keys","d":"Per-shard metrics (ops/s, network, CPU) and top-key sampling. One shard far above the others usually means a hot key."},{"h":"Near-cache in each instance","d":"A tiny in-process cache with a TTL of a few seconds for the hottest keys removes most network reads. Accept a few seconds of staleness."},{"h":"Replicate hot keys","d":"Write key:1..key:N across shards; readers pick one at random. Updates write all copies or use short TTLs."},{"h":"Cap and compress large values","d":"Set a maximum size; compress (often a big win for JSON); avoid storing large blobs that are rarely read in full."},{"h":"Split or reshape large objects","d":"Store parts separately (profile summary vs full history) so common reads fetch only what they need."},{"h":"Large blobs belong elsewhere","d":"Images, documents and big payloads go to object storage or a CDN; cache a pointer or metadata."},{"h":"Mind single-threaded caches","d":"In Redis, a command on a very large value or a huge collection blocks other requests on that node."}],"decision":"Near-cache for read-hot keys with tolerable staleness; key replication for extreme cases; size caps, compression and reshaping for large values; object storage for blobs.","numbers":["Network math: a 500 KB value read 2,000 times/s is 1 GB/s from one shard, enough to saturate a typical node's network. A near-cache with a 2-second TTL across 50 instances cuts that to about 25 reads/s.","Compression: JSON often compresses several times over with fast codecs, trading a little CPU for memory and bandwidth."],"pitfalls":["Assuming consistent hashing spreads load; it spreads keys, not traffic to one key.","Storing multi-megabyte objects in Redis and blocking the event loop.","Near-caches with long TTLs, serving stale data for minutes.","Large values evicting many small hot ones under memory pressure."],"followups":[{"q":"How do you update a replicated hot key consistently?","a":"Write all replicas (or bump a version used in the key) and rely on short TTLs to cover any copy that missed the update."},{"q":"How do you find big keys in Redis?","a":"Use sampling tools such as redis-cli --bigkeys or memory usage commands, and enforce size limits in the client library."},{"q":"When is a near-cache dangerous?","a":"For data that must be consistent across instances, such as permissions just revoked. Keep TTLs very short or invalidate via events."}],"example":"During a flash sale one product key took 40% of all cache traffic and saturated a shard. A 1-second near-cache in each API instance dropped that shard's load by 95%; product detail objects were also split so the sale page fetched a 4 KB summary instead of a 300 KB document.","related":["hot-partition-shows-up-2","handle-cache-stampede-thundering-herd","should-cache-client-edge-service"],"sources":[{"title":"AWS Builders' Library: Caching challenges and strategies","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/builders-library/caching-challenges-and-strategies/"},{"title":"Redis documentation: Client-side caching","url":"https://redis.io/docs/latest/develop/reference/client-side-caching/"}]}},{"id":"measure-whether-cache-actually-helping","cat":"caching","topic":"Caching strategy","level":"fundamental","source":"ref","q":"How do I measure whether a cache is actually helping?","also":[],"a":["Hit ratio by key class, latency with and without, origin load saved, eviction rate, and cost per hit. A high hit ratio on cheap reads can still be pointless."],"tags":["Caching","Observability"],"legacy":[62],"answer":{"thesis":"A cache helps if it reduces user latency or origin cost enough to justify its cost and complexity. Measure hit ratio by key class, latency with and without hits, origin load saved, evictions, and cost per hit. A high hit ratio on cheap reads can still be pointless.","pitch":"I'd measure outcomes, not just hit ratio. Per key class, record hit ratio and the latency of hits versus misses, which tells you the latency saved per hit. Compare origin load, database queries or CPU, against what it would be without the cache, which you can estimate from misses plus hits that would have gone through. Track eviction rate and memory usage: high evictions with a low hit ratio means the cache is too small or the access pattern has a long tail. Then cost: cache cluster cost versus database capacity saved. And complexity cost: staleness bugs and invalidation code. Sometimes the answer is that the cache isn't helping: the query was already fast, or the keys are too diverse to ever hit. A clean experiment is to bypass the cache for a small percentage of traffic and compare latency and origin load directly.","points":[{"h":"Hit ratio per key class","d":"An overall hit ratio hides that some key classes hit 99% and others 5%. Break it down by key prefix or data type."},{"h":"Latency saved","d":"Measure p50 and p99 for hits and misses. If misses take 3 ms and hits 1 ms, the cache saves little; if misses take 300 ms, it matters a lot."},{"h":"Origin load saved","d":"Database queries or CPU avoided. This is often the real value: the database can be smaller or survive peaks."},{"h":"Evictions and memory","d":"High eviction rates with low hit ratio suggest the cache is too small or caching the wrong things. Look at time-to-eviction versus time-to-reuse."},{"h":"Cost per hit","d":"Cache infrastructure cost divided by hits, compared with the cost of serving those reads from the origin."},{"h":"Bypass experiment","d":"Route a small percentage of requests around the cache and compare user latency and origin load. It gives a direct counterfactual."},{"h":"Complexity and correctness cost","d":"Count staleness incidents and invalidation code. A cache that saves little but causes bugs should go."}],"decision":"Keep caches that measurably reduce user latency or origin capacity needs. Remove or shrink caches for already-fast queries or key classes with persistently low hit ratios.","numbers":["Expected latency = hit ratio × hit latency + (1 − hit ratio) × miss latency. At 90% hits, 1 ms hits and 50 ms misses: 0.9 + 5 = 5.9 ms average, versus 50 ms without the cache.","With 3 ms misses, the same 90% hit ratio gives 1.2 ms vs 3 ms: probably not worth a cluster."],"pitfalls":["Celebrating a 95% hit ratio on data that was cheap to fetch anyway.","Measuring only average latency and missing that misses dominate p99.","Ignoring evictions caused by a few large values.","Keeping a cache because removing it feels risky, without data."],"followups":[{"q":"Hit ratio dropped from 90% to 60% overnight. What do you check?","a":"A deploy that changed key construction, a new traffic pattern (crawler, new feature), memory pressure causing evictions, or a cache node failure that remapped keys."},{"q":"How do you size a cache?","a":"From the working set: measure how many distinct keys are reused within the TTL and their size; plot hit ratio against cache size using a replay or simulation of access logs."},{"q":"Should every service have a cache?","a":"No. Add one when measurements show expensive repeated reads; otherwise it's extra failure modes for little gain."}],"example":"A team found their Redis cache for user settings had a 97% hit ratio, but the database query took 1.5 ms at p99 and the cache hit 0.8 ms. They removed the cache, deleted the invalidation code and saw no change in user latency.","related":["should-cache-client-edge-service","design-cache-invalidation-rules-not","design-metrics-actually-tell-me"],"sources":[{"title":"AWS Builders' Library: Caching challenges and strategies","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/builders-library/caching-challenges-and-strategies/"}]}},{"id":"design-pagination-massive-datasets-efficiently","cat":"caching","topic":"Pagination","level":"intermediate","source":"ref","q":"How do I design pagination for massive datasets efficiently?","also":[],"a":["Keyset (cursor) pagination over offset, a stable sort key with a tiebreaker, bounded page size, and a plan for results that change between pages."],"tags":["Databases","Scaling"],"legacy":[63],"answer":{"thesis":"Use keyset (cursor) pagination: sort by a stable, indexed key with a unique tiebreaker and fetch rows after the last one seen. Offset pagination gets slower with every page and shifts when data changes. Bound page size and decide how results behave as data changes underneath.","pitch":"Offset pagination, LIMIT 50 OFFSET 100000, makes the database read and discard a hundred thousand rows to return fifty, so deep pages get slow, and if rows are inserted or deleted while a user pages, they see duplicates or miss items. Keyset pagination fixes both: order by an indexed column plus a unique tiebreaker, like (created_at, id), and ask for rows where (created_at, id) is less than the last row of the previous page, limit 50. Each page is an index range scan of fifty rows regardless of depth. I'd return an opaque cursor encoding the last key and the sort, so clients can't construct arbitrary positions and the server can change the encoding. Page size is capped. The trade-offs: no jumping to page 237, and total counts are expensive, so show approximate counts or 'more results' instead. For search engines, use their search-after or scroll mechanisms rather than deep from/size.","points":[{"h":"Why offset hurts","d":"OFFSET N reads and discards N rows, so cost grows linearly with depth. Concurrent inserts and deletes shift rows between pages, causing duplicates or skips."},{"h":"Keyset pagination","d":"WHERE (sort_key, id) < (:last_sort, :last_id) ORDER BY sort_key DESC, id DESC LIMIT :n. Uses the index directly; every page costs about the same."},{"h":"Stable sort with a tiebreaker","d":"The sort must be deterministic: add the primary key as a tiebreaker so rows with equal sort values don't flip between pages."},{"h":"Opaque cursors","d":"Encode the last key (and filters) into a signed or encoded token. Clients pass it back; the server can change the format without breaking them."},{"h":"Bounded page size","d":"Cap the limit (for example 100) to protect the database and response size."},{"h":"Counts and jumping","d":"Exact totals over huge filtered sets are expensive; use estimates, cached counts, or 'load more'. Arbitrary page jumps aren't supported; offer filters and search instead."},{"h":"Changing data","d":"Keyset pagination naturally shows new items only at the start; items edited to move in the sort order may appear twice or be skipped. Snapshot reads are an option if exact consistency matters."}],"decision":"Keyset pagination for any large or growing dataset and all APIs. Offset only for small, bounded result sets or admin tools where deep pages are rare. Search engines: search_after with a point-in-time view for deep result sets.","numbers":["Depth cost: page 2,000 at 50 per page with OFFSET reads about 100,000 rows; keyset reads about 50 rows via the index for any page."],"pitfalls":["Sorting by a non-unique column without a tiebreaker.","No index matching the sort and filter columns, so keyset pagination still scans.","Exposing raw keys as cursors and letting clients craft them.","Computing COUNT(*) over millions of rows on every page request."],"followups":[{"q":"How do you support both forward and backward paging?","a":"Store both first and last keys in the cursor and flip the comparison and sort direction for previous pages, then reverse the results."},{"q":"What if the user changes filters or sort?","a":"The cursor encodes them; a mismatch invalidates the cursor and the client starts from the first page."},{"q":"How do you export all rows of a huge table?","a":"Keyset iteration by primary key in batches, ideally from a replica or snapshot, not offset paging through an API."}],"example":"A transactions API switched from offset to keyset pagination on (posted_at, id). Page latency at depth 10,000 went from 4 seconds to 15 ms, and clients stopped seeing duplicate transactions while new ones arrived.","related":["safely-backfill-data-production","design-indexes-table-takes-90","version-public-api-cannot-force"],"sources":[{"title":"Markus Winand: No offset (keyset pagination)","url":"https://use-the-index-luke.com/no-offset"},{"title":"Elasticsearch documentation: Paginate search results","url":"https://www.elastic.co/guide/en/elasticsearch/reference/current/paginate-search-results.html"}]}},{"id":"build-idempotency-write-api-clients","cat":"consistency","topic":"Idempotency","level":"advanced","source":"both","q":"Build idempotency for a write API that clients and your own gateway can both retry.","also":["How do I handle idempotency for write APIs?"],"a":["Client-supplied key, atomic claim of the key with the write, stored response for replays, key expiry, and the in-flight duplicate case. Say what you do when the request body differs.","Idempotency key per logical operation, atomically recorded with the write, replayed response on duplicates, a defined behavior for a duplicate that arrives while the first is still running, and key expiry."],"tags":["Consistency","APIs","Databases"],"legacy":[13,90],"answer":{"thesis":"The client names each logical operation with an idempotency key; the server records that key atomically with the effect and replays the stored result for any repeat. Everything else is handling the edges of that one rule.","pitch":"I'd require an Idempotency-Key header on every mutating endpoint, generated by the client once per logical operation and reused on every retry. On the server, the first request inserts the key, scoped to the account and stored with a hash of the request, in the same transaction as the business write; when it finishes we store the response. A retry with a finished key gets that stored response replayed. A retry that arrives while the original is still running gets a 409 with Retry-After instead of running twice. The same key with a different body is a 422, because that's a client bug, not a retry. Keys live longer than the longest client retry window, typically 24 hours. The gateway forwards the key and never mints one, or every gateway retry would look like a new operation.","points":[{"h":"One key per logical operation, owned by the client","d":"Generated before the first attempt (a UUID is fine) and persisted client-side, so a retry after an app restart reuses it. SDK and gateway retries pass the key through. If the gateway generated keys, its own retries would each look new and dedupe would never fire."},{"h":"Scope and fingerprint the key","d":"Make (account_id, key) unique, not the key alone, so tenants can't collide or see each other's responses. Store a hash of method, path and canonical body so a reused key with different parameters is detected and rejected."},{"h":"Claim the key atomically with the effect","d":"Insert the key row with status 'started' in the same database transaction as the business write. The unique constraint makes a concurrent duplicate lose. Either the effect and the key both commit, or neither does, so a crash can't leave a half-applied operation."},{"h":"Replay what executed; don't save what never ran","d":"Once execution has started, store the status code and body (including failures produced during execution) and return them byte-for-byte on a repeat. Requests rejected before execution, such as validation errors or a concurrent conflict, are not saved, so the client can fix the request and retry with the same key."},{"h":"Handle the in-flight duplicate explicitly","d":"If the key exists with status 'started', return 409 Conflict with Retry-After rather than waiting or running again. If a 'started' row is older than a takeover timeout, a later retry may resume it, guarded by a version check so two takeovers can't both proceed."},{"h":"External side effects need recovery points","d":"A card charge or an email can't join your database transaction. Model the request as a state machine (started, charge_requested, charged, completed), commit progress between steps, and pass a key derived from the client's key to the provider so it dedupes too. A retry resumes from the last recovery point."},{"h":"Expire keys deliberately","d":"Keep keys longer than the longest client retry horizon, then purge. After expiry the same key is a new operation; document that so clients don't retry days later and get charged again."}],"decision":"If the effect is a write in one database, claim the key and write in a single transaction: simplest and correct. If the effect is external, use the recovery-point state machine plus a downstream idempotency key. If the operation can be made naturally idempotent (PUT of full state, conditional write on a version, set-based update), do that and skip the key store entirely.","numbers":["Key storage ≈ writes/day × bytes per key × retention days. At 5M writes/day, about 1 KB per key (key, request hash, stored response) and 1 day of retention: about 5 GB, small enough to live in the primary database.","Latency cost of the same-database design is one extra indexed insert inside a transaction you already run. A separate key store adds a network round trip per write and, worse, a window where the key and the effect can disagree."],"pitfalls":["Recording the key after the side effect: a crash in between means the retry applies the effect twice.","Letting the gateway or load balancer generate keys: every retry gets a fresh key and nothing is deduplicated.","Saving a transient failure (timeout, 503 from a dependency) as the final result: that key can now never succeed.","Making keys globally unique instead of per account: one tenant's key can collide with another's or return their response.","Expiry shorter than the client's retry window, for example a mobile app that retries after hours offline.","Keys in a separate store such as a cache, with no transaction spanning it and the write: the double-apply window is back."],"followups":[{"q":"The idempotency store is down. Do you fail open or closed?","a":"If keys live in the same database as the write, the question disappears: no database means no write. With a separate store, fail closed for money movement (return 503 and let the client retry) and fail open only for low-risk writes that are deduplicated downstream. Say which and why."},{"q":"Two requests with the same key arrive 5 ms apart on different servers.","a":"The unique constraint on (account_id, key) decides. One insert wins; the other hits the conflict and returns 409 with Retry-After. No distributed lock is needed when the database enforces uniqueness."},{"q":"How does this extend to a saga across several services?","a":"Derive child keys deterministically from the parent key plus the step name, for example parent:reserve-stock. Every step is idempotent on its own, and replaying the saga hits the same keys."},{"q":"What changes in an active-active, multi-region setup?","a":"Uniqueness has to be enforced in one place per key. Pin each account's writes to a home region, which account-scoped keys make natural, or accept a cross-region dedupe window and run a reconciliation job."}],"example":"A mobile app sends POST /payments with key k1. The server commits the charge, but the response is lost when the phone switches networks. The app retries with k1 a few seconds later; the server finds k1 completed and replays the original 201 with the same payment id. The user is charged once and sees one payment.","related":["replace-two-phase-commit-across","design-outbox-inbox-pattern-reliable","retries-amplifying-outage-design-retries","exactly-once-practice-kafka-flink"],"sources":[{"title":"Stripe API reference: Idempotent requests","url":"https://docs.stripe.com/api/idempotent_requests"},{"title":"Brandur Leach: Implementing Stripe-like idempotency keys in Postgres","url":"https://brandur.org/idempotency-keys"},{"title":"IETF draft: The Idempotency-Key HTTP header field","url":"https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-httpapi-idempotency-key-header/"}]}},{"id":"eventual-consistency-acceptable-payments-flow","cat":"consistency","topic":"Consistency models & CAP","level":"fundamental","source":"both","q":"Where is eventual consistency acceptable in a payments flow, and where is it never acceptable?","also":["When do I accept eventual consistency versus strict consistency?"],"a":["Balances and ledger postings need strong guarantees. Notifications, search views and analytics can lag. State the invariant first, then pick the weakest consistency that protects it.","Strict where a violation causes money, safety or legal harm. Eventual where staleness is invisible or recoverable. Decide from the invariant, not from habit."],"tags":["Consistency","Transactions","Databases"],"legacy":[12,102],"answer":{"thesis":"Start from the invariants, not the components. Anything that can create or lose money (balances, ledger postings, the authorisation decision) needs strong consistency; everything derived from those facts can lag.","pitch":"In a payments flow I'd first write down the invariants: a ledger entry is never lost or duplicated, debits equal credits, and a balance never goes below its limit because of a race. The ledger write and the balance check that guards it must be strongly consistent, usually a single transactional store with the check and the posting in one transaction. The decision returned to the merchant has to reflect that committed state. Everything downstream (notifications, the customer's transaction list, search, analytics, fraud features, the statement) is derived from the ledger and can be eventually consistent, as long as it converges and is idempotent. The rule is: pick the weakest consistency that still protects the invariant, and make the boundary explicit so nobody reads a derived view to make a money decision.","points":[{"h":"Name the invariants first","d":"No double spend, no lost or duplicated postings, debits equal credits, balance never below limit. Each component's consistency need follows from which invariant it protects. Without this list, consistency debates turn into taste."},{"h":"Strong where money is decided","d":"The authorisation check and the ledger posting happen in one transaction against one source of truth, or under a per-account serialisation (single writer per account). This is where linearizable reads or serializable transactions earn their cost."},{"h":"Eventual where state is derived","d":"Notifications, activity feeds, search indexes, analytics and reporting are projections of the ledger. They can lag seconds or minutes as long as they converge and replays don't double-count."},{"h":"The dangerous middle: reads used for decisions","d":"A 'available balance' shown in the app can be stale; a balance used to approve a transfer cannot. Make sure decision paths read from the primary, not from a cache or replica built for display."},{"h":"External systems are always eventually consistent","d":"Card networks, banks and payment providers settle later and sometimes disagree. Model states explicitly (pending, authorised, captured, settled, reversed) and reconcile against their reports daily."},{"h":"Make the boundary visible","d":"Label stores and APIs with their guarantee, for example 'ledger: serializable' and 'activity feed: eventual, under 30 s typical'. Teams then can't accidentally build a money decision on a lagging view."}],"decision":"Strong consistency for anything that changes or authorises money and for reads that feed those decisions. Eventual consistency for projections, notifications and analytics, with idempotent consumers and reconciliation. If you can't say which invariant a component protects, it probably doesn't need strong consistency.","numbers":["Typical acceptable lag for derived views: seconds for a customer's activity list, minutes for analytics, a day for settlement reconciliation. State the number per view so it can be monitored."],"pitfalls":["Approving a payment from a cached or replica balance: a race lets two payments both see enough funds.","Treating the payment provider's webhook as the source of truth without reconciliation; webhooks are delayed, duplicated and occasionally missing.","Making everything strongly consistent 'to be safe': it couples services, hurts availability and doesn't improve correctness for derived data.","Notifications sent before the ledger commit, so customers are told about payments that later roll back."],"followups":[{"q":"Two transfers from the same account arrive at the same moment. How do you prevent overdraft?","a":"Serialise per account: a conditional update (balance >= amount) in one transaction, row locking, or routing all writes for an account to a single writer. The check and the debit must be atomic."},{"q":"The ledger and the notification service disagree. Which wins?","a":"The ledger, always. Notifications are a projection; fix them by replaying from the ledger. That's why the projection must be idempotent."},{"q":"Can the balance shown in the app be stale?","a":"Yes, if it's labelled and never used for decisions. Many products show 'available' and 'pending' separately for exactly this reason."}],"example":"A wallet app debits the ledger in a serializable transaction and returns success. An outbox event then updates the activity feed and sends a push notification within a few seconds. A nightly job reconciles the ledger against the bank's settlement file and flags mismatches for review.","related":["build-idempotency-write-api-clients","model-consistency-levels-across-services","reconcile-divergent-data-across-systems","design-outbox-inbox-pattern-reliable"],"sources":[{"title":"Werner Vogels: Eventually consistent","url":"https://www.allthingsdistributed.com/2008/12/eventually_consistent.html"},{"title":"Jepsen: Consistency models","url":"https://jepsen.io/consistency"}]}},{"id":"give-users-read-writes-reads","cat":"consistency","topic":"Consistency models & CAP","level":"intermediate","source":"both","q":"Give users read-your-writes when reads are served from replicas.","also":["How do I handle read your own writes in a distributed system?"],"a":["Route a user's reads to the primary for a short window, or carry a log position token and wait for replicas to reach it. Compare cost and complexity.","Sticky reads from the primary after a write, session tokens tracking a replica position, or client-side caching of the just-written value."],"tags":["Databases","Consistency"],"legacy":[15,104],"answer":{"thesis":"Read-your-writes is a per-session guarantee, so solve it per session: either route a user's reads to the primary for a short window after they write, or carry a replication position with the session and only read from replicas that have caught up to it.","pitch":"With replicas, a user can write, refresh, and not see their own change, which feels like data loss. I wouldn't make all reads go to the primary; the guarantee only matters for the writer. The simple option is sticky-to-primary: after a write, mark the session and route that user's reads to the primary for a window a bit longer than typical replication lag. The precise option is a position token: the write returns the primary's log position, the client or session carries it, and a read is served only by a replica at or past that position, otherwise it waits briefly or falls back to the primary. For a single page you can also just render the value you wrote. I'd pick sticky-to-primary first and move to tokens if primary read load becomes a problem.","points":[{"h":"Scope the guarantee correctly","d":"Only the session that wrote needs to see the write immediately. Other users can tolerate lag. Making all reads strongly consistent wastes the replicas."},{"h":"Option 1: sticky reads to the primary","d":"After a write, set a session flag or cookie with an expiry (for example 5–10 seconds, above p99 replication lag) and route that session's reads to the primary. Simple and robust; costs extra primary reads proportional to write rate."},{"h":"Option 2: replication position tokens","d":"The write returns a log position (an LSN, GTID or commit timestamp). Reads carry it; the router picks a replica whose applied position is at least that, or waits up to a small timeout, then falls back to the primary."},{"h":"Option 3: client-side echo","d":"The client renders the value it just wrote instead of re-reading it. Works for simple forms; breaks when the server computes derived fields or other devices are involved."},{"h":"Monitor replication lag as a first-class metric","d":"Every option depends on lag staying bounded. Alert on p99 lag, and have the router stop using replicas that fall too far behind."},{"h":"Cross-device needs a server-side session","d":"If the user writes on a phone and reads on a laptop, a cookie on one device doesn't help. Store the last-write position against the user id instead."}],"decision":"Sticky-to-primary when write volume is modest and primary has headroom. Position tokens when primary read capacity is the bottleneck or lag varies widely. Client echo for single-screen flows. Always pair with lag monitoring and replica eviction.","numbers":["Sticky window sizing: set it above p99 replication lag. If p99 lag is 800 ms, a 5 second window is generous and costs one primary read per page view for 5 seconds after each write.","Extra primary load ≈ writes/sec × reads per session in the window. 200 writes/sec × 10 reads in the window = 2,000 extra primary reads/sec."],"pitfalls":["Fixed sticky window shorter than lag spikes: the bug reappears during peak, exactly when it's hardest to debug.","Ignoring cross-device and cross-service reads: a write through service A and a read through service B with its own replica.","Caches in front of replicas: the replica caught up but the cache still holds the old value.","Routing all reads to the primary after an incident 'temporarily' and never undoing it."],"followups":[{"q":"What about monotonic reads, so a user never sees time go backwards?","a":"Pin a session to one replica, or carry the highest position seen and never read from a replica behind it. Position tokens give you both guarantees with one mechanism."},{"q":"A replica falls 30 seconds behind. What happens?","a":"With tokens, reads for recent writers fall back to the primary automatically. The router should also eject the replica from rotation past a lag threshold."},{"q":"How do you do this with a cache in front?","a":"Invalidate or update the cache on write in the same request path, or version cache entries with the position so a stale entry is detectable."}],"example":"A user updates their shipping address and is redirected to the profile page. The write sets a 'recent-write' marker valid for 5 seconds, so the profile read goes to the primary and shows the new address. After 5 seconds the user's reads return to replicas.","related":["model-consistency-levels-across-services","eventual-consistency-acceptable-payments-flow","design-cache-invalidation-rules-not"],"sources":[{"title":"Jepsen: Read your writes","url":"https://jepsen.io/consistency/models/read-your-writes"},{"title":"Martin Kleppmann: Designing Data-Intensive Applications, chapter 5 (Replication)","url":"https://dataintensive.net/"}]}},{"id":"model-consistency-levels-across-services","cat":"consistency","topic":"Consistency models & CAP","level":"intermediate","source":"ref","q":"How do I model consistency levels across services and data stores?","also":[],"a":["Decide per data class: strong for money and inventory, read-your-writes for user-facing state, eventual for derived views. Document the guarantee at each service boundary."],"tags":["Consistency"],"legacy":[64],"answer":{"thesis":"Assign a consistency level to each kind of data, not to the whole system, and publish it as part of every service's contract. Strong for money and inventory, session guarantees for user-facing state, eventual for derived views.","pitch":"I'd classify data rather than services. Money, inventory counts and uniqueness constraints get strong consistency inside one owning service. User-facing state like profiles and settings gets session guarantees, read-your-writes and monotonic reads, which is what users actually notice. Derived data, such as search, feeds, recommendations and analytics, is eventual with a stated lag. Then I'd make the guarantee explicit at every boundary: an API that serves from a cache says so, an event stream states its ordering and delivery guarantees. Across services you generally can't have strong consistency without coupling, so cross-service flows use sagas and reconciliation, and each service owns the strong guarantees for its own data.","points":[{"h":"Classify data by invariant","d":"Money, inventory, uniqueness (usernames, seat bookings): strong. User-visible state: session guarantees. Derived and aggregated data: eventual. The class drives the store and the access path."},{"h":"One owner per piece of strongly consistent data","d":"Strong consistency is cheap inside one database and expensive across many. Put each invariant inside one service's transactional boundary; others ask that service rather than copy the data."},{"h":"Session guarantees are the user-facing sweet spot","d":"Read-your-writes, monotonic reads and consistent prefix cover most 'the app looks broken' complaints without global coordination."},{"h":"State lag and ordering for every projection","d":"A search index or feed should publish its expected lag and whether it preserves per-entity ordering. That makes it monitorable and stops people treating it as a source of truth."},{"h":"Document guarantees in the contract","d":"API docs and event schemas say: source of truth or projection, staleness bound, ordering, delivery semantics (at-least-once, deduplicate by id)."},{"h":"Cross-service invariants use sagas and reconciliation","d":"If an invariant spans services, accept temporary inconsistency, drive convergence with sagas, and detect drift with reconciliation jobs."}],"decision":"Default to eventual plus idempotency across service boundaries. Upgrade to session guarantees where users see their own changes. Reserve strong consistency for invariants that cost money, safety or legal standing, and keep each one inside a single owner.","numbers":["Write the staleness bound as a number per view, for example 'search: p99 under 60 s', so it can be alerted on rather than argued about."],"pitfalls":["One global setting ('we're eventually consistent') that's wrong for half the data.","Copying strongly consistent data into other services and making decisions from the copy.","Undocumented caches in an API path that silently weaken its guarantee.","Distributed transactions across services to get strong consistency that a single owner could provide."],"followups":[{"q":"How do you enforce uniqueness, such as usernames, across regions?","a":"Route the uniqueness check to one authority (a single home region or a consensus-backed store), or partition the namespace so each region owns a slice. Uniqueness can't be eventual."},{"q":"What's the difference between session guarantees and strong consistency for users?","a":"Session guarantees only promise that a user sees their own history consistently; strong consistency promises everyone sees one global order. The first is far cheaper and usually enough."},{"q":"How would you test that the guarantees hold?","a":"Contract tests for the documented bounds, lag metrics with alerts, and fault-injection tests (partitions, replica lag) for the strong paths. Tools in the style of Jepsen exist for the latter."}],"example":"An e-commerce platform: orders and inventory are strongly consistent inside the order service; the product catalogue page reads from a cache with a 5-minute TTL; the customer's order history uses read-your-writes; search and recommendations lag by up to a minute.","related":["eventual-consistency-acceptable-payments-flow","give-users-read-writes-reads","reason-about-cap-trade-offs","replace-two-phase-commit-across"],"sources":[{"title":"Jepsen: Consistency models","url":"https://jepsen.io/consistency"},{"title":"Werner Vogels: Eventually consistent","url":"https://www.allthingsdistributed.com/2008/12/eventually_consistent.html"}]}},{"id":"reason-about-cap-trade-offs","cat":"consistency","topic":"Consistency models & CAP","level":"intermediate","source":"ref","q":"How do I reason about CAP trade-offs for a given product requirement?","also":[],"a":["CAP only matters during a partition. For each operation decide whether it must refuse (consistency) or may proceed on stale or divergent data (availability), and what reconciliation follows."],"tags":["Consistency","Reliability"],"legacy":[67],"answer":{"thesis":"CAP only bites during a network partition, and then it's a per-operation choice: refuse the request to stay consistent, or serve it and accept divergence you'll reconcile later. Most of the time the real trade-off is latency against consistency.","pitch":"I treat CAP as a question I ask per operation: when a partition separates replicas, should this operation fail or proceed? Placing an order against limited inventory should probably fail or queue rather than oversell. Adding an item to a cart or liking a post should proceed and merge later. Outside partitions, which is most of the time, the trade-off is latency against consistency, which is what PACELC adds: synchronous cross-region replication costs a round trip on every write. So for each operation I state what happens on a partition, what the user sees, and how divergent state is reconciled afterwards. 'We chose AP' as a system-wide label is usually too coarse to be useful.","points":[{"h":"CAP is about behaviour during partitions","d":"Partitions will happen, so the real choice is what each operation does when replicas can't talk: refuse (consistent) or proceed (available). It's not a choice between three properties."},{"h":"Decide per operation","d":"Booking the last seat: refuse or queue. Updating a shopping cart: proceed and merge. Reading a product description: serve stale. Different operations in one product make different choices."},{"h":"Most of the time it's latency vs consistency","d":"Without a partition you still pay for consistency: synchronous replication across zones or regions adds round-trip latency to every write. PACELC names this: else, latency or consistency."},{"h":"Available choices need a reconciliation story","d":"If you accept writes on both sides, define how divergence resolves: last-writer-wins (loses data), merge functions or CRDTs, or flagging conflicts for a human."},{"h":"Consistent choices need a user story","d":"If you refuse, decide what the user sees: an error, a retry later, or a queued request with a promise. 'Unavailable' must still be designed."},{"h":"Shrink the blast radius","d":"Partition data so a network split only affects the slice whose owner is unreachable. A home region per account keeps most operations available and consistent."}],"decision":"Refuse during partitions when divergence would violate a money, safety or uniqueness invariant. Proceed when operations commute or merge cleanly (counters, sets, carts). For everything, state the normal-case latency cost of the consistency you pick.","numbers":["Synchronous replication cost: a cross-region round trip is often tens of milliseconds and can exceed 100 ms between distant continents, added to every consistent write. In-zone replication is typically around a millisecond or less."],"pitfalls":["Labelling the whole system 'AP' or 'CP' and ignoring that operations differ.","Choosing availability without designing conflict resolution; last-writer-wins silently drops writes.","Forgetting the normal-case latency cost and blaming the database for slow writes.","Treating CAP's 'consistency' as the C in ACID; CAP's C means linearizability."],"followups":[{"q":"Give an example where you'd choose availability.","a":"A shopping cart: accept adds on both sides of a partition and merge as a union, accepting that a removed item might reappear. Amazon's Dynamo paper describes this trade-off."},{"q":"How do CRDTs help?","a":"They're data types whose concurrent updates merge deterministically (counters, sets, registers with defined rules), so replicas converge without coordination. They suit collaborative and counter-style data, not invariants like 'balance never negative'."},{"q":"What does the user see when you choose consistency during a partition?","a":"Design it: a clear message, a retry with backoff, or accept the request into a durable queue and confirm later. Silent hangs are the worst choice."}],"example":"A ticketing system keeps seat reservation consistent (a partition makes the minority side refuse bookings) but keeps event browsing and wish-lists available from local replicas, merging wish-list changes afterwards.","related":["model-consistency-levels-across-services","fail-over-between-two-regions","raft-paxos-quorums-gossip-appear"],"sources":[{"title":"Eric Brewer: CAP twelve years later","url":"https://www.infoq.com/articles/cap-twelve-years-later-how-the-rules-have-changed/"},{"title":"DeCandia et al.: Dynamo, Amazon's highly available key-value store","url":"https://www.allthingsdistributed.com/files/amazon-dynamo-sosp2007.pdf"}]}},{"id":"replace-two-phase-commit-across","cat":"consistency","topic":"Distributed transactions & reconciliation","level":"advanced","source":"both","q":"Replace a two-phase commit across three services.","also":["How do I implement distributed transactions without 2PC pain?"],"a":["Saga with compensations, transactional outbox, idempotent consumers. Be honest about what you give up: isolation. Show how a half-finished saga is visible and recoverable.","Sagas with compensating actions, outbox for reliable event emission, idempotent steps, and clear handling for stuck or partially complete flows."],"tags":["Transactions","Messaging"],"legacy":[14,103],"answer":{"thesis":"Replace 2PC with a saga: a sequence of local transactions, each publishing its outcome reliably through an outbox, with a compensating action for every step that can be undone. You give up isolation, so design for visible intermediate states.","pitch":"Two-phase commit across three services couples their availability: any participant or the coordinator being slow blocks everyone holding locks. I'd replace it with a saga. Each service does a local transaction and records an event in an outbox table in the same transaction, so state change and event can't diverge. The next step consumes that event idempotently. For each step I define a compensation, like releasing reserved stock or refunding a charge, that runs if a later step fails. I'd use orchestration, with one coordinator holding the saga's state machine, when there are more than two or three steps, because it makes progress and failures visible. The honest cost is isolation: other readers can see the order as 'pending' between steps, so the domain needs explicit pending states, and some steps, like sending an email, can't be compensated and should go last.","points":[{"h":"Why 2PC hurts across services","d":"Participants hold locks while waiting for the coordinator; a coordinator failure can leave them blocked. Every service's availability now depends on all the others, and many brokers and APIs don't support XA anyway."},{"h":"Local transactions plus an outbox","d":"Each step commits its state change and an outgoing event row in one local transaction. A relay publishes the outbox to the broker. This removes the dual-write gap between database and message."},{"h":"Idempotent consumers","d":"Events are delivered at least once, so each step dedupes by event or saga id (an inbox table or idempotency key) before acting."},{"h":"Compensations, not rollbacks","d":"Every step that can be undone gets a semantic inverse: release stock, refund payment, cancel shipment. Compensations must themselves be idempotent and retryable."},{"h":"Order steps by reversibility","d":"Put easily reversed steps first and irreversible ones (shipping, notifying the customer) last, after every step that can fail has succeeded."},{"h":"Orchestration vs choreography","d":"Choreography (services react to each other's events) is fine for two or three steps. Beyond that, an orchestrator with an explicit state machine makes the flow, timeouts and stuck sagas observable."},{"h":"Make stuck sagas visible","d":"Persist saga state with timestamps; alert on sagas stuck in a state too long; provide tooling to retry, compensate or resolve manually."}],"decision":"Use a saga with outbox and idempotent consumers for cross-service workflows. Keep 2PC only inside one database or between components that natively support it and share an availability fate. If an invariant truly needs atomicity across services, reconsider the service boundary instead.","numbers":["Latency: a saga's end-to-end time is the sum of steps plus broker hops, often tens to hundreds of milliseconds, so return to the client after the first step with a pending status rather than blocking on the whole saga."],"pitfalls":["Publishing the event outside the local transaction (dual write): a crash between commit and publish loses the event.","Compensations that aren't idempotent, so a retried compensation refunds twice.","No timeouts: a saga waits forever on a step that will never answer.","Ignoring isolation: another flow reads the half-finished state and acts on it.","Putting the irreversible step in the middle."],"followups":[{"q":"A compensation fails. Now what?","a":"Retry with backoff; compensations must be idempotent. If it keeps failing, park the saga in a 'needs attention' state with full context and alert. Some failures need a human and a runbook."},{"q":"How do you handle the lack of isolation?","a":"Semantic locks (mark the order 'pending' so other flows wait or skip it), commutative updates where possible, and reading only committed final states for decisions."},{"q":"Orchestrator or choreography for this case?","a":"Three services with compensations: orchestrator. It centralises the state machine, timeouts and visibility, while each service keeps its own data and logic."}],"example":"Order placement: the order service creates an order (pending) and an outbox event; inventory reserves stock; payment charges the card; then the order is confirmed and the confirmation email is sent last. If payment fails, the orchestrator triggers 'release stock' and marks the order failed.","related":["design-outbox-inbox-pattern-reliable","build-idempotency-write-api-clients","reconcile-divergent-data-across-systems","one-request-fans-out-five"],"sources":[{"title":"microservices.io: Saga pattern","url":"https://microservices.io/patterns/data/saga.html"},{"title":"microservices.io: Transactional outbox","url":"https://microservices.io/patterns/data/transactional-outbox.html"}]}},{"id":"design-outbox-inbox-pattern-reliable","cat":"consistency","topic":"Distributed transactions & reconciliation","level":"advanced","source":"ref","q":"How do I design an outbox and inbox pattern for reliable events?","also":[],"a":["Write the event in the same transaction as the state change, relay it to the broker, and dedupe on the consumer with an inbox table keyed by event id. Cover relay lag and ordering."],"tags":["Transactions","Messaging"],"legacy":[65],"answer":{"thesis":"The outbox makes 'change state and publish an event' atomic by writing both in one local transaction; the inbox makes 'consume and apply' idempotent by recording processed event ids in the consumer's own transaction. Together they give effectively-once processing over at-least-once delivery.","pitch":"The problem is the dual write: updating the database and publishing to a broker are two systems, so a crash between them loses or invents events. With an outbox, the service writes its state change and an event row into an outbox table in the same transaction. A relay, either a poller or change data capture on the outbox table, publishes those rows to the broker and marks them sent. Delivery is at least once, so on the consumer side an inbox table keyed by event id is written in the same transaction as the consumer's state change; a duplicate hits the unique key and is skipped. I'd care about ordering, since a per-aggregate key and a single relay per partition preserve it, about relay lag as a metric, and about cleaning up old outbox and inbox rows.","points":[{"h":"Outbox: one transaction for state and event","d":"INSERT/UPDATE business rows and INSERT INTO outbox(id, aggregate_id, type, payload, created_at) in one transaction. Either both commit or neither does."},{"h":"Relay: polling or CDC","d":"A poller selects unsent rows in order and publishes them; simple but adds load and latency. CDC tails the database log and streams outbox inserts; lower latency and load, more infrastructure."},{"h":"Inbox: dedupe in the consumer's transaction","d":"The consumer inserts the event id into its inbox table with a unique constraint and applies the change in the same transaction. A redelivered event fails the insert and is acknowledged without reapplying."},{"h":"Preserve per-aggregate ordering","d":"Use the aggregate id as the message key so events for one entity land on one partition, and publish outbox rows for that aggregate in commit order."},{"h":"Monitor relay lag and backlog","d":"Alert on the age of the oldest unsent outbox row. A stuck relay looks like 'events stopped' downstream, which is easy to miss."},{"h":"Retention and cleanup","d":"Delete or archive sent outbox rows after a period, and keep inbox ids at least as long as the broker can redeliver (its retention plus replay window)."}],"decision":"Use the outbox whenever a service must publish events about its own state changes. Choose a poller for modest volume and simplicity; CDC for high volume or low latency. Use an inbox (or a natural idempotency key) on every consumer that changes state.","numbers":["Poller tuning: polling every 100–500 ms adds that much latency on average; batch sizes of hundreds keep database load low. CDC latency is typically lower, bounded by log shipping.","Inbox size ≈ events/day × retention days × ~50–100 bytes per id row. At 10M events/day and 7 days, about 70M rows, which needs a cleanup job and an index on the id."],"pitfalls":["Publishing directly to the broker inside the transaction 'just to be safe': the broker call can succeed while the transaction rolls back.","Multiple pollers without coordination publishing the same rows out of order.","Consumers that dedupe in a cache rather than in their own transaction; the cache and the state change can disagree.","Never cleaning the outbox, so the poller's query slows down over months."],"followups":[{"q":"Why not just make the consumer idempotent and skip the outbox?","a":"Idempotency fixes duplicates, not loss. Without the outbox a crash after commit but before publish loses the event entirely."},{"q":"How do you scale the relay?","a":"Partition the outbox by aggregate key and run one relay per partition, or use CDC, which reads the log sequentially and scales with the broker."},{"q":"What if the consumer's side effect is external, like sending an email?","a":"Record the inbox entry and a 'to send' state in one transaction, then send with a provider idempotency key derived from the event id, and mark it sent."}],"example":"The order service writes order status 'paid' and an OrderPaid outbox row together. Debezium streams the row to Kafka keyed by order id. The fulfilment service inserts the event id into its inbox and creates a shipment in one transaction; a redelivery is skipped.","related":["replace-two-phase-commit-across","exactly-once-practice-kafka-flink","build-idempotency-write-api-clients","keep-ordering-also-need-parallelism"],"sources":[{"title":"microservices.io: Transactional outbox","url":"https://microservices.io/patterns/data/transactional-outbox.html"},{"title":"Debezium: change data capture","url":"https://debezium.io/"}]}},{"id":"reconcile-divergent-data-across-systems","cat":"consistency","topic":"Distributed transactions & reconciliation","level":"intermediate","source":"ref","q":"How do I reconcile divergent data across systems?","also":[],"a":["Pick a source of truth per field, run periodic comparison jobs, classify differences, auto-repair the safe ones, and route the rest for review. Track drift as a metric."],"tags":["Transactions"],"legacy":[66],"answer":{"thesis":"Divergence is inevitable between systems that share facts, so plan for it: pick a source of truth per field, compare systematically, classify differences, auto-repair the safe classes, and route the rest to people. Track drift as a metric, not a surprise.","pitch":"Whenever two systems hold the same fact, such as our ledger and the bank, the order service and the warehouse, or a database and its search index, they will drift because of bugs, missed events and partial failures. I'd first decide, per field, which system is the source of truth. Then run reconciliation jobs that compare the two on a schedule, matching on stable ids, and classify each difference: missing on one side, value mismatch, timing difference that will resolve itself, or genuinely unexplained. Safe classes, like a projection missing a record, are repaired automatically by replaying from the source of truth. Money-affecting or ambiguous ones go to a review queue with context. And I'd publish drift counts as a metric with an alert, because a sudden rise is often the first sign of a broken pipeline.","points":[{"h":"Source of truth per field, not per system","d":"The bank is authoritative for settled amounts; your ledger is authoritative for what you authorised. Ambiguity about authority is the root of most reconciliation pain."},{"h":"Match on stable identifiers","d":"Carry a shared reference (payment id, order id, external transaction id) through every system. Fuzzy matching on amount and date is a last resort and a source of false matches."},{"h":"Classify differences","d":"Missing in target, missing in source, value mismatch, timing (in flight, will settle), duplicate. Each class has a different owner and action."},{"h":"Auto-repair the safe classes","d":"Replay missing events into projections, rebuild a search document from the database. Repairs must be idempotent and logged."},{"h":"Human review for the rest","d":"Money mismatches and unexplained differences go to a queue with both records, history and suggested action. Track time to resolution."},{"h":"Measure drift continuously","d":"Count differences per class per run; alert on spikes. Run incremental checks frequently and full scans less often."}],"decision":"Automate repair where the source of truth is clear and the fix is replayable. Escalate where money or customer impact is involved, or where authority is ambiguous. Run small, frequent incremental reconciliations plus periodic full ones.","numbers":["Cadence example: incremental reconciliation of the last 24 hours every hour, full reconciliation weekly. Use a grace window (for example 2 hours) so in-flight items aren't flagged as missing."],"pitfalls":["No shared id, so matching relies on heuristics and produces false positives.","Auto-repairing in the wrong direction because authority wasn't defined.","Flagging in-flight records as mismatches and drowning reviewers in noise.","Treating reconciliation as a one-off cleanup instead of a permanent control."],"followups":[{"q":"Reconciliation finds 0.1% of payments mismatched with the provider. What do you do?","a":"Classify first: timing, fees, currency rounding, duplicates, missing webhooks. Fix the systematic causes (often a missed webhook path or rounding rule) and send the residue to review."},{"q":"How do you reconcile huge tables cheaply?","a":"Compare checksums of key ranges or time buckets first, and only diff rows inside buckets whose checksums differ (a Merkle-tree style approach)."},{"q":"Who owns reconciliation?","a":"The team owning the source of truth owns the job and the metric; finance or operations often owns the review queue for money cases."}],"example":"A lender reconciles its loan ledger with the payment processor's daily settlement file. 98% match by transaction id; timing differences clear the next day; the remainder, mostly reversals the webhook never delivered, are auto-imported or queued for the finance team.","related":["eventual-consistency-acceptable-payments-flow","design-outbox-inbox-pattern-reliable","replace-two-phase-commit-across"],"sources":[{"title":"Martin Kleppmann: Designing Data-Intensive Applications, chapter 12 (derived data and integrity)","url":"https://dataintensive.net/"}]}},{"id":"generate-unique-ids-across-regions","cat":"consistency","topic":"Consensus & distributed IDs","level":"intermediate","source":"both","q":"Generate unique IDs across regions without a central counter.","also":["How do I design id generation that works across regions?"],"a":["Snowflake-style, ULID, UUIDv7. Clock skew, sortability, index locality, and what breaks if a node reuses a worker id.","Time-ordered unique ids (Snowflake, ULID, UUIDv7), per-node identifiers, clock skew handling, and index-friendly ordering. Avoid a global counter on the hot path."],"tags":["Consistency","Databases"],"legacy":[16,105],"answer":{"thesis":"Generate IDs locally from time plus node identity plus randomness or a sequence, so no request waits on a central counter. Choose the format for the properties you need: sortability, index locality, size, and how much clock skew you can tolerate.","pitch":"A central counter is a single point of failure and a cross-region round trip on every insert. Instead, each node generates IDs locally. The common choices are Snowflake-style 64-bit IDs (timestamp, worker id, per-millisecond sequence), which are compact and time-ordered but need unique worker ids and care with clock skew; ULID or UUIDv7, 128 bits with a timestamp prefix plus randomness, which need no coordination and are roughly time-ordered, so they index well; and random UUIDv4, which needs no coordination but scatters inserts across a B-tree. For most systems today I'd pick UUIDv7: no worker-id management, good index locality, and a standard. I'd choose Snowflake-style when 64 bits matter for storage or a downstream system requires integers.","points":[{"h":"Why not a central counter","d":"Every insert pays a network round trip, and the counter's availability caps the whole system's. Across regions that's tens of milliseconds per id and a cross-region dependency."},{"h":"Snowflake-style 64-bit","d":"Timestamp in milliseconds, a worker id, and a sequence number per millisecond. Compact and sortable. Requires assigning unique worker ids (config, a lease in a coordination service) and handling clocks moving backwards."},{"h":"UUIDv7 and ULID","d":"48-bit millisecond timestamp prefix followed by random bits. No coordination, roughly time-ordered, standard 128-bit format (UUIDv7 is specified in RFC 9562)."},{"h":"Index locality matters","d":"Random UUIDv4 keys insert all over a B-tree, causing page splits and poor cache use at scale. Time-prefixed ids append near the right edge of the index."},{"h":"Clock skew and regression","d":"Time-based ids assume clocks roughly agree. If a clock jumps back, a Snowflake generator must wait or refuse; UUIDv7 implementations typically keep a monotonic counter within a millisecond."},{"h":"Don't leak information unintentionally","d":"Time-ordered ids reveal creation time and, with sequences, volume. For public identifiers that matters; use an opaque external id if needed."}],"decision":"Default: UUIDv7. Snowflake-style when you need 64-bit integers or strict per-node ordering and can manage worker ids. UUIDv4 when ordering is irrelevant and volume is low. Never a global counter on the hot path.","numbers":["Snowflake capacity: with a 12-bit sequence, one worker can mint 4,096 ids per millisecond, about 4 million per second.","UUIDv7 has 74 bits of randomness and version info beyond the 48-bit timestamp; collision risk within one millisecond is negligible at realistic rates."],"pitfalls":["Reusing a worker id after a node is replaced, producing duplicates within the same millisecond.","Assuming time-ordered ids give a global order of events across regions; skew makes them only roughly ordered.","UUIDv4 primary keys on a very large, write-heavy table, then wondering why insert performance degrades.","Exposing sequential ids publicly, making enumeration and volume estimation trivial."],"followups":[{"q":"How do you assign Snowflake worker ids safely?","a":"Lease them from a coordination service with a TTL, or derive them from stable infrastructure identity, and refuse to start if the lease can't be acquired."},{"q":"Can you sort events across regions by these ids?","a":"Only approximately. If you need a true order, use a single sequencer per partition or a logical clock; physical time can't give a total order under skew."},{"q":"What if the database wants integer keys?","a":"Snowflake-style 64-bit ids fit a bigint, or keep an internal bigint surrogate and expose a UUID externally."}],"example":"A multi-region order system switches from a central sequence service to UUIDv7 generated in each service. Insert latency drops by the cross-region round trip, the sequence service disappears as a dependency, and index locality stays good because ids are time-prefixed.","related":["raft-paxos-quorums-gossip-appear","pick-partition-key-multi-tenant"],"sources":[{"title":"RFC 9562: Universally Unique IDentifiers (UUIDs), including UUIDv7","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562"},{"title":"ULID specification","url":"https://github.com/ulid/spec"}]}},{"id":"raft-paxos-quorums-gossip-appear","cat":"consistency","topic":"Consensus & distributed IDs","level":"intermediate","source":"both","q":"Where do Raft, Paxos, quorums and gossip appear in a system you would really build?","also":["How do Raft, Paxos, gossip, and quorum based replication actually fit into my design?"],"a":["Coordination and metadata on a consensus log, tunable quorums for data, gossip for membership and failure detection. Show that you use them, not reimplement them.","Explain the guarantee each one provides, what it costs in latency and availability, and where it sits in the design (coordination service, replication layer, membership). Name a real system for each.","Consensus for leader election and metadata, quorum reads and writes for tunable consistency, gossip for membership and failure detection. Use proven implementations."],"tags":["Consistency","Reliability"],"legacy":[17,107,106],"answer":{"thesis":"You use these protocols, you don't implement them. Consensus (Raft, Paxos) sits under coordination and metadata, such as leader election, configuration and locks. Quorum replication sits in data stores to tune consistency against latency. Gossip spreads membership and failure detection where eventual agreement is enough.","pitch":"In a system I'd actually build, consensus shows up inside the coordination layer: etcd or ZooKeeper for leader election, configuration and service discovery, or inside a database that uses Raft per data range, like CockroachDB or TiKV. It gives a linearizable log with a majority, so it survives a minority of failures but needs a majority to make progress, and every write costs a round trip to a majority. Quorum replication, where W plus R exceeds N, appears in Dynamo-style stores like Cassandra, letting you trade consistency for latency per operation. Gossip appears in membership and failure detection, for example in Cassandra or Consul, where nodes converge on who is alive without a central authority. My point in an interview is placement: consensus for small, critical metadata; quorums or single-leader replication for bulk data; gossip for membership.","points":[{"h":"Consensus gives a single agreed log","d":"Raft and Multi-Paxos elect a leader and commit entries once a majority acknowledges. That yields linearizable reads and writes for small, critical state."},{"h":"Where consensus belongs","d":"Leader election, distributed locks and leases (with fencing tokens), cluster configuration, schema and shard maps. Typically through etcd, ZooKeeper or Consul, or inside a database's replication layer."},{"h":"Cost of consensus","d":"Every write needs a majority round trip; across regions that's slow. A cluster of 2f+1 nodes tolerates f failures and stops accepting writes without a majority."},{"h":"Quorum replication for data","d":"Dynamo-style stores write to W replicas and read from R of N. W + R > N makes read and write sets overlap. Lower W or R trades consistency for latency per operation."},{"h":"Gossip for membership","d":"Nodes periodically exchange state with random peers, so information spreads in roughly logarithmic rounds. Good for liveness and metadata where eventual agreement is fine; not for decisions needing a single answer."},{"h":"Leases need fencing","d":"A node that believes it holds a lock can pause (GC, network) and act after the lease expired. Attach a monotonically increasing fencing token that the storage layer checks."}],"decision":"Use a consensus-backed service for anything that must have exactly one answer (who is leader, who holds the lock, what the config is). Use single-leader or quorum replication for bulk data depending on your consistency needs. Use gossip only for membership and soft state. Never write your own consensus implementation for production.","numbers":["Fault tolerance: 3 nodes tolerate 1 failure, 5 tolerate 2. Going from 3 to 5 adds resilience but also adds write latency because the commit needs 3 acknowledgements instead of 2.","Quorum example: N = 3, W = 2, R = 2 gives overlapping quorums; W = 1, R = 1 is fast but can read stale data."],"pitfalls":["Putting bulk or high-volume data into the coordination store; etcd and ZooKeeper are built for small metadata.","Using a lock without fencing tokens and getting two writers after a pause.","Running an even number of consensus nodes, which adds cost without adding fault tolerance.","Assuming W + R > N alone gives linearizability; sloppy quorums, hinted handoff and concurrent writes can still produce anomalies."],"followups":[{"q":"Why not run consensus across three regions for all writes?","a":"Every write would wait for a cross-region majority, adding tens to hundreds of milliseconds. Reserve it for metadata or data that truly needs global linearizability."},{"q":"What happens to a Raft cluster during a network partition?","a":"The side with a majority keeps a leader and accepts writes; the minority side can't commit and its old leader steps down when it can't reach a quorum."},{"q":"How does gossip detect failures?","a":"Nodes exchange heartbeats or use indirect probing (as in SWIM); a node is suspected after missed responses and declared dead after a timeout, trading detection speed for false positives."}],"example":"A job platform uses etcd leases to elect one scheduler leader per shard, with the lease revision as a fencing token on job claims. Job data lives in Postgres with a single primary, and worker membership spreads by gossip so workers find each other without a central registry.","related":["reason-about-cap-trade-offs","generate-unique-ids-across-regions","fail-over-between-two-regions","design-scheduler-millions-delayed-jobs"],"sources":[{"title":"The Raft consensus algorithm","url":"https://raft.github.io/"},{"title":"Martin Kleppmann: How to do distributed locking (fencing tokens)","url":"https://martin.kleppmann.com/2016/02/08/how-to-do-distributed-locking.html"}]}},{"id":"list-ways-three-tier-service","cat":"reliability","topic":"Failure modes & degradation","level":"fundamental","source":"both","q":"List the ways a three-tier service fails under real load before drawing any boxes.","also":["What are all the ways my system can fail under real traffic?"],"a":["Slow dependencies, retries, queue growth, GC pauses, connection pool exhaustion, bad deploys, clock drift, partial network partitions. Slow is worse than down.","Overload, slow or failing dependencies, resource exhaustion, bad deploys, data corruption, network partitions, cascading retries, and operator error. Group by blast radius."],"tags":["Reliability","Observability"],"legacy":[18,108],"answer":{"thesis":"Before drawing boxes, list how it breaks, grouped by blast radius. Under real load the dangerous failures are rarely clean crashes; they're slowness, saturation and feedback loops, because slow is worse than down.","pitch":"For a load balancer, app tier and database, I'd list failures in three groups. Resource exhaustion: connection pools running dry, thread pools saturated, memory and GC pauses, disk filling up, file descriptors. Dependency problems: the database slowing under a bad query plan or lock contention, a downstream API timing out, DNS or certificate expiry. Feedback loops: retries multiplying load on a struggling dependency, queues growing without bound, cache expiry causing a stampede, autoscaling reacting too late or thrashing. Then the human and change causes: bad deploys, config pushes, schema migrations, which cause a large share of real incidents. The key insight is that a slow database is worse than a dead one: requests pile up holding connections and threads, and the failure spreads upward. So the design needs timeouts, bounded queues, load shedding and safe deploys before it needs more boxes.","points":[{"h":"Saturation and exhaustion","d":"Connection pools, thread pools, CPU, memory and GC, disk space, ephemeral ports, file descriptors. Each has a hard limit, and hitting it usually looks like latency first, then errors."},{"h":"Slow dependencies are worse than dead ones","d":"A dead dependency fails fast; a slow one ties up callers' threads and connections until they also stall. Without timeouts, one slow query can take down the whole app tier."},{"h":"Feedback loops amplify small problems","d":"Retries multiply load, queues grow until latency is unbounded, cache expiry sends a herd to the database, and autoscaling adds instances that all open new connections to an already struggling database."},{"h":"Change is the biggest single cause","d":"Deploys, config changes, feature flags and migrations trigger many incidents. Progressive rollout and fast rollback matter as much as redundancy."},{"h":"Partial and gray failures","d":"One instance with a bad disk, one zone with packet loss, a load balancer health check that passes while real requests fail. Averages hide these; per-instance metrics find them."},{"h":"Time and expiry bombs","d":"Expiring TLS certificates, clock drift breaking token validation, leap-second and timezone bugs, credentials rotating without the app reloading them."},{"h":"Group by blast radius","d":"Single request, single instance, one zone, whole service, all regions. The design response differs: retry, eject, fail over, shed load, or freeze changes."}],"decision":"Address failures in this order: change safety (canary, rollback), timeouts and bounded queues everywhere, load shedding and retry budgets, then redundancy. Redundancy without the first three just gives you more instances to fail identically.","numbers":["Little's law: concurrent requests = arrival rate × latency. At 1,000 requests/s and 50 ms latency you hold 50 in flight; if the database slows to 2 s, you need 2,000 concurrent slots. Pools of 200 exhaust immediately, which is why slowness cascades."],"pitfalls":["Designing only for crash failures and ignoring slowness.","Health checks that test 'process is up' rather than 'can serve a real request'.","Retries at every layer, multiplying load during exactly the moments you can least afford it.","Unbounded queues that convert overload into ever-growing latency instead of fast, honest rejection."],"followups":[{"q":"Which failure would you design for first?","a":"Slow database. It's common and it cascades. Timeouts, bounded pools, a circuit breaker and load shedding contain it."},{"q":"How do you find gray failures?","a":"Per-instance and per-zone metrics, outlier detection in the load balancer, and synthetic requests that exercise real paths rather than a health endpoint."},{"q":"How do you stop autoscaling from making it worse?","a":"Cap connections per instance and total, scale on saturation signals rather than latency alone, and make sure the database isn't the bottleneck new instances pile onto."}],"example":"A single missing index makes a popular query take 3 seconds under load. App threads block on database connections, the pool empties, health checks start failing, the load balancer removes instances, and the remaining ones fail faster. A 300 ms query timeout and load shedding would have kept the rest of the site up.","related":["retries-amplifying-outage-design-retries","choose-size-circuit-breakers-bulkheads","design-admission-control-service-stays","design-graceful-degradation-instead-hard"],"sources":[{"title":"Google SRE book: Addressing cascading failures","url":"https://sre.google/sre-book/addressing-cascading-failures/"},{"title":"AWS Builders' Library: Avoiding insurmountable queue backlogs","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/builders-library/avoiding-insurmountable-queue-backlogs/"}]}},{"id":"design-graceful-degradation-instead-hard","cat":"reliability","topic":"Failure modes & degradation","level":"fundamental","source":"ref","q":"How do I design for graceful degradation instead of hard failure?","also":[],"a":["Rank features by importance, define a fallback for each dependency (cached, default, hidden), and test the degraded modes. Decide in advance what the user sees."],"tags":["Reliability"],"legacy":[68],"answer":{"thesis":"Rank features by importance, decide in advance what each one does when its dependency fails, and test those degraded modes. Graceful degradation is a product decision implemented in code, not an emergent property.","pitch":"I'd start with a ranked list: what must work, like checkout and login, what's valuable but optional, like recommendations and reviews, and what's decoration. For every dependency of the critical path, define a fallback: a cached or default value, hiding the feature, a simplified version, or queueing the action to complete later. The core path must not depend on optional features at all, so recommendations timing out never blocks checkout. Implement it with tight timeouts on optional calls, circuit breakers, and feature flags that operators can flip to shed optional work under load. Then test the degraded modes deliberately, because a fallback that's never run tends to be broken when you need it.","points":[{"h":"Rank features explicitly","d":"Critical, important, optional. Do it with product owners, because the ranking is a business decision. It decides what gets shed first under load."},{"h":"Define a fallback per dependency","d":"Serve stale from cache, return a sensible default, hide the widget, show a simplified page, or accept the request and process it later. Write down what the user sees."},{"h":"Decouple the critical path","d":"Critical requests should never wait on optional calls. Fetch optional data in parallel with short timeouts, or load it client-side after the main content."},{"h":"Operator controls","d":"Kill switches and load-shedding flags let on-call turn off expensive optional features within seconds during an incident."},{"h":"Static stability","d":"Prefer designs that keep working with the last known good state when control planes or config services fail, instead of fallbacks that kick in only during failure."},{"h":"Test degraded modes","d":"Fault-inject dependency failures in staging and in game days. Measure that the critical path's latency and success rate stay within SLO."}],"decision":"Degrade optional features first, then simplify important ones, and protect critical paths at all costs. Prefer fallbacks that are exercised regularly (cached data served all the time with refresh) over rarely used emergency code paths, which tend to be broken.","numbers":["Timeout budget example: page budget 500 ms; recommendations get 80 ms and are dropped if late; the critical product and price data get the rest."],"pitfalls":["Fallbacks that call another dependency which fails the same way.","Degraded modes never tested, discovered broken during the incident.","Silent degradation with no metric, so the team doesn't notice a feature has been off for weeks.","Fallback paths that are more expensive than the normal path, adding load during overload."],"followups":[{"q":"Isn't a fallback just more code that can fail?","a":"Yes, which is why the best fallbacks are always-on (serve from cache, refresh in background) or very simple (hide the feature). AWS's Builders' Library argues against complex rarely used fallbacks for this reason."},{"q":"How does the user know something is degraded?","a":"Only when it matters to them: 'prices may be a few minutes old' or 'we'll email your receipt shortly'. Hiding a recommendation widget needs no message."},{"q":"Who decides the ranking?","a":"Product and engineering together, written down before incidents, and revisited when features change."}],"example":"During a review-service outage, product pages hide the reviews section and show a cached average rating. Checkout never calls the review service. A dashboard counter shows how many pages were served in degraded mode.","related":["list-ways-three-tier-service","choose-size-circuit-breakers-bulkheads","one-request-fans-out-five","design-admission-control-service-stays"],"sources":[{"title":"AWS Builders' Library: Avoiding fallback in distributed systems","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/builders-library/avoiding-fallback-in-distributed-systems/"},{"title":"AWS Builders' Library: Static stability using Availability Zones","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/builders-library/static-stability-using-availability-zones/"}]}},{"id":"retries-amplifying-outage-design-retries","cat":"reliability","topic":"Retries, breakers & bulkheads","level":"intermediate","source":"both","q":"Retries are amplifying an outage. Design retries that do not.","also":["How do I implement retries without causing storms and loops?"],"a":["Retry budgets, exponential backoff with jitter, deadline propagation, retry at one layer only, idempotent operations only, and a kill switch.","Capped attempts, exponential backoff with jitter, retry budgets, deadline propagation, retries at a single layer, and only for idempotent operations."],"tags":["Reliability"],"legacy":[19,109],"answer":{"thesis":"Retries help with transient blips and hurt in overload. Make them safe by retrying at one layer only, with capped exponential backoff and jitter, a retry budget that limits retries to a small fraction of traffic, deadlines propagated end to end, and only for idempotent operations.","pitch":"Retries amplify outages because they multiply load exactly when a dependency is struggling. If five layers each retry three times, one user request can become 243 calls at the bottom. I'd fix it in layers. Retry at one place only, usually the edge closest to the failing call, and have everyone else fail fast. Use capped exponential backoff with jitter so retries spread out instead of arriving in synchronised waves. Add a retry budget, for example retries may be at most 10% of requests, so retries stop automatically when failures are widespread. Propagate a deadline so nobody retries work whose caller has already given up. Only retry idempotent operations or those with idempotency keys, and honour Retry-After and 429 or 503 signals. Finally, a kill switch to disable retries during an incident.","points":[{"h":"Understand the amplification","d":"Retries per layer multiply: 3 attempts at each of 5 layers is 3^5 = 243 attempts at the bottom for one request. Under overload that turns a slowdown into a collapse."},{"h":"Retry at one layer","d":"Pick the layer closest to the failing dependency, or the client, and make intermediate layers pass errors through. Document the choice so new code doesn't add retries silently."},{"h":"Capped exponential backoff with jitter","d":"Wait base × 2^attempt up to a cap, with randomisation (full jitter: a random value between 0 and that bound). Jitter prevents synchronised retry storms."},{"h":"Retry budgets","d":"Track the ratio of retries to requests per client; stop retrying when it exceeds a threshold such as 10%. When most calls fail, retrying adds load without adding success."},{"h":"Deadlines, not just timeouts","d":"Pass the remaining time budget downstream. A service shouldn't start or retry work if the caller's deadline has passed."},{"h":"Only safe operations","d":"Retry reads and idempotent writes; for other writes use idempotency keys. Don't retry errors that won't change, such as 400 or 403."},{"h":"Respect server signals and keep a kill switch","d":"Honour 429/503 with Retry-After. Give operators a flag to turn retries off or down during incidents."}],"decision":"Few attempts (2–3 total), one layer, jittered backoff, a budget, and deadline propagation. Prefer hedged requests only for idempotent reads with tight tail-latency needs, and only with a budget.","numbers":["Backoff example: base 100 ms, cap 2 s, full jitter, 3 attempts: waits are random in [0, 100 ms], then [0, 200 ms].","Budget example: allow retries up to 10% of request volume per client; above that, fail immediately."],"pitfalls":["Library defaults that retry silently at every layer.","Backoff without jitter: all clients retry on the same schedule and hammer the server in waves.","Retrying non-idempotent writes, causing duplicates.","Retrying after the caller's deadline has passed, doing work nobody will use."],"followups":[{"q":"Should the client or the server-side caller retry?","a":"Usually the layer closest to the failing dependency, because it knows the error type and has the shortest loop. The client retries only when the whole request failed and is idempotent."},{"q":"How do circuit breakers relate to retry budgets?","a":"Both stop hammering a failing dependency. A budget limits extra load from retries; a breaker stops calls entirely when the error rate is high. Use both."},{"q":"When are hedged requests appropriate?","a":"For idempotent reads where tail latency matters: send a second request after the p95 latency and take the first response, with a cap on the extra load."}],"example":"A payment service's database slowed; three layers each retried three times, so database load grew 27x and it fell over. The fix: retries only in the client SDK with jitter and a 10% budget, deadline headers between services, and fail-fast elsewhere. The next slowdown stayed a slowdown.","related":["choose-size-circuit-breakers-bulkheads","build-idempotency-write-api-clients","design-admission-control-service-stays","list-ways-three-tier-service"],"sources":[{"title":"AWS Builders' Library: Timeouts, retries and backoff with jitter","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/builders-library/timeouts-retries-and-backoff-with-jitter/"},{"title":"Google SRE book: Addressing cascading failures","url":"https://sre.google/sre-book/addressing-cascading-failures/"}]}},{"id":"choose-size-circuit-breakers-bulkheads","cat":"reliability","topic":"Retries, breakers & bulkheads","level":"intermediate","source":"both","q":"Choose and size circuit breakers, bulkheads and timeouts.","also":["How do I design circuit breakers and bulkheads correctly?"],"a":["Timeout below the caller's deadline, bulkhead per dependency, breaker thresholds from observed error rate. Cover half-open probing and fallback behavior.","Breaker opens on error or latency thresholds and probes in half-open state. Bulkheads isolate thread or connection pools per dependency. Tune from measured behavior and test them."],"tags":["Reliability"],"legacy":[20,110],"answer":{"thesis":"Timeouts bound how long you wait, bulkheads bound how much of your capacity one dependency can consume, and circuit breakers stop calling a dependency that's clearly failing. Size each from measured behaviour, not defaults.","pitch":"I'd set timeouts first, because without them nothing else works. Each call's timeout sits a little above that dependency's healthy p99 and always below the caller's own remaining deadline. Then bulkheads: a separate connection or concurrency pool per dependency, sized by Little's law from its normal throughput and latency plus headroom, so a slow recommendation service can only exhaust its own slots, not the threads checkout needs. Then circuit breakers: open when the error or slow-call rate over a rolling window crosses a threshold with a minimum request count, fail fast while open, and after a cool-down let a few probe requests through in half-open state. When open, return the fallback. I'd validate the numbers with load tests and fault injection.","points":[{"h":"Timeouts from data","d":"Set each call's timeout slightly above the dependency's healthy p99 (for example p99 × 1.5), and never longer than the caller's remaining deadline. Defaults like 30 or 60 seconds are almost always wrong."},{"h":"Bulkheads per dependency","d":"Separate pools (threads, connections, semaphores) so one slow dependency can't consume shared capacity. Critical dependencies get reserved capacity."},{"h":"Size pools with Little's law","d":"Needed concurrency = throughput × latency. 500 calls/s at 40 ms is 20 concurrent; add headroom for spikes (say 2x) and cap there. A larger pool only queues more work behind a slow dependency."},{"h":"Breaker trip conditions","d":"Open when the failure rate or slow-call rate over a rolling window exceeds a threshold (for example 50%), with a minimum number of calls so a handful of errors at low traffic doesn't trip it."},{"h":"Half-open probing","d":"After a cool-down, allow a few trial calls; close on success, reopen on failure. Add jitter so many instances don't probe at the same instant."},{"h":"Fallback while open","d":"Fail fast with a clear error, serve cached data, or degrade the feature. An open breaker without a fallback is still better than piling up timeouts."}],"decision":"Every remote call gets a timeout. Dependencies that are optional or historically flaky get a bulkhead and a breaker. Critical dependencies get reserved capacity and tight timeouts. Tune from production metrics and verify with fault injection.","numbers":["Example: dependency p99 = 120 ms healthy → timeout 200 ms. Throughput 300/s × 0.12 s = 36 concurrent → bulkhead of about 70.","Breaker example: rolling window of 20 seconds, minimum 50 calls, open at 50% failures or slow calls, 10-second cool-down, 5 probe calls in half-open."],"pitfalls":["Timeouts longer than the caller's deadline, so work continues after nobody is waiting.","One shared thread pool for all dependencies, which defeats bulkheads.","Breakers with no minimum call count, flapping on low traffic.","Per-instance breakers that each need to see failures before opening; at large fleet sizes that's a lot of failed calls. Consider shared signals or outlier detection at the load balancer."],"followups":[{"q":"How do you pick the breaker threshold?","a":"From the dependency's normal error rate: well above normal noise but low enough to trip before callers saturate. Validate by injecting failures and watching caller saturation."},{"q":"Where should breakers live: client library, sidecar or gateway?","a":"Closest to the caller that knows the fallback, often the client library. A service mesh sidecar can provide timeouts and outlier ejection uniformly but doesn't know business fallbacks."},{"q":"What do you do about retries with breakers?","a":"Retries count toward the breaker's window, and an open breaker should stop retries entirely."}],"example":"A product page calls pricing (critical) and recommendations (optional). Recommendations get a 20-slot bulkhead, an 80 ms timeout and a breaker; when it degrades, the breaker opens within seconds and the page renders without recommendations while pricing keeps its reserved pool.","related":["retries-amplifying-outage-design-retries","design-graceful-degradation-instead-hard","one-request-fans-out-five","list-ways-three-tier-service"],"sources":[{"title":"Martin Fowler: Circuit breaker","url":"https://martinfowler.com/bliki/CircuitBreaker.html"},{"title":"AWS Builders' Library: Timeouts, retries and backoff with jitter","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/builders-library/timeouts-retries-and-backoff-with-jitter/"}]}},{"id":"fail-over-between-two-regions","cat":"reliability","topic":"Multi-region & outages","level":"advanced","source":"both","q":"Fail over between two regions without split brain.","also":["How do I run in multiple regions and fail over cleanly?"],"a":["Single writer per partition, fencing tokens, health-based traffic shifting, data loss window (RPO) and recovery time (RTO) stated up front. Practice the failover, do not just draw it.","Active-active or active-passive, a single writer per data partition, replication lag and loss window stated, health-driven DNS or traffic management, and regular failover drills."],"tags":["Reliability","Consistency"],"legacy":[21,111],"answer":{"thesis":"Split brain happens when both regions believe they own the same data. Prevent it structurally: one writer per data partition at any time, ownership decided by a quorum or an explicit operator action, and fencing tokens so a deposed writer's late writes are rejected. State the data-loss window (RPO) and recovery time (RTO) up front.","pitch":"With two regions, the hard part isn't moving traffic, it's making sure only one region accepts writes for a given piece of data. I'd assign each data partition a single writer region. Failover changes that ownership through something that can't itself split: either a consensus service with a tiebreaker in a third location, or a deliberate operator decision, because two regions alone can't distinguish 'the other is dead' from 'the link is down'. Every ownership change increments an epoch, and storage rejects writes with an old epoch, which is fencing. With asynchronous replication, failover loses whatever hadn't replicated, so the RPO is roughly the replication lag; synchronous replication removes that loss but adds cross-region latency to every write. Then traffic shifting, health checks and, most importantly, regular failover drills.","points":[{"h":"Single writer per partition","d":"At any moment exactly one region may write a given partition. Reads can be served anywhere with known staleness. This is the structural defence against split brain."},{"h":"Two regions can't vote alone","d":"If the link drops, each side sees the other as dead. Use a witness in a third location for quorum, or make failover a manual, deliberate action with a runbook."},{"h":"Fencing with epochs","d":"Each ownership change increments an epoch or term. Writes carry it, and the storage layer rejects writes from older epochs, so a region that comes back still thinking it's primary can't corrupt data."},{"h":"State RPO and RTO","d":"Async replication: RPO roughly equals replication lag at failure time. Sync: RPO near zero, but every write pays a cross-region round trip. RTO is detection plus decision plus promotion plus DNS or traffic shift time."},{"h":"Traffic shifting","d":"Health-checked global load balancing or DNS with low TTLs, plus clients that respect it. Account for long-lived connections and DNS caching beyond the TTL."},{"h":"Capacity in the surviving region","d":"The survivor must handle the full load. Either keep it provisioned (active-active at under 50% each) or have reliable fast scaling and test it."},{"h":"Practise failover","d":"Run scheduled drills. Untested failover is a theory; the first real use usually uncovers stale runbooks, missing permissions and hidden dependencies on the failed region."}],"decision":"Active-passive with async replication and manual or witness-based failover is the common, defensible default. Active-active needs partitioned ownership (home region per account) or conflict-free data types. Synchronous cross-region replication only where zero data loss justifies the write latency.","numbers":["RPO example: async replication lag of 2 seconds at 1,000 writes/s means up to about 2,000 writes at risk on failover; they must be reconciled or are lost.","RTO budget example: 1 minute detection, 5 minutes decision, 2 minutes promotion, 5 minutes for traffic to move = about 13 minutes."],"pitfalls":["Automatic failover based on one region's view of the other's health; flaky links cause flapping or split brain.","No fencing, so the old primary keeps accepting writes after it's been replaced.","Hidden dependencies on the failed region: auth service, secrets store, container registry, DNS control plane.","Never failing back: the procedure to return to the original region is untested too."],"followups":[{"q":"What happens to the writes that didn't replicate?","a":"With async replication, they exist only in the failed region. When it comes back, they must be reconciled: replayed if safe, flagged for review if they conflict. Decide this before the incident."},{"q":"Why not active-active everywhere?","a":"Concurrent writes to the same data in two regions need conflict resolution, which is wrong for invariants like balances. Active-active works when each account or partition has one home region."},{"q":"How often would you drill?","a":"Quarterly at minimum for full failover; smaller component failovers more often. Measure actual RTO and RPO each time."}],"example":"A lending platform keeps each customer's data homed in one region with async replication to the other. A regional outage triggers an operator-approved failover: the coordination service bumps the epoch, the standby database is promoted, and global load balancing moves traffic. Measured RTO was 11 minutes, with about 3 seconds of writes to reconcile.","related":["design-data-center-outage-scenarios","raft-paxos-quorums-gossip-appear","reason-about-cap-trade-offs","rehearse-failure-before-production"],"sources":[{"title":"Martin Kleppmann: How to do distributed locking (fencing tokens)","url":"https://martin.kleppmann.com/2016/02/08/how-to-do-distributed-locking.html"},{"title":"AWS Well-Architected: Disaster recovery objectives (RTO and RPO)","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/reliability-pillar/disaster-recovery-dr-objectives.html"}]}},{"id":"design-data-center-outage-scenarios","cat":"reliability","topic":"Multi-region & outages","level":"advanced","source":"ref","q":"How do I design for data center outage scenarios?","also":[],"a":["Spread across zones first, then regions. Know your capacity after losing one site, replicate data with a stated loss window, and keep traffic shifting and runbooks tested."],"tags":["Reliability"],"legacy":[69],"answer":{"thesis":"Design for the loss of a whole site by spreading across failure domains (zones first, then regions), keeping enough capacity to survive the loss without scaling up, replicating data with a stated loss window, and proving it with regular drills.","pitch":"I'd treat it as a sequence of failure domains: instance, zone, region. Zone loss is common enough that the system should survive it with no human action: run in at least three zones, keep enough capacity that losing one leaves the rest under their limits, replicate data synchronously within the region, and avoid depending on control planes during the failure, which AWS calls static stability. Region loss is rarer and more expensive to handle; there I'd choose a strategy per service tier, from backup-and-restore for internal tools to warm standby or active-active for revenue-critical paths, each with a stated RPO and RTO. Then I'd look hard for hidden single points of failure, like DNS, identity, secrets, CI/CD and third-party SaaS, and run drills to measure real recovery times.","points":[{"h":"Failure domains in layers","d":"Instance, rack, zone, region, provider. Each layer is rarer and costlier to survive. Decide explicitly which layer each service must survive automatically."},{"h":"N+1 capacity per zone","d":"With three zones, each should run at no more than about two thirds of its capacity so losing one leaves the other two able to absorb the load without new instances."},{"h":"Static stability","d":"Keep the system working without control-plane actions during the failure: pre-provisioned capacity, cached configuration, no dependency on launching instances or changing DNS to survive a zone loss."},{"h":"Data replication with stated loss","d":"Synchronous replication across zones (low latency, near-zero loss). Asynchronous across regions, with RPO equal to lag. Backups for the case where replication copies corruption too."},{"h":"Tiered regional strategies","d":"Backup and restore (hours), pilot light (core data replicated, compute off), warm standby (scaled-down copy running), active-active (full capacity in both). Match each service's tier to its business impact."},{"h":"Hunt hidden dependencies","d":"Authentication, secrets, DNS, container registry, CI/CD, monitoring and third-party APIs often live in one region. If they're down, your failover may be impossible."},{"h":"Drill and measure","d":"Simulate zone loss regularly (block traffic to one zone), and region loss on a schedule. Track measured RTO and RPO against targets."}],"decision":"Every production service survives a zone loss automatically. Regional resilience is tiered by business impact, because full active-active everywhere is expensive. Backups and restore tests are mandatory regardless, since replication faithfully copies mistakes.","numbers":["Capacity rule: with N zones, run each at most (N − 1)/N of its capacity: 66% for three zones, 50% for two.","Cost intuition: warm standby for a region roughly adds a fraction of production cost; active-active roughly doubles infrastructure."],"pitfalls":["Running at 80% in each of three zones and discovering a zone loss overloads the rest.","Failover that requires launching instances during a regional event, when the control plane is also struggling.","Monitoring and alerting hosted in the region that failed.","Treating replication as backup; a bad migration or deletion replicates instantly."],"followups":[{"q":"How do you test zone loss without causing an outage?","a":"Start in staging, then shift traffic away from one zone in production during low-traffic hours with a quick rollback, and expand the scope as confidence grows."},{"q":"Which services deserve active-active?","a":"Those where minutes of downtime cost more than doubling infrastructure: usually the core transaction path, not internal tools or batch analytics."},{"q":"What's the most common surprise in regional drills?","a":"Hidden dependencies: an auth or secrets service that only runs in the primary region, or deploy tooling that can't reach the standby."}],"example":"A payments service runs in three zones at 60% utilisation with synchronous database replicas across zones. A zone outage removed a third of instances; the rest absorbed load with p99 rising from 120 to 180 ms and no errors, and no human action was needed.","related":["fail-over-between-two-regions","make-rebuild-scratch-possible-if","rehearse-failure-before-production","list-ways-three-tier-service"],"sources":[{"title":"AWS Builders' Library: Static stability using Availability Zones","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/builders-library/static-stability-using-availability-zones/"},{"title":"AWS Well-Architected: Disaster recovery objectives","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/reliability-pillar/disaster-recovery-dr-objectives.html"}]}},{"id":"plan-rollback-release-has-already","cat":"reliability","topic":"Releases & rollback","level":"advanced","source":"both","q":"Plan a rollback for a release that has already migrated data.","also":["How do I design a rollback strategy for bad releases?"],"a":["Backward compatible schema, feature flags, forward-fix versus rollback decision rule, and data written by the new code that the old code must still read.","Canary and progressive rollout, automatic rollback on SLO regression, backward-compatible schemas, feature flags, and a clear rule for rolling back versus fixing forward."],"tags":["Migration","Reliability"],"legacy":[23,112],"answer":{"thesis":"Once new code has written data, rolling back the code isn't enough: the old version must still read what the new version wrote. Design releases so the schema and data formats are backward and forward compatible across adjacent versions, and decide in advance when you roll back versus fix forward.","pitch":"The rule I'd follow is that any release must be safe to run alongside, and roll back to, the previous version. That means separating schema changes from behaviour changes: expand the schema first in a release that doesn't use it yet, then ship code that writes the new shape while still writing or tolerating the old one, and only contract after the new code has been stable for a full cycle. New data formats are introduced behind a feature flag, so turning the flag off stops new writes without a deploy. Old code must ignore unknown fields and tolerate new enum values. If something goes wrong after data was written, I'd decide using a pre-agreed rule: roll back if the new data is readable by the old code and the issue is user-facing; fix forward if rollback would orphan or misread data. And keep a script ready to repair or translate the records written during the bad window.","points":[{"h":"N and N-1 must coexist","d":"During rollout and after a rollback, two versions run against the same data. Every change must be compatible in both directions across adjacent versions."},{"h":"Expand, migrate, contract","d":"Release 1 adds nullable columns or new tables. Release 2 writes both old and new shapes. Release 3 reads new. Release 4 removes old, only after a full cycle with no rollback."},{"h":"Feature flags for new data paths","d":"Gate new write formats behind flags so you can stop producing new-format data instantly, independent of a code rollback."},{"h":"Tolerant readers","d":"Old code ignores unknown fields, handles new enum values with a safe default, and doesn't crash on nulls in new columns."},{"h":"Rollback vs fix-forward rule","d":"Roll back when the old version can read everything written and the fix isn't trivial. Fix forward when rollback would misread new data or the fix is a small, low-risk change."},{"h":"Repair tooling for the bad window","d":"Identify records written during the faulty period (by version tag or timestamp) and have a script to correct or translate them."},{"h":"Test rollback in CI","d":"Run the previous version against a database migrated by the new one. If tests fail, the release isn't rollback-safe."}],"decision":"Make rollback-safety a release requirement, tested automatically. Destructive schema changes (dropping or renaming columns, changing types) always go in a separate, later release.","numbers":["Contract delay example: keep the old column until the new release has run for at least one full business cycle (often a week, or a month for monthly batch jobs) with no rollback."],"pitfalls":["Renaming a column in the same release that starts using the new name: rollback breaks immediately.","Old code that crashes on an unknown enum value written by new code.","Contracting too early, then needing to roll back.","Assuming 'it's just a code rollback' when caches, queues and search indexes now hold new-format data."],"followups":[{"q":"The new version wrote 50,000 records in a format the old version misreads. Now what?","a":"Don't roll back blindly. Disable the flag to stop new writes, fix forward or deploy a version of old code that can read both formats, then repair the 50,000 records with a script."},{"q":"How do you enforce rollback-safety?","a":"A CI job that runs N-1 against the schema and data produced by N, plus migration linting that blocks destructive changes in the same release as code changes."},{"q":"What about messages already in queues?","a":"Same rule: consumers of N-1 must handle messages produced by N, which is why event schemas need compatibility checks too."}],"example":"A release added a 'repayment_schedule_v2' JSON field and started writing it. Calculations were wrong for some products. Because v2 was behind a flag and the old field was still written, on-call turned the flag off, rolled back the code, and a repair job recomputed affected schedules from the old field.","related":["schema-changes-downtime","migrate-billion-row-table-new","evolve-event-schema-breaking-consumers","design-mistakes-cheap-not-catastrophic"],"sources":[{"title":"AWS Builders' Library: Ensuring rollback safety during deployments","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/builders-library/ensuring-rollback-safety-during-deployments/"},{"title":"Martin Fowler: Parallel change (expand and contract)","url":"https://martinfowler.com/bliki/ParallelChange.html"}]}},{"id":"define-slos-lending-platform-derive","cat":"reliability","topic":"SLOs","level":"advanced","source":"both","q":"Define SLOs for a lending platform and derive alerts from the error budget.","also":["How do I define SLOs and design around them?"],"a":["User-visible indicators such as approval latency and disbursal success. Multi-window burn rate alerts. Link budget exhaustion to a release freeze policy.","Pick user-visible indicators, set targets from user need and cost, track error budget, alert on burn rate, and let budget exhaustion change release behavior."],"tags":["Observability","Reliability"],"legacy":[22,113],"answer":{"thesis":"Pick a few indicators that reflect what borrowers and partners experience, set targets from user need and cost, and alert on how fast the error budget is burning rather than on raw thresholds. Tie budget exhaustion to a release policy so the SLO changes behaviour.","pitch":"For a lending platform I'd choose user-journey SLIs: application submission success rate and latency, time to credit decision for instant products, disbursal success within the promised window, repayment processing success, and availability of the account API for partners. Each SLI is measured close to the user, at the load balancer or from client telemetry. Targets come from user expectations and cost, for example 99.9% of applications submitted successfully over 28 days. The error budget is the allowed failure, 0.1%. Alerts use multi-window burn rates: page when the budget is burning fast enough to be exhausted in about two days, measured over both one hour and five minutes so alerts are fast and reset quickly; open a ticket for slow burns. And the policy: when the budget is spent, feature releases pause and reliability work takes priority until it recovers.","points":[{"h":"SLIs follow user journeys","d":"Submission success and latency, decision time, disbursal completed within the promised window, repayment posted correctly, partner API availability. Avoid CPU or pod counts; users don't feel those directly."},{"h":"Measure near the user","d":"Use load balancer logs, API gateway metrics or client telemetry, not only server-side success counters, which miss requests that never arrived."},{"h":"Targets from need and cost","d":"Each extra nine costs a lot more. 99.9% over 28 days allows about 40 minutes of full outage; 99.99% about 4 minutes. Choose per journey."},{"h":"Error budget = 1 − target","d":"It quantifies acceptable unreliability and turns 'is it reliable enough?' into a number shared by product and engineering."},{"h":"Burn-rate alerts","d":"Burn rate is how fast you consume budget relative to plan. Page on fast burns (for example 14.4x over 1 hour and 5 minutes, which spends about 2% of a 30-day budget in an hour), ticket on slow burns (for example 6x over 6 hours, 1x over 3 days)."},{"h":"Policy with teeth","d":"Agree in advance: budget exhausted means release freeze for features, focus on reliability, and a review. Without a policy, SLOs become dashboards."},{"h":"Asynchronous journeys need freshness SLIs","d":"Disbursal and repayment are often batch or event-driven; measure 'completed within X minutes of request' rather than request success alone."}],"decision":"Three to five SLOs per product, covering the journeys that cost money or trust when they fail. Page only on fast burn of a user-facing SLO; everything else becomes a ticket or dashboard. Revisit targets quarterly.","numbers":["99.9% over 30 days = 43.2 minutes of total downtime equivalent. 99.95% = 21.6 minutes. 99.99% = 4.3 minutes.","Fast-burn rule from the Google SRE workbook: burn rate 14.4 over a 1-hour window (with a 5-minute confirmation window) consumes 2% of a 30-day budget."],"pitfalls":["SLOs on internal metrics (CPU, queue depth) that don't reflect user pain.","A target of 100%, which leaves no room for change and is never true.","Static threshold alerts that page on brief blips and miss slow, steady burns.","No release policy, so budget exhaustion changes nothing."],"followups":[{"q":"How do you set an SLO for disbursal, which depends on a bank?","a":"Measure the part you own (request accepted and sent to the bank within X) separately from end-to-end completion, and track the bank's performance as a dependency SLI with its own report."},{"q":"What if a partner demands 99.99%?","a":"Price it: show the architecture and cost needed, and offer an SLA below your internal SLO so there's margin."},{"q":"Why multi-window alerts?","a":"The long window gives significance and avoids paging on blips; the short window makes the alert stop quickly once the problem is fixed."}],"example":"The instant-loan journey has an SLO of 99.5% of decisions within 30 seconds over 28 days. A slow credit bureau integration starts burning budget at 10x; the 1-hour/5-minute alert pages on-call, who enables a cached-score fallback for repeat customers while the bureau recovers.","related":["alert-user-pain-not-noise","design-metrics-actually-tell-me","p50-flat-but-p99-doubled"],"sources":[{"title":"Google SRE workbook: Alerting on SLOs","url":"https://sre.google/workbook/alerting-on-slos/"},{"title":"Google SRE book: Service level objectives","url":"https://sre.google/sre-book/service-level-objectives/"}]}},{"id":"make-rebuild-scratch-possible-if","cat":"reliability","topic":"Recovery","level":"advanced","source":"ref","q":"How do I make rebuild from scratch possible if everything is lost?","also":[],"a":["Infrastructure as code, tested backups with restore drills, a replayable event log as source of truth, documented bootstrap order, and a measured recovery time."],"tags":["Reliability","Databases"],"legacy":[71],"answer":{"thesis":"Rebuilding from nothing requires three things to exist outside the failed environment: the definition of the infrastructure (as code), the data (as tested, isolated backups or a durable log), and the knowledge of order (a documented, rehearsed bootstrap). If any one is missing, the rebuild is a guess.","pitch":"I'd make everything reproducible from a small set of durable artefacts. Infrastructure and configuration as code in version control, so a new account or region can be provisioned by running pipelines, not by memory. Data backed up to an isolated location, in a different account with deletion protection, so ransomware or a compromised admin can't delete production and backups together, with restore tests on a schedule because untested backups are hopes. Where possible, an event log or ledger as the source of truth, so derived stores can be rebuilt by replay. Secrets and keys escrowed separately. Then a written bootstrap order, covering identity, networking, secrets, data stores, core services and edge, rehearsed in game days with a measured recovery time. The goal is that a different team could rebuild it from the runbook.","points":[{"h":"Infrastructure as code","d":"Networks, clusters, databases, DNS, IAM and configuration are defined in code and applied by pipelines. Manual console changes are detected as drift and reverted."},{"h":"Isolated, immutable backups","d":"Backups in a separate account or provider with write-once or deletion-protection settings, so an attacker or mistake with production credentials can't destroy them."},{"h":"Restore drills","d":"Regularly restore real backups into a clean environment and verify data integrity and application function. Measure time to restore."},{"h":"Replayable source of truth","d":"An append-only event log or ledger lets you rebuild caches, search indexes and read models by replay instead of backing up each one."},{"h":"Secrets and keys escrow","d":"Encryption keys, root credentials and break-glass access stored separately with documented recovery. Losing the key to encrypted backups is the same as losing the backups."},{"h":"Documented bootstrap order","d":"Dependencies form an order: identity and networking, secrets, data stores, core services, edge and DNS. Circular dependencies (deploy tool needs the service it deploys) must be found and broken."},{"h":"Measure recovery time","d":"Game days that rebuild a service or environment from scratch give a real RTO, which is usually much longer than assumed the first time."}],"decision":"Tier the effort: core data and the systems needed to rebuild everything else get the strongest isolation and the most frequent drills. Derived data relies on replay. Everything is code; nothing depends on one person's memory.","numbers":["Restore-time estimate: data size ÷ effective restore throughput. 10 TB at 500 MB/s is about 5.5 hours before indexes are rebuilt and the application is verified, which often doubles it."],"pitfalls":["Backups in the same account as production, deletable with the same credentials.","Never testing restores, then discovering a backup format or key problem during the disaster.","Circular dependencies: the CI system, secrets store or auth service needed to rebuild is itself part of what was lost.","Console-created resources no one captured in code."],"followups":[{"q":"How do you protect against ransomware specifically?","a":"Immutable backups with retention locks in an isolated account, separate credentials, monitoring for mass deletion or encryption, and practised restores."},{"q":"What's the minimum restore drill?","a":"Quarterly restore of the most important database to an isolated environment, run application checks against it, and record time and issues."},{"q":"How do you handle third-party SaaS data?","a":"Export it regularly to your own storage where possible, and document how to rebuild configuration if the vendor account is lost."}],"example":"A team runs an annual 'empty account' drill: a fresh cloud account, the infrastructure repo and the backup vault. The first drill took four days and found an IAM role created by hand and a secrets bootstrap loop. After fixes, the second took nine hours.","related":["design-data-center-outage-scenarios","fail-over-between-two-regions","rehearse-failure-before-production","design-systems-owned-teams-not"],"sources":[{"title":"AWS Well-Architected: Disaster recovery objectives","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/reliability-pillar/disaster-recovery-dr-objectives.html"},{"title":"Google SRE book: Data integrity","url":"https://sre.google/sre-book/data-integrity/"}]}},{"id":"event-driven-request-response-order","cat":"streaming","topic":"Event-driven design","level":"fundamental","source":"both","q":"Event-driven or request-response for order placement? Decide and defend it.","also":["When do I choose event-driven over request-response?"],"a":["Customer needs an answer now, so the order write is synchronous. Downstream work (fulfilment, notifications) is asynchronous. Say where you need a durable event and where a call is enough.","When producers should not wait or know about consumers, when work can be asynchronous, when many consumers need the same fact, or when you need replay. Not when the caller needs an immediate answer."],"tags":["Messaging","Architecture"],"legacy":[24,114],"answer":{"thesis":"Both. The customer needs an answer now, so placing the order is a synchronous request that commits the order and returns its status. Everything that follows (fulfilment, notifications, loyalty points, analytics) reacts to an OrderPlaced event published reliably from that commit.","pitch":"I'd make the order write synchronous: validate the cart, check price and stock, authorise payment if that's part of placement, commit the order, and return an order id and status in one request. The customer gets a definite answer, and errors like out-of-stock or a declined card show immediately. In the same transaction I'd write an OrderPlaced event to an outbox, and everything downstream consumes it: warehouse fulfilment, confirmation email, loyalty, fraud review, analytics. That keeps the user-facing path short and dependent on only the services needed for the decision, while downstream consumers can be slow, fail or be added later without touching checkout. Where something downstream can still change the order, like fraud review cancelling it, the order has explicit states and the customer is notified when it changes.","points":[{"h":"The decision is synchronous","d":"The customer needs to know immediately whether the order went through. Validation, pricing, stock reservation and payment authorisation produce that answer."},{"h":"Side effects are events","d":"Fulfilment, emails, loyalty, analytics and CRM don't affect the immediate answer. They subscribe to OrderPlaced and act independently."},{"h":"Publish reliably with an outbox","d":"Write the order and the event in one transaction, then relay the event. Never commit the order and then hope the publish succeeds."},{"h":"Minimise synchronous dependencies","d":"Every synchronous call on the order path adds latency and a way to fail. Keep only what's needed for the decision; use local data or cached reference data where possible."},{"h":"Explicit order states","d":"Placed, paid, fulfilling, shipped, cancelled. Asynchronous steps move the order between states and the customer can see where it is."},{"h":"Defend the trade-off","d":"Fully event-driven placement would mean the customer waits on a 'pending' screen for an unknown time. Fully synchronous would chain fulfilment and email into checkout and make it fragile."}],"decision":"Synchronous for the steps that decide the order's success; events for everything after. If a downstream step can reverse the order, model it as a state change with customer notification rather than making it synchronous.","numbers":["Latency budget example: placement path under 1 second at p99, including payment authorisation; downstream processing within seconds to minutes depending on the consumer."],"pitfalls":["Calling fulfilment, email and loyalty synchronously during checkout.","Publishing the event outside the order transaction (dual write).","Going fully async and leaving the customer unsure whether the order exists.","Consumers that aren't idempotent, sending duplicate emails or double points on redelivery."],"followups":[{"q":"What if stock reservation is in another service?","a":"Call it synchronously with a short timeout if the answer is needed before confirming, or accept the order optimistically and compensate (cancel and notify) if the reservation later fails. The business decides which experience is acceptable."},{"q":"How do you handle payment that takes several seconds?","a":"Authorise synchronously if it fits the budget; otherwise accept the order as 'payment pending', return quickly, and notify on completion."},{"q":"How do downstream teams get the data they need?","a":"The event carries the facts consumers commonly need (order id, items, amounts, customer id), with a versioned schema, so they don't call back into the order service for every event."}],"example":"Checkout reserves stock and authorises payment synchronously, commits the order with an outbox event, and returns 'Order placed' in 700 ms. The warehouse, email service and loyalty service each consume OrderPlaced; when the email provider is down for an hour, orders keep flowing and emails catch up.","related":["choose-between-synchronous-asynchronous-communication","design-outbox-inbox-pattern-reliable","replace-two-phase-commit-across","evolve-event-schema-breaking-consumers"],"sources":[{"title":"Martin Fowler: What do you mean by 'event-driven'?","url":"https://martinfowler.com/articles/201701-event-driven.html"},{"title":"microservices.io: Transactional outbox","url":"https://microservices.io/patterns/data/transactional-outbox.html"}]}},{"id":"exactly-once-practice-kafka-flink","cat":"streaming","topic":"Delivery semantics","level":"advanced","source":"both","q":"Exactly-once in practice: what do Kafka and Flink give you, and what do you still own?","also":["How do I handle exactly once versus at least once semantics in practice?"],"a":["Idempotent producers, transactions, checkpointed state. The sink and any external side effect still need idempotency. Be precise: exactly-once processing, not exactly-once delivery.","Assume at-least-once delivery and make consumers idempotent. Use transactional or idempotent producers and checkpointed processing where available, and note that external side effects need their own dedupe."],"tags":["Messaging"],"legacy":[25,116],"answer":{"thesis":"Kafka and Flink give exactly-once processing within their boundary: idempotent producers, transactions and checkpointed state make each record's effect on Kafka topics and Flink state happen once. Side effects outside that boundary (databases, APIs, emails) are still your job: make them idempotent or transactional with the checkpoint.","pitch":"Delivery over a network is at least once; what systems provide is exactly-once effect. Kafka's idempotent producer removes duplicates from producer retries per partition. Kafka transactions let a consume-transform-produce application atomically write output records and commit its consumer offsets, so downstream consumers reading with read_committed isolation see each result once. Flink checkpoints its state and source offsets consistently; on failure it rewinds to the last checkpoint and reprocesses, and its two-phase-commit sinks, including the Kafka sink, commit output only when a checkpoint completes. What you still own: any sink that isn't transactional with Flink or Kafka, such as a database write, an HTTP call to a partner, or an email. On replay after failure those happen again, so they need idempotency keys or upserts keyed by a deterministic id. Also, consumers reading with read_uncommitted see aborted data, and exactly-once adds latency, because output becomes visible only at transaction or checkpoint commit.","points":[{"h":"Be precise about the claim","d":"Exactly-once processing (effects applied once) not exactly-once delivery. Records may be read and processed more than once internally; the system makes the visible result as if once."},{"h":"Kafka idempotent producer","d":"Producer id plus sequence numbers per partition let the broker discard duplicate writes from retries."},{"h":"Kafka transactions","d":"Atomically write to multiple partitions and commit consumer offsets. Consumers with read_committed see only committed transactions."},{"h":"Flink checkpoints","d":"Consistent snapshots of operator state and source positions. After a failure, the job restores state and replays input from the checkpointed offsets."},{"h":"Two-phase-commit sinks","d":"Flink sinks that support it pre-commit output during a checkpoint and commit when the checkpoint completes. Output visibility is tied to checkpoint frequency."},{"h":"What you still own","d":"External side effects: database writes, API calls, emails, payments. Make them idempotent (upsert by deterministic key, idempotency keys) because replay will repeat them."},{"h":"Costs","d":"Latency equal to checkpoint or transaction interval for visible output, more coordination overhead, and careful configuration (isolation level, transaction timeouts)."}],"decision":"Use Kafka transactions or Flink exactly-once for pipelines where Kafka-to-Kafka or Flink state correctness matters (aggregations, billing counts). For external sinks, rely on idempotent writes and treat the pipeline as at-least-once into them. If latency matters more than duplicates, at-least-once with idempotent consumers is simpler.","numbers":["Visibility latency: with a 10-second checkpoint interval, transactional sink output appears in roughly 10-second steps; shortening the interval reduces latency but adds checkpoint overhead."],"pitfalls":["Claiming exactly-once end to end while the sink is a non-idempotent HTTP call.","Downstream consumers using read_uncommitted and seeing aborted records.","Transaction timeouts shorter than checkpoint intervals, causing aborted transactions and data loss or stalls.","Using random ids generated during processing as idempotency keys; replay generates new ids. Keys must be deterministic from input."],"followups":[{"q":"How do you write exactly-once into Postgres from Flink?","a":"Either a two-phase-commit sink that commits the database transaction on checkpoint completion, or idempotent upserts keyed by a deterministic id derived from the input, which is simpler and usually enough."},{"q":"Does Kafka exactly-once cover consumers in other services?","a":"Only if they participate in the transaction or dedupe themselves. A normal consumer that writes to its own database must be idempotent."},{"q":"What about sending emails from a stream?","a":"Emails can't be undone, so dedupe before sending: record the deterministic message id in a store and send with a provider idempotency key; accept a small risk window or confirm via provider status."}],"example":"A billing pipeline aggregates usage events in Flink with exactly-once checkpoints and writes invoice line items into Kafka transactionally. The invoicing service consumes with read_committed and upserts line items by (customer, period, meter) so a replay never double-bills.","related":["design-outbox-inbox-pattern-reliable","build-idempotency-write-api-clients","poison-message-blocks-partition-design","keep-ordering-also-need-parallelism"],"sources":[{"title":"Apache Kafka documentation: Message delivery semantics","url":"https://kafka.apache.org/documentation/#semantics"},{"title":"Apache Flink blog: End-to-end exactly-once processing with Apache Kafka","url":"https://flink.apache.org/2018/02/28/an-overview-of-end-to-end-exactly-once-processing-in-apache-flink-with-apache-kafka-too/"}]}},{"id":"poison-message-blocks-partition-design","cat":"streaming","topic":"Poison messages & dead letters","level":"advanced","source":"both","q":"A poison message blocks a partition. Design the handling.","also":["How do I avoid processing the same event forever due to poison messages?"],"a":["Bounded retries, retry topics with delay, dead-letter topic with context, alert on DLQ depth, and a safe replay tool. Decide when skipping is acceptable.","Attempt counters, routing to a dead-letter destination after a limit, per-message isolation so one bad record does not block the stream, and alerts for investigation."],"tags":["Messaging","Reliability"],"legacy":[26,119],"answer":{"thesis":"A poison message is one that fails every time. Don't let it block everything behind it: retry a bounded number of times, then move it aside to a retry or dead-letter topic with full context, keep the partition moving, alert on it, and provide a safe way to replay once fixed.","pitch":"In an ordered partition, a consumer that keeps failing on one message stops all progress for that partition, and lag climbs. I'd first separate transient from permanent failures: a timeout to a dependency is worth retrying; a deserialisation error or validation failure isn't. Transient failures get a few in-place retries with backoff, then move to a delayed retry topic, so the main partition continues. Permanent failures, or messages that exhaust retries, go to a dead-letter topic with the original payload, headers, error, stack trace, attempt count and source offset. The consumer commits past it and keeps going. I'd alert on DLQ arrivals, provide tooling to inspect, fix and replay messages from the DLQ back into the main flow, and decide whether skipping is acceptable: for strictly ordered entities, later messages for the same key may need to wait or be parked too.","points":[{"h":"Classify failures","d":"Transient (timeouts, 503s, lock conflicts) vs permanent (schema errors, invalid data, bugs). Only transient failures deserve retries."},{"h":"Bounded in-place retries","d":"A few attempts with short backoff for transient errors. More than that stalls the partition."},{"h":"Retry topics with delay","d":"Move the message to a retry topic consumed after a delay (for example 1 minute, then 10, then 1 hour) so the main partition keeps flowing."},{"h":"Dead-letter topic with context","d":"Include the original payload and headers, error message, stack trace, attempt count, timestamps and source topic, partition and offset."},{"h":"Alert and own the DLQ","d":"DLQ arrivals are signals of bugs or bad data. Alert on rate and depth, assign ownership, and review regularly."},{"h":"Safe replay","d":"Tooling to replay selected DLQ messages after a fix, through the same idempotent consumer path, with rate limits."},{"h":"Ordering implications","d":"If later messages for the same key depend on the poisoned one, either park them too (per-key parking) or accept that they may be applied out of order. Decide per domain."}],"decision":"Always have a dead-letter path for consumers of untrusted or evolving input. Use retry topics for transient failures in high-throughput streams. For strictly ordered domains, park per key rather than skipping silently.","numbers":["Retry schedule example: 3 in-place attempts at 100 ms, 500 ms, 2 s; then retry topics at 1 min, 10 min, 1 h; then DLQ. Total time before DLQ about 71 minutes."],"pitfalls":["Infinite retries in place, stalling the partition for hours.","Dead-lettering without context, so nobody can diagnose or replay.","Nobody watching the DLQ, which silently accumulates lost business events.","Skipping a message for an entity whose later messages depend on it, corrupting state."],"followups":[{"q":"How do you find out whether a failure is permanent?","a":"By error type: deserialisation, schema and validation errors are permanent; network and dependency errors are usually transient. Unknown errors get limited retries then DLQ."},{"q":"How do you park messages for one key without blocking the partition?","a":"Keep a 'parked keys' store; when a key is parked, route its subsequent messages to a parking topic until the poisoned one is resolved, then replay them in order."},{"q":"What if thousands of messages hit the DLQ at once?","a":"That's an incident, likely a bad deploy or schema change. Pause consumers or roll back, fix, then replay in bulk with rate limits."}],"example":"A schema change produced events with a missing required field. Consumers moved them to the DLQ with full context after one attempt, kept processing valid events, and paged the producing team. After the producer fix, a replay tool reprocessed the 18,000 dead-lettered events in 10 minutes.","related":["design-dead-letter-queues-retry","keep-ordering-also-need-parallelism","evolve-event-schema-breaking-consumers","exactly-once-practice-kafka-flink"],"sources":[{"title":"Enterprise Integration Patterns: Dead letter channel","url":"https://www.enterpriseintegrationpatterns.com/patterns/messaging/DeadLetterChannel.html"},{"title":"Confluent: Error handling patterns for Apache Kafka applications","url":"https://www.confluent.io/blog/error-handling-patterns-in-kafka/"}]}},{"id":"design-dead-letter-queues-retry","cat":"streaming","topic":"Poison messages & dead letters","level":"intermediate","source":"ref","q":"How do I design dead-letter queues and retry policies?","also":[],"a":["Bounded retries with backoff, delayed retry queues, a dead-letter queue carrying error context, alerting on its depth, and a safe replay path."],"tags":["Messaging","Reliability"],"legacy":[118],"answer":{"thesis":"A retry policy decides which failures are worth trying again, how often and for how long; a dead-letter queue catches what's left so it isn't lost or blocking. Both need owners, alerts and a replay path, or they become a place where business events go to die.","pitch":"I'd define per consumer: which errors are retryable, the backoff schedule with jitter, the maximum attempts or total time, and what happens after, which is a move to a dead-letter queue. Retries should be delayed rather than immediate, using the broker's visibility timeout or delay features or separate retry queues, so a failing dependency gets breathing room. The DLQ message keeps the original payload plus metadata: error, attempts, first and last failure times, and source. Then operations: an alert when anything lands in the DLQ or when depth grows, a dashboard, a named owner, and a replay tool that sends messages back through the normal consumer after a fix. I'd also set DLQ retention long enough to investigate, and make consumers idempotent because replay means reprocessing.","points":[{"h":"Retryable vs non-retryable","d":"Retry timeouts, throttling and temporary dependency errors. Don't retry validation failures, authorisation errors or deserialisation errors; send them straight to the DLQ."},{"h":"Delayed retries with backoff and jitter","d":"Use visibility timeouts or delay queues so retries happen after increasing intervals, not in a tight loop."},{"h":"Bound the attempts","d":"A maximum count (for example SQS's maxReceiveCount on a redrive policy) or a maximum age. After that, dead-letter."},{"h":"Enrich dead-lettered messages","d":"Keep payload and headers intact and add error details, attempt count and timestamps, so investigation doesn't require log archaeology."},{"h":"Alert, own, review","d":"An alert on any DLQ arrival for critical flows, or on rate for noisy ones. A team owns each DLQ and reviews it regularly."},{"h":"Replay tooling","d":"Inspect, filter, edit if needed, and redrive messages to the source queue at a controlled rate. Idempotent consumers make this safe."},{"h":"Retention","d":"DLQ retention longer than the main queue's, so messages survive weekends and investigations."}],"decision":"Every production queue consumer has a DLQ and a retry policy. Critical business events alert on the first DLQ message; high-volume, low-value events alert on rates. Replay is a tested, routine operation.","numbers":["Example policy: visibility timeout 30 s, exponential backoff on retries, maxReceiveCount 5, DLQ retention 14 days with an alert on depth greater than 0 for payment events."],"pitfalls":["Retrying non-retryable errors, wasting capacity and delaying the DLQ signal.","A DLQ with no alert and no owner.","Replaying into non-idempotent consumers and creating duplicates.","Retry delays that are too short, hammering a struggling dependency."],"followups":[{"q":"How do you prevent a downstream outage from filling the DLQ?","a":"Use a circuit breaker or pause consumption when the downstream is failing, so messages stay in the main queue rather than burning through retries and dead-lettering."},{"q":"Should the DLQ be per consumer or shared?","a":"Per consumer (or per source queue), so ownership is clear and replay targets the right consumer."},{"q":"How do you replay safely?","a":"Fix the cause, replay a small sample, watch for errors, then replay the rest with rate limiting."}],"example":"An SQS-based notification consumer retries provider timeouts up to 5 times with visibility-based backoff. Invalid phone numbers go straight to the DLQ. When the provider had a 2-hour outage, a circuit breaker paused consumption, so the queue grew but the DLQ stayed empty; processing resumed automatically.","related":["poison-message-blocks-partition-design","retries-amplifying-outage-design-retries","should-use-queue-versus-direct"],"sources":[{"title":"AWS SQS documentation: Using dead-letter queues","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSSimpleQueueService/latest/SQSDeveloperGuide/sqs-dead-letter-queues.html"},{"title":"AWS Builders' Library: Timeouts, retries and backoff with jitter","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/builders-library/timeouts-retries-and-backoff-with-jitter/"}]}},{"id":"keep-ordering-also-need-parallelism","cat":"streaming","topic":"Ordering","level":"advanced","source":"both","q":"Keep ordering when you also need parallelism.","also":["How do I handle ordering of events when it matters?"],"a":["Key by the entity whose order matters, choose partition count for peak consumers, sequence numbers per key, and what happens during rebalance.","Partition by the key whose order matters, sequence numbers for detecting gaps and duplicates, single consumer per partition, and care during rebalances and retries."],"tags":["Consistency","Messaging"],"legacy":[27,120],"answer":{"thesis":"You rarely need a global order, only order per entity. Partition by the key whose order matters, process each key sequentially, and run many keys in parallel. Size partitions for peak parallelism, and protect order during retries and rebalances.","pitch":"The trick is to find the scope of ordering. For an account, deposits and withdrawals must apply in order, but two different accounts are independent. So I'd key messages by account id: Kafka guarantees order within a partition, so all of an account's events go to one partition and are consumed in order, while different partitions are consumed in parallel. Partition count caps parallelism, so I'd choose it for peak consumer count with room to grow, because changing it later remaps keys. Inside a consumer I can go further: process different keys from the same partition concurrently while keeping each key sequential, with per-key queues, as Confluent's parallel consumer does. Then the edge cases: a retry must not let a later message for the same key overtake the failed one; during a rebalance, a partition's in-flight work must finish or be abandoned before the new owner starts; and sequence numbers per key let consumers detect gaps and duplicates.","points":[{"h":"Order per key, not globally","d":"Identify the entity whose events must be ordered (account, order, device). Global ordering forces a single consumer and kills throughput."},{"h":"Partition by that key","d":"Same key, same partition, which gives ordered delivery. Different partitions run in parallel across consumers in a group."},{"h":"Partition count sets max parallelism","d":"A consumer group can't have more active consumers than partitions. Choose the count for peak load plus growth; increasing it later changes key-to-partition mapping for new messages."},{"h":"Key-level parallelism within a partition","d":"A consumer can process different keys concurrently while serialising per key, raising throughput without more partitions."},{"h":"Retries without reordering","d":"If a message for key K fails, later messages for K must wait (block that key, not the partition) or be parked with it."},{"h":"Rebalances","d":"Commit offsets only after processing; on revocation, finish or abandon in-flight work cleanly. Cooperative rebalancing reduces disruption."},{"h":"Sequence numbers per key","d":"Producers stamp a per-key sequence; consumers detect gaps (missing events) and duplicates, and can reorder small windows if needed."}],"decision":"Key by the ordering entity and parallelise across keys. Add key-level concurrency in consumers when partitions are the bottleneck. Use per-key sequence numbers wherever correctness depends on order.","numbers":["Parallelism: 48 partitions allow up to 48 consumers in a group. If each processes 500 msgs/s, peak throughput is about 24,000 msgs/s.","Hot keys limit throughput: one key can only go as fast as one sequential processor. A key at 2,000 msgs/s needs per-message processing under 0.5 ms."],"pitfalls":["Keying by a random or round-robin value and losing per-entity order.","Retrying a failed message asynchronously while later messages for the same key proceed.","Committing offsets before processing completes, so a rebalance skips messages.","A very hot key that one consumer can't keep up with."],"followups":[{"q":"You need more partitions. What happens to ordering?","a":"Adding partitions changes which partition new messages for a key go to, so for a short time old and new messages for a key may be on different partitions. Drain or pause producers, or create a new topic and migrate."},{"q":"How do you handle a very hot key?","a":"Check whether its events truly need total order. If only sub-entities need order, refine the key. Otherwise make processing for that key faster or batch it."},{"q":"How do you order events from multiple producers for the same key?","a":"Route writes for a key through a single producer or the owning service, or use per-key sequence numbers assigned by the source of truth."}],"example":"A wallet service keys transaction events by account id across 64 partitions. Consumers use key-level concurrency, so each account is processed sequentially while thousands of accounts run in parallel. A failed event parks only that account's subsequent events until it's resolved.","related":["poison-message-blocks-partition-design","exactly-once-practice-kafka-flink","design-outbox-inbox-pattern-reliable","hot-partition-shows-up-2"],"sources":[{"title":"Apache Kafka documentation: Design (partitions and ordering)","url":"https://kafka.apache.org/documentation/#design"},{"title":"Confluent: Parallel consumer for Apache Kafka","url":"https://github.com/confluentinc/parallel-consumer"}]}},{"id":"design-scheduler-millions-delayed-jobs","cat":"streaming","topic":"Schedulers & long-running jobs","level":"advanced","source":"both","q":"Design a scheduler for millions of delayed jobs a day.","also":["How do I design a job scheduler for millions of jobs a day?"],"a":["Time-bucketed storage, a sharded poller with leases, at-least-once execution with idempotent jobs, clock skew, backlog catch-up after an outage.","Sharded time-bucketed storage, workers claiming jobs with leases, idempotent job execution, retry with backoff, and recovery of jobs held by crashed workers."],"tags":["Messaging","Scaling"],"legacy":[28,117],"answer":{"thesis":"Store jobs by due time in time-bucketed, sharded storage; have workers claim due jobs with leases; execute at least once and make jobs idempotent; and design explicitly for clock skew and catching up after an outage.","pitch":"Millions of delayed jobs a day is tens to hundreds per second on average, with peaks, so throughput isn't the hard part; correctness and recovery are. I'd store each job with its due time, partitioned by shard and time bucket, say per minute, so finding due jobs is a cheap range scan rather than scanning everything. A poller per shard, with one active owner per shard enforced by a lease, moves due jobs into a ready queue, or workers claim them directly by atomically setting a lease with an expiry. Workers execute and mark done; if a worker dies, the lease expires and the job is reclaimed, so execution is at least once and jobs must be idempotent. Clocks: use the database's or coordinator's time, not each worker's, and tolerate small skew. After an outage there's a backlog of overdue jobs, so catch-up must be rate-limited and prioritised, with a policy for jobs that are now too late to matter.","points":[{"h":"Storage by due time","d":"Index or partition by (shard, due_time). Time buckets (per minute) make 'what's due now' a small range query. Old buckets are dropped once processed."},{"h":"Sharding for scale","d":"Hash job id into N shards; each shard has one active poller at a time, which avoids duplicate polling and contention."},{"h":"Claim with leases","d":"A worker atomically marks a job claimed with a lease expiry (conditional update). If it doesn't finish in time, another worker can reclaim it."},{"h":"At-least-once, idempotent jobs","d":"Leases can expire while a slow worker still runs, so jobs may run twice. Use idempotency keys or check-and-set in the job's own effects."},{"h":"Clock skew","d":"Use one time source (the database's now()) for due checks and lease expiry. Allow a small tolerance; don't depend on second-level precision across machines."},{"h":"Catch-up after an outage","d":"Rate-limit the backlog, process the most urgent or oldest first, and define expiry rules (for example skip reminders more than 1 hour late)."},{"h":"Observability","d":"Metrics on scheduling lag (actual start minus due time), backlog per shard, failures and retries."}],"decision":"For millions of jobs a day, a relational or key-value store with time-bucketed indexes and leases is enough; reach for dedicated workflow engines (Temporal, etc.) when jobs have multi-step state and long durations. Use broker delay features only for short delays within their limits.","numbers":["Load: 5 million jobs/day ≈ 58 jobs/s average; with a 10x peak at certain times (for example the top of the hour), plan for around 600 jobs/s.","Storage: 5 million jobs × 1 KB = 5 GB/day; with 7-day retention of completed jobs, about 35 GB."],"pitfalls":["Polling a single table with 'WHERE due_time <= now()' and no partitioning, which slows as the table grows.","Multiple pollers per shard grabbing the same jobs.","Non-idempotent jobs running twice after a lease expiry.","Releasing a huge backlog all at once after an outage, overloading downstream services."],"followups":[{"q":"Many jobs are due at exactly 09:00. How do you avoid a thundering herd?","a":"Spread them with small jitter if the use case allows, rate-limit execution, and pre-load the bucket into the ready queue slightly ahead of time."},{"q":"How do you cancel or reschedule a job?","a":"Update its due time or status by job id; the poller only picks jobs in 'scheduled' status, and workers re-check status before executing."},{"q":"Why not just use Kafka with delayed delivery?","a":"Kafka has no native per-message delay; you'd build delay topics or a scheduler anyway. Some queues (SQS) support delays only up to a limit (15 minutes), not days."}],"example":"A lending platform schedules 4 million repayment reminders and retries per day. Jobs are stored in Postgres partitioned by day with an index on (shard, due_time); 32 shard pollers move due jobs into a queue; workers use the reminder id as an idempotency key with the SMS provider. After a 40-minute outage, catch-up ran at 3x normal rate and skipped reminders more than 2 hours late.","related":["track-status-long-running-jobs","raft-paxos-quorums-gossip-appear","build-idempotency-write-api-clients","handle-cache-stampede-thundering-herd"],"sources":[{"title":"Google SRE book: Distributed periodic scheduling with Cron","url":"https://sre.google/sre-book/distributed-periodic-scheduling/"},{"title":"AWS SQS documentation: Delay queues","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSSimpleQueueService/latest/SQSDeveloperGuide/sqs-delay-queues.html"}]}},{"id":"track-status-long-running-jobs","cat":"streaming","topic":"Schedulers & long-running jobs","level":"intermediate","source":"ref","q":"How do I track the status of long-running jobs for users?","also":[],"a":["A job record with state, progress and error, returned immediately with an id. Clients poll or subscribe. Make states resumable and make the record the source of truth, not the queue."],"tags":["Messaging"],"legacy":[72],"answer":{"thesis":"Return a job id immediately and make a durable job record the source of truth for its state, progress, result and error. Clients poll or subscribe for updates. The queue only carries work; it never answers 'what's the status?'.","pitch":"When a request kicks off work that takes seconds to hours, like an export, a report, or video processing, I'd create a job record first, with id, owner, state, progress, timestamps, result location and error, and return 202 Accepted with the job id and a status URL. Workers update the record as they go: queued, running with a percentage or step, succeeded with a result link, failed with a reason, or cancelled. Clients poll the status endpoint with sensible intervals, or subscribe via WebSocket, server-sent events or a webhook for completion. The record is the source of truth, not the queue, so status survives worker restarts and queue redeliveries. I'd add heartbeats so stuck jobs are detected, make job steps resumable for long work, and support cancellation by having workers check a cancel flag between steps.","points":[{"h":"202 Accepted with a job id","d":"Return immediately with the job id and a status URL. The client doesn't hold a connection open for minutes."},{"h":"Job record as source of truth","d":"A table with state, progress, timestamps, owner, input reference, result reference and error. The queue message only carries the job id."},{"h":"Clear state machine","d":"queued, running, succeeded, failed, cancelled, with allowed transitions enforced by conditional updates."},{"h":"Progress and heartbeats","d":"Workers update progress and a heartbeat timestamp. A sweeper marks jobs with stale heartbeats as failed or requeues them."},{"h":"Notification options","d":"Polling with backoff (simple), WebSocket or server-sent events (live UI), webhooks (server-to-server), or email for very long jobs."},{"h":"Resumability","d":"Break long jobs into checkpointed steps so a retry continues from the last step rather than starting over."},{"h":"Cancellation and cleanup","d":"A cancel endpoint sets a flag; workers check it between steps. Results expire after a retention period."}],"decision":"Polling for most cases; push (WebSocket or SSE) when users watch progress live; webhooks for API clients. Always back the API with a durable job record.","numbers":["Polling cost: 10,000 active jobs polled every 5 seconds = 2,000 status reads/s, cheap if served from an indexed table or cache; use backoff (2 s, 5 s, 10 s) to cut it further."],"pitfalls":["Inferring status from the queue (message present means queued), which breaks on redelivery and doesn't record results.","No heartbeat, so crashed jobs show 'running' forever.","Status endpoints that leak other users' jobs; check ownership.","Clients polling every 100 ms."],"followups":[{"q":"How do you handle the same export requested twice?","a":"Dedupe with an idempotency key or by input fingerprint, returning the existing job id."},{"q":"How do you show accurate progress?","a":"Report progress in known units (rows processed of total, steps completed) rather than time-based guesses."},{"q":"How would webhooks be made reliable?","a":"Sign payloads, retry with backoff, record delivery attempts, and let clients fetch status as a fallback if a webhook is missed."}],"example":"A loan portfolio export returns 202 with job id J. The worker updates the job every 10,000 rows; the UI polls with backoff and shows a progress bar; on completion the record holds a signed download URL valid for 24 hours.","related":["design-scheduler-millions-delayed-jobs","choose-between-synchronous-asynchronous-communication","design-fan-out-fan-pipeline"],"sources":[{"title":"Microsoft Azure Architecture Center: Asynchronous request-reply pattern","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/patterns/async-request-reply"}]}},{"id":"design-fan-out-fan-pipeline","cat":"streaming","topic":"Schedulers & long-running jobs","level":"advanced","source":"ref","q":"How do I design a fan-out and fan-in pipeline for heavy computations?","also":[],"a":["Split into idempotent tasks, track completion by counter or barrier, handle partial failure and stragglers, and bound concurrency to protect shared resources."],"tags":["Scaling","Messaging"],"legacy":[73],"answer":{"thesis":"Split the work into independent, idempotent tasks; track completion with a durable counter or barrier; handle stragglers and partial failure explicitly; and cap concurrency so the fan-out doesn't overwhelm shared resources.","pitch":"For a heavy computation, say scoring a million loans, I'd fan out by splitting into tasks of a sensible size, for example 1,000 loans each, and writing a parent job record with the total task count. Tasks go onto a queue; workers process them idempotently, writing results keyed by task id, so retries don't double-count. Fan-in happens when all tasks complete: each task atomically records completion, through a counter decremented in the database or a set of completed task ids, and whoever completes the last task triggers aggregation. I'd plan for stragglers, a few tasks taking much longer, with timeouts, retries and possibly speculative duplicates, and for failures, deciding whether the job fails if one task fails or completes with partial results. And I'd limit concurrency, since a thousand workers hitting the same database at once is a self-inflicted outage.","points":[{"h":"Task granularity","d":"Big enough that per-task overhead is small, small enough to balance load and retry cheaply. Seconds to a few minutes per task is a common target."},{"h":"Idempotent tasks with keyed results","d":"Write results by task id with upsert semantics, so a retried or duplicated task doesn't corrupt the aggregate."},{"h":"Completion tracking","d":"Parent record with total and completed counts, updated atomically, or a set of completed ids. The transition to 'all done' triggers fan-in exactly once (conditional update)."},{"h":"Stragglers","d":"Timeouts and retries for stuck tasks; speculative execution of slow tasks if idempotent; splitting tasks that are unexpectedly large."},{"h":"Partial failure policy","d":"Fail the whole job, retry failed tasks a few times, or complete with partial results and a report. Decide per use case."},{"h":"Concurrency limits","d":"Bound concurrent workers per shared resource (database, external API) with semaphores or worker pool sizes."},{"h":"Use an engine when it pays","d":"Workflow engines and data processing frameworks (Step Functions map states, Spark, Beam) handle fan-out, retries and fan-in for you."}],"decision":"Hand-rolled queue plus job record for moderate scale and simple aggregation. A workflow engine for multi-step orchestration with retries and visibility. A data processing framework for large-scale data-parallel work.","numbers":["Sizing: 1 million items, 1,000 per task = 1,000 tasks. With 50 workers and 30 s per task, about 10 minutes, plus straggler time.","Downstream load: 50 workers each issuing 100 queries/s = 5,000 queries/s on the shared database; confirm it can take that."],"pitfalls":["Non-idempotent tasks that double-count on retry.","Counting completions in memory on one coordinator that can crash.","Unbounded fan-out overwhelming a database or third-party API.","Fan-in triggered twice because two tasks see 'all complete' at the same time."],"followups":[{"q":"How do you make sure fan-in runs exactly once?","a":"A conditional update on the parent record from 'running' to 'aggregating'; only the update that succeeds triggers aggregation."},{"q":"One task is 100x slower than others. What do you do?","a":"Detect via timeout, split it if the input can be subdivided, or run a speculative copy, then investigate the skew."},{"q":"How do you report progress?","a":"Completed and total task counts on the parent record, exposed through the job status API."}],"example":"Monthly risk scoring splits 2 million loans into 2,000 tasks on SQS. Forty workers write scores keyed by task id; a database counter tracks completions; the last task's conditional update triggers the portfolio aggregation. Concurrency is capped at 40 to protect the database.","related":["track-status-long-running-jobs","design-scheduler-millions-delayed-jobs","design-backpressure-high-traffic-system"],"sources":[{"title":"Enterprise Integration Patterns: Scatter-gather","url":"https://www.enterpriseintegrationpatterns.com/patterns/messaging/BroadcastAggregate.html"},{"title":"Jeff Dean and Sanjay Ghemawat: MapReduce, simplified data processing on large clusters","url":"https://research.google/pubs/mapreduce-simplified-data-processing-on-large-clusters/"}]}},{"id":"evolve-event-schema-breaking-consumers","cat":"streaming","topic":"Event schema evolution","level":"intermediate","source":"both","q":"Evolve an event schema without breaking consumers.","also":["How do I design an event schema that can evolve safely?"],"a":["Schema registry with compatibility mode, additive changes, tolerant readers, versioned topics only as a last resort, and a deprecation window with consumer inventory.","Schema registry with a compatibility rule, additive-only changes by default, tolerant readers, explicit versioning for breaks, and a plan to retire old versions."],"tags":["Migration","Messaging"],"legacy":[29,115],"answer":{"thesis":"Treat event schemas as public contracts: register them, enforce a compatibility rule in CI and at publish time, make additive changes by default, and write consumers as tolerant readers. Breaking changes get a new event type or topic with a deprecation window, informed by a list of who consumes what.","pitch":"Events outlive the code that produced them, and producers often don't know all consumers. So I'd use a schema registry with Avro, Protobuf or JSON Schema and a compatibility mode, usually backward or full, checked in CI and enforced when producers register a schema. Safe changes: adding optional fields with defaults, adding new event types. Unsafe changes: removing or renaming fields, changing types, or changing meaning. Consumers ignore unknown fields and handle missing optional ones. When a breaking change is truly needed, I'd publish a new version as a new event type or topic, dual-publish for a period, track which consumers have migrated, and retire the old one on a date. Semantic changes are the sneaky ones: same field, different meaning, such as amount switching from pounds to pence, which no schema check catches, so they also need a new field name.","points":[{"h":"Schema registry with compatibility","d":"Producers register schemas; the registry rejects changes that violate the configured compatibility (backward, forward or full)."},{"h":"Additive by default","d":"New optional fields with defaults are safe. Never remove, rename or change the type of a field consumers may read."},{"h":"Tolerant readers","d":"Consumers ignore unknown fields, tolerate missing optional fields, and handle unknown enum values with a safe default."},{"h":"Compatibility checks in CI","d":"Fail the build if a schema change breaks compatibility, before it ever reaches production."},{"h":"Breaking changes as new versions","d":"New event type or topic (OrderPlacedV2), dual-publish during migration, then retire the old one."},{"h":"Know your consumers","d":"Maintain an inventory of consumer groups per topic and their owners, so deprecation is communicated and tracked."},{"h":"Semantic changes need new names","d":"Changing units, meaning or business rules of an existing field breaks consumers silently. Add a new field instead."}],"decision":"Backward or full compatibility enforced by a registry for all shared topics. Additive evolution for most changes; versioned new types for breaking ones, with dual-publishing and a dated deprecation.","numbers":["Deprecation window example: dual-publish for at least one full release cycle of the slowest consumer team, often 1–3 months for internal consumers."],"pitfalls":["Renaming a field because it reads better, breaking every consumer.","Strict deserialisation that rejects unknown fields.","Changing the meaning or units of an existing field.","Deleting the old topic before all consumers have moved, with no inventory to check."],"followups":[{"q":"Backward vs forward compatibility?","a":"Backward: new readers can read old data, so consumers upgrade first. Forward: old readers can read new data, so producers can upgrade first. Full means both; it's the safest for independently deployed teams."},{"q":"How do you replay old events with a new schema?","a":"The registry stores every version; consumers deserialise with the writer's schema and project into their current model, with defaults for added fields."},{"q":"JSON without a registry?","a":"Possible with JSON Schema and contract tests, but enforcement is weaker. At least validate in CI and in the producer, and version event types explicitly."}],"example":"The order service needed to split 'address' into structured fields. It added the new fields alongside the old string, consumers migrated over six weeks tracked via the consumer inventory, and the old field was deprecated, never repurposed.","related":["version-public-api-cannot-force","design-backward-compatibility-schemas-change","poison-message-blocks-partition-design","plan-rollback-release-has-already"],"sources":[{"title":"Confluent documentation: Schema evolution and compatibility","url":"https://docs.confluent.io/platform/current/schema-registry/fundamentals/schema-evolution.html"},{"title":"Martin Fowler: Tolerant reader","url":"https://martinfowler.com/bliki/TolerantReader.html"}]}},{"id":"draw-service-boundaries-lending-platform","cat":"apis","topic":"Service boundaries & gateway","level":"intermediate","source":"both","q":"Draw service boundaries for a lending platform: origination, underwriting, servicing, collections.","also":["How do I define clear boundaries between services?"],"a":["Bound by data ownership and change rate, not by nouns. Decide which data is copied by event and which is queried live. Show where a loan's state machine lives.","One owner per piece of data, interfaces that match business capabilities, low coupling on change, and a test: can a team ship without coordinating with others?"],"tags":["APIs","Architecture"],"legacy":[30,121]},{"id":"design-gateway-evolve-independently-services","cat":"apis","topic":"Service boundaries & gateway","level":"intermediate","source":"both","q":"Design a gateway that can evolve independently of the services behind it.","also":["How do I design a gateway that can evolve independently of services?"],"a":["Keep it thin: routing, auth, limits, observability. No business logic. Config-driven routes, canary support, and its own failure budget.","Thin gateway with routing, auth and limits only, declarative configuration, versioned routes, and no knowledge of service internals."],"tags":["APIs","Architecture"],"legacy":[35,125]},{"id":"version-public-api-cannot-force","cat":"apis","topic":"Versioning, compatibility & deprecation","level":"fundamental","source":"both","q":"Version a public API when you cannot force clients to upgrade.","also":["How do I design versioning for public APIs?","How do I design APIs so they can evolve without breaking clients?"],"a":["Additive changes by default, explicit versions for breaking ones, a support policy with dates, usage telemetry per version, and contract tests.","Additive changes within a version, a new version for breaking changes, a published support policy, usage metrics per version, and clear deprecation dates.","Additive changes only, optional fields with defaults, tolerant readers, explicit versioning for breaks, and contract tests against real client usage."],"tags":["APIs","Migration"],"legacy":[31,122,52]},{"id":"deprecate-endpoint-safely","cat":"apis","topic":"Versioning, compatibility & deprecation","level":"intermediate","source":"both","q":"Deprecate an endpoint safely.","also":["How do I design a safe deprecation process?"],"a":["Find real callers from logs, announce with dates, add Sunset headers, brown-out in staging then production, and keep a path for stragglers.","Identify callers from telemetry, announce with dates, warn in responses, run brownouts, support stragglers, and only then remove."],"tags":["Migration","APIs","Reliability"],"legacy":[34,127]},{"id":"design-backward-compatibility-older-clients","cat":"apis","topic":"Versioning, compatibility & deprecation","level":"intermediate","source":"ref","q":"How do I design for backward compatibility with older clients?","also":[],"a":["Support-window policy, version telemetry, feature negotiation, and server-side shims for the oldest supported clients. Know when you can finally stop supporting one."],"tags":["APIs","Migration"],"legacy":[78]},{"id":"handle-breaking-change-scenarios-minimal","cat":"apis","topic":"Versioning, compatibility & deprecation","level":"intermediate","source":"ref","q":"How do I handle breaking change scenarios with minimal disruption?","also":[],"a":["Run old and new in parallel, migrate clients with tooling and dates, monitor who still calls the old path, and offer a rollback during the cutover."],"tags":["APIs","Migration"],"legacy":[82]},{"id":"design-backward-compatibility-schemas-change","cat":"apis","topic":"Versioning, compatibility & deprecation","level":"intermediate","source":"ref","q":"How do I design for backward compatibility when schemas change?","also":[],"a":["Additive fields, defaults for missing values, never reuse or repurpose a field, readers that ignore unknown fields, and compatibility checks in CI."],"tags":["Migration","APIs"],"legacy":[126]},{"id":"design-per-tenant-rate-limits","cat":"apis","topic":"Rate limits & quotas","level":"intermediate","source":"both","q":"Design per-tenant rate limits that do not punish well-behaved bursty clients.","also":["How do I design tenant-level rate limits and quotas?","How do I design rate limiting that does not break good users?"],"a":["Token bucket with burst allowance, shared counters in a fast store, local approximation to cut round trips, clear 429 responses, and tiered quotas.","Limits per tenant and per plan, burst allowance, shared counters in a fast store, fair sharing under contention, and visibility so tenants see their usage.","Token bucket with burst headroom, limits per identity rather than per IP where possible, clear 429 responses with retry hints, and separate limits for abusive and normal traffic."],"tags":["APIs","Reliability","Scaling"],"legacy":[32,123,101]},{"id":"one-request-fans-out-five","cat":"apis","topic":"Partial failure","level":"advanced","source":"both","q":"One request fans out to five services and two fail. What do you return?","also":["How do I handle partial failures in multi-service calls?"],"a":["Classify calls as required or optional, return degraded results with a marker, set per-call timeouts, never block the whole response on the slowest optional call.","Timeouts per call, required versus optional dependencies, degraded responses, compensations for completed steps, and idempotency so retries are safe."],"tags":["Reliability","APIs"],"legacy":[33,124]},{"id":"should-each-layer-log-must","cat":"observability","topic":"Logging & tracing","level":"fundamental","source":"both","q":"What should each layer log, and what must never be logged?","also":["What should I log at each layer, and what should I never log?"],"a":["Structured logs with a request id at every layer. Never log secrets, tokens, full card numbers or personal data. Sampling for high-volume paths and retention by sensitivity.","Structured logs with correlation ids, entry and exit of requests, errors with context, and decisions. Never secrets, tokens, full payment data or unnecessary personal data."],"tags":["Observability","Cost & compliance","Reliability"],"legacy":[36,128]},{"id":"trace-request-across-50-services","cat":"observability","topic":"Logging & tracing","level":"advanced","source":"both","q":"Trace a request across 50 services without a runaway bill.","also":["How do I design tracing across 50+ microservices?"],"a":["Context propagation everywhere, tail-based sampling that keeps errors and slow traces, span attributes that are queryable, and a budget per team.","Standard context propagation, consistent span naming, tail-based sampling to keep slow and failed traces, and links from metrics and logs to traces."],"tags":["Observability","Cost & compliance"],"legacy":[37,129]},{"id":"alert-user-pain-not-noise","cat":"observability","topic":"Metrics, dashboards & alerts","level":"intermediate","source":"both","q":"Alert on user pain, not on noise.","also":["How do I design alerting that catches real issues but avoids noise?"],"a":["Page on symptoms such as error rate and latency against SLOs, ticket on causes. Every page needs an owner and a runbook link. Delete alerts that nobody acted on.","Alert on user-facing symptoms and SLO burn, attach runbooks, deduplicate and group, review noisy alerts regularly, and delete the ones that never lead to action."],"tags":["Observability","Reliability"],"legacy":[39,130]},{"id":"design-metrics-actually-tell-me","cat":"observability","topic":"Metrics, dashboards & alerts","level":"intermediate","source":"ref","q":"How do I design metrics that actually tell me system health?","also":[],"a":["Latency, traffic, errors and saturation per service and per dependency, with histograms rather than averages and business metrics alongside technical ones."],"tags":["Observability"],"legacy":[83]},{"id":"design-dashboards-help-during-incidents","cat":"observability","topic":"Metrics, dashboards & alerts","level":"intermediate","source":"ref","q":"How do I design dashboards that help during incidents?","also":[],"a":["One top-level health view, then drill-down by service and dependency. Put symptoms first, show deploy markers, and keep the layout the same across services."],"tags":["Observability","Operations"],"legacy":[84]},{"id":"p50-flat-but-p99-doubled","cat":"observability","topic":"Latency debugging","level":"advanced","source":"both","q":"p50 is flat but p99 doubled. How do you debug it?","also":["How do I debug high tail latency in a distributed system?"],"a":["Look for queueing, GC, noisy neighbors, a slow shard, retries, lock contention. Compare by instance, endpoint and dependency. Use exemplars to jump from a metric to a trace.","Compare percentiles by instance, endpoint and dependency, look for queueing, GC, retries and hot shards, and use traces to find the slow span. Hedged requests can mask but not fix it."],"tags":["Latency","Observability"],"legacy":[38,131]},{"id":"rehearse-failure-before-production","cat":"observability","topic":"Runbooks & failure drills","level":"intermediate","source":"both","q":"Rehearse failure before production does it for you.","also":["How do I simulate failures before they hit production?"],"a":["Game days, dependency fault injection, region evacuation drills, restore-from-backup tests. Start in staging with a hypothesis and a stop condition.","Fault injection for dependencies and infrastructure, game days, load and soak tests, and restore drills, starting with a hypothesis and a clear abort condition."],"tags":["Reliability","Observability"],"legacy":[40,133]},{"id":"write-runbook-call-engineer-follow","cat":"observability","topic":"Runbooks & failure drills","level":"fundamental","source":"both","q":"Write a runbook the on-call engineer can follow at 3 a.m.","also":["How do I design runbooks so on-call engineers can act fast?"],"a":["Symptom first, then checks in order, then safe actions with exact commands, then when to escalate. Tested by someone who did not write it.","Start from the alert, give ordered checks, exact commands, safe mitigations, escalation paths, and keep them tested and reviewed after incidents."],"tags":["Operations","Observability","Reliability"],"legacy":[41,132]},{"id":"design-something-mvp-first-still","cat":"evolution","topic":"MVP, platforms & cost","level":"fundamental","source":"ref","q":"How do I design something MVP-first that can still grow?","also":[],"a":["Make the cheapest design that satisfies today's load, but keep the seams that are expensive to add later: identifiers, data ownership, API contracts and observability."],"tags":["Scaling","Architecture"],"legacy":[74]},{"id":"choose-between-building-platform-one","cat":"evolution","topic":"MVP, platforms & cost","level":"intermediate","source":"ref","q":"How do I choose between building a platform and a one-off service?","also":[],"a":["Build a platform when three or more teams need the same capability and the interface is stable. Otherwise ship the one-off and extract later from real usage."],"tags":["Architecture"],"legacy":[75]},{"id":"design-cost-visibility-optimization-day","cat":"evolution","topic":"MVP, platforms & cost","level":"intermediate","source":"ref","q":"How do I design for cost visibility and optimization from day one?","also":[],"a":["Tag every resource by team and service, expose cost per request or per tenant, set budgets and alerts, and review the top spenders on a schedule."],"tags":["Cost & compliance","Observability"],"legacy":[76]},{"id":"decide-buy-build","cat":"evolution","topic":"Build vs buy","level":"intermediate","source":"both","q":"Decide what to buy and what to build.","also":["How do I decide what to buy versus what to build?"],"a":["Build what differentiates you and what you must control. Buy commodity. Count integration, lock-in, data egress and the people cost of running either.","Build what differentiates you or needs deep control. Buy commodity capability. Weigh lock-in, integration cost, data portability, and the ongoing cost of operating either choice."],"tags":["Architecture","Cost & compliance"],"legacy":[47,140]},{"id":"design-systems-multiple-teams-safely","cat":"evolution","topic":"Extensibility, experiments & compliance","level":"advanced","source":"ref","q":"How do I design systems that multiple teams can safely extend?","also":[],"a":["Clear extension points, stable contracts, isolation so one team's plugin cannot take down others, and ownership documented per component."],"tags":["Architecture","Leadership"],"legacy":[77]},{"id":"plan-data-residency-regional-regulations","cat":"evolution","topic":"Extensibility, experiments & compliance","level":"intermediate","source":"ref","q":"How do I plan for data residency and regional regulations?","also":[],"a":["Tag data by jurisdiction, pin storage and processing to regions, control cross-region replication and logs, and design deletion and access requests per region."],"tags":["Cost & compliance","Reliability"],"legacy":[79]},{"id":"design-support-experiments-such-as","cat":"evolution","topic":"Extensibility, experiments & compliance","level":"intermediate","source":"ref","q":"How do I design to support experiments such as A/B tests and flags cleanly?","also":[],"a":["Central flag service with stable user bucketing, exposure logging, kill switches, and a cleanup process for dead flags. Keep experiment logic out of business code paths where possible."],"tags":["Architecture"],"legacy":[80]},{"id":"design-migration-paths-legacy-system","cat":"evolution","topic":"Legacy migration","level":"advanced","source":"ref","q":"How do I design migration paths when the legacy system cannot just die?","also":[],"a":["Strangler pattern: route slices of traffic to the new system, keep both in sync during the overlap, migrate data by domain, and define an exit criterion for the old system."],"tags":["Migration","Architecture"],"legacy":[81]},{"id":"defend-design-trade-off-skeptical","cat":"judgment","topic":"Trade-offs & design reviews","level":"intermediate","source":"both","q":"Defend a design trade-off to a skeptical architect in five minutes.","also":["How do I defend trade-offs to a sceptical architect?"],"a":["State the constraint, the options you rejected and why, what would change your mind, and how you will find out cheaply if you are wrong.","State requirements and constraints, show alternatives with costs, say what you are giving up, and name the signal that would make you reverse the decision."],"tags":["Leadership","Architecture"],"legacy":[42,134],"answer":{"thesis":"Lead with the constraint, not the design. A skeptic is persuaded by seeing that you considered their option, priced your own downside honestly, and know what evidence would change your mind.","pitch":"I'd spend the first minute restating the requirement and the one or two constraints that drive the decision, for example p99 under 100 ms and a team of four, and check we agree on them. Then the decision in one sentence. Then the alternatives I rejected, including the one the architect probably prefers, each with the specific reason it lost against those constraints. Then what we're giving up, said plainly before they have to point it out. I'd close with the trigger that would make me reverse the decision and how cheaply we'll detect it: a metric, a load test, a date. If they still disagree, I'd ask which constraint they weigh differently, because that's usually where the real disagreement is.","points":[{"h":"Agree on the problem first","d":"Restate the requirement and the deciding constraints and get a nod. Many design arguments are really disagreements about requirements, and they dissolve once that's surfaced."},{"h":"State the decision in one sentence","d":"For example: 'One Postgres primary with read replicas, not a distributed SQL database.' A crisp claim is easier to examine than a diagram."},{"h":"Steelman the alternatives","d":"Present the skeptic's preferred option at its strongest, then show why it loses on these constraints: cost, team skills, latency, timeline. Strawmanning it loses the room immediately."},{"h":"Name your costs before they do","d":"'This caps us at roughly X writes per second and means a migration in about 18 months if growth holds.' Saying it first removes the gotcha and shows you've done the arithmetic."},{"h":"Show how reversible it is","d":"Distinguish one-way doors (data model, public API, deep vendor lock-in) from two-way doors. Point to the seams that make reversal cheaper: an interface boundary, an export path."},{"h":"Set kill criteria","d":"A measurable trigger, an owner and a check date. 'If p99 write latency exceeds 50 ms at peak or we pass 5 TB, we revisit.' That turns the debate into a plan."},{"h":"Find the crux if you still disagree","d":"Locate the specific assumption you differ on (growth rate, failure tolerance, team size) and propose a cheap experiment to settle it. If it's their call, record the reasoning and commit."}],"decision":"Push hard on one-way doors; concede quickly on two-way doors where trying is cheaper than arguing. A disagreement about facts needs data; a disagreement about values (cost against safety, speed against flexibility) needs a decider.","numbers":["A five-minute shape: about 1 minute on problem and constraints, 30 seconds on the decision, 2 minutes on alternatives and costs, 1 minute on reversal criteria, the rest for questions."],"pitfalls":["Defending the diagram instead of the decision: reasons persuade, boxes don't.","Strawmanning the alternative; the skeptic notices and stops listening.","Hiding the downside and letting them find it.","Treating it as a contest: being right while leaving the architect unheard costs you the next review.","Unfalsifiable claims such as 'it will scale' instead of a number and a test."],"followups":[{"q":"What would make you change your mind?","a":"Have a specific answer ready: a metric threshold, a requirement change or a test result, plus when you'll check it."},{"q":"Why not just use X? Everyone does.","a":"Acknowledge where X is genuinely better, then tie your choice back to the constraints. Popularity is evidence about ecosystem and hiring, which you can price, not a reason on its own."},{"q":"I'm the approver and I think you're wrong.","a":"Ask which constraint they weigh differently, record the decision and its rationale, and commit. If the decision is reversible, propose a checkpoint to revisit it with data."}],"example":"Proposing a modular monolith instead of microservices for a new lending product, with a team of six and a nine-month deadline. The architect preferred services for future scale. The defence conceded that independent scaling and deploys were being given up, showed module boundaries drawn along the future service lines, and set a trigger: split a module out when its team or deploy cadence diverges from the rest.","related":["review-peer-s-design-deeply","tell-design-over-engineered","write-design-doc-still-useful","decide-buy-build"],"sources":[{"title":"Michael Nygard: Documenting architecture decisions","url":"https://cognitect.com/blog/2011/11/15/documenting-architecture-decisions"}]}},{"id":"review-peer-s-design-deeply","cat":"judgment","topic":"Trade-offs & design reviews","level":"advanced","source":"both","q":"Review a peer's design deeply and still be easy to work with.","also":["How do I review someone else's design deeply but respectfully?"],"a":["Ask questions that expose assumptions, separate blocking concerns from preferences, propose an alternative when you object, and credit what is good.","Understand the goal first, ask questions about failure and scale, separate must-fix from preference, suggest alternatives, and acknowledge what is solid."],"tags":["Leadership"],"legacy":[46,136]},{"id":"explain-design-junior-developer-they","cat":"judgment","topic":"Trade-offs & design reviews","level":"intermediate","source":"ref","q":"How do I explain my design to a junior developer so they truly get it?","also":[],"a":["Start from the problem and one request path, add pieces only as the problem demands them, use a single diagram, and check understanding by asking them to predict a failure."],"tags":["Leadership"],"legacy":[85]},{"id":"tell-design-over-engineered","cat":"judgment","topic":"Simplicity & over/under-engineering","level":"intermediate","source":"both","q":"How do you tell when a design is over-engineered?","also":["How do I know when a design is over-engineered?"],"a":["Components with no current requirement behind them, flexibility nobody has asked for, more moving parts than people to run them.","Complexity without a current requirement, abstractions with one user, more services than teams, and anything you cannot explain the need for in a sentence."],"tags":["Leadership","Architecture"],"legacy":[43,137]},{"id":"tell-design-dangerously-under-engineered","cat":"judgment","topic":"Simplicity & over/under-engineering","level":"intermediate","source":"both","q":"How do you tell when a design is dangerously under-engineered?","also":["How do I know when a design is dangerously under-engineered?"],"a":["No answer for the failure you can name, no way to roll back, no capacity numbers, one person who understands it.","No failure handling for known risks, no capacity estimate, no rollback or recovery path, unclear ownership, and a single point of failure on a critical path."],"tags":["Leadership","Reliability"],"legacy":[44,138]},{"id":"simplify-complex-design-losing-guarantees","cat":"judgment","topic":"Simplicity & over/under-engineering","level":"advanced","source":"ref","q":"How do I simplify a complex design without losing guarantees?","also":[],"a":["List the guarantees that must hold, remove components that do not protect one, merge things that always change together, and prove the invariants still hold."],"tags":["Leadership","Reliability"],"legacy":[86]},{"id":"design-boring-reliability-instead-cleverness","cat":"judgment","topic":"Simplicity & over/under-engineering","level":"intermediate","source":"ref","q":"How do I design for boring reliability instead of cleverness?","also":[],"a":["Choose proven components, minimize moving parts, prefer simple failure modes, and spend novelty budget only where it creates real advantage."],"tags":["Leadership","Reliability"],"legacy":[88]},{"id":"decide-not-build-system","cat":"judgment","topic":"Simplicity & over/under-engineering","level":"intermediate","source":"ref","q":"How do I decide what not to build into a system?","also":[],"a":["Tie each feature to a present requirement or measured risk, defer what is cheap to add later, and write the non-goals down."],"tags":["Leadership","Architecture"],"legacy":[89]},{"id":"design-mistakes-cheap-not-catastrophic","cat":"judgment","topic":"Simplicity & over/under-engineering","level":"advanced","source":"ref","q":"How do I design so that mistakes are cheap, not catastrophic?","also":[],"a":["Small blast radius, progressive rollout, reversible changes, backups and restore tests, feature flags, and guardrails on destructive operations."],"tags":["Reliability","Leadership"],"legacy":[139]},{"id":"write-design-doc-still-useful","cat":"judgment","topic":"Design docs & ownership","level":"advanced","source":"both","q":"Write a design doc that is still useful in three years.","also":["How do I write a design doc that survives 3 years of evolution?"],"a":["Context and constraints, alternatives considered, decisions with reasons, non-goals, and open questions. The reasons age better than the diagrams.","Record context, goals and non-goals, alternatives considered, decisions and their reasons, and known risks. Date it, link follow-ups, and keep diagrams simple."],"tags":["Leadership"],"legacy":[45,135]},{"id":"design-systems-owned-teams-not","cat":"judgment","topic":"Design docs & ownership","level":"advanced","source":"ref","q":"How do I design systems that can be owned by teams, not heroes?","also":[],"a":["Documented runbooks, shared on-call, boring technology, clear ownership boundaries, and no component only one person understands."],"tags":["Leadership","Operations"],"legacy":[87]}]};
